// Generated from sources/question-bank/*.xlsx. Do not edit by hand.
window.AZ104_LEARN_QUESTIONS=[{"id":"AZ104-D1-001","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"You have a Microsoft Entra tenant that contains 5,000 users. You need to ensure that new users who have the department attribute set to Engineering are added automatically to a group named EngineeringTeam. The solution must minimize administrative effort. What should you do?","choices":["Create a group that has a Dynamic User membership type and configure a rule that uses the user.department attribute.","Create a group that has an Assigned membership type and add the users by using a scheduled PowerShell script.","Create a group that has a Dynamic Device membership type and configure a rule that uses the device.department attribute.","Create a Microsoft 365 group and configure a group naming policy that appends the department."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dynamic membership groups use rules based on user attributes such as user.department, so users are added and removed automatically with no ongoing manual work. An Assigned group requires manual or scripted membership, and a Dynamic Device rule can reference only device attributes, not user.department.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/groups-dynamic-membership","sourceTitle":"Manage rules for dynamic membership groups in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","groups","dynamic membership"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-002","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You are building a dynamic membership rule that must include only users who are in the marketing department AND whose country is France. Which rule syntax should you use?","choices":["(user.department -eq \"marketing\") -or (user.country -eq \"France\")","(user.department -eq \"marketing\") and (user.country -eq \"France\")","(user.department -eq \"marketing\") -and (user.country -eq \"France\")","(user.department -eq \"marketing\") -and (user.usageLocation -eq \"France\")"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The -and logical operator requires both expressions to be true, and -eq performs an exact string match on user.department and user.country. The -or operator would include users in either Marketing or France, the word and without a hyphen is not valid rule syntax, and usageLocation stores a two-letter code such as FR rather than a country name.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/groups-dynamic-membership","sourceTitle":"Manage rules for dynamic membership groups in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","dynamic membership","rule syntax"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-003","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to create a group whose membership is populated automatically from user attribute rules. Which group types support this configuration?","choices":["Distribution groups and mail-enabled security groups only.","Security groups and Microsoft 365 groups.","Mail-enabled security groups and Microsoft 365 groups only.","Any group type in Microsoft Entra ID."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dynamic membership is supported for security groups and Microsoft 365 groups. Distribution groups and mail-enabled security groups are managed through Exchange, so they cannot use Microsoft Entra dynamic membership rules.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/groups-dynamic-membership","sourceTitle":"Manage rules for dynamic membership groups in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","groups","dynamic membership"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-004","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"You have a Microsoft 365 group. You need the group membership to be updated automatically when a user's jobTitle property changes in Microsoft Entra ID. Which membership type should you configure for the group?","choices":["Assigned","External authority","Rule-based device","Dynamic User"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A Dynamic User membership type re-evaluates the membership rule whenever a user attribute changes, so members are added or removed automatically. Assigned membership is static and requires manual changes, and rule-based device membership evaluates device attributes only.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/concept-learn-about-groups","sourceTitle":"Learn about group types, membership types, and access management","tags":["Entra ID","groups","membership types"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-005","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You plan to bulk-create Microsoft Entra users by uploading a CSV file. Which columns are required in the bulk create template?","choices":["Name, User name, Department, and Usage location.","User name, Job title, Department, and Initial password.","Name, User name, Initial password, and Block sign in.","Name, User name, Usage location, and Job title."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The bulk create CSV template requires only four columns: Name, User name, Initial password, and Block sign in. Optional columns such as department, job title, and usage location can be left empty.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/users-bulk-add","sourceTitle":"Bulk create users in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","bulk operations","CSV"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-006","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to grant a helpdesk employee the ability to create new user accounts in Microsoft Entra ID and set their user properties. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege. Which Microsoft Entra role should you assign?","choices":["Global Administrator","User Administrator","Password Administrator","Helpdesk Administrator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"User Administrator is the least-privileged role that can create user accounts and manage user properties. Global Administrator is far broader than required, while Helpdesk Administrator and Password Administrator can reset passwords but cannot create users.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/how-to-create-delete-users","sourceTitle":"How to create, invite, and delete users","tags":["Entra ID","roles","least privilege"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-007","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to allow a specific support user to invite external guest users to the tenant. The solution must use the least privileged Microsoft Entra role. Which role should you assign to the support user?","choices":["Guest Inviter","User Administrator","External Identity Provider Administrator","Application Administrator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Guest Inviter role exists specifically to let a user invite external guest users without granting broader directory administration. User Administrator has much wider permissions, and the other two roles do not govern guest invitations.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/external-id/external-collaboration-settings-configure","sourceTitle":"Configure external collaboration settings for B2B in Microsoft Entra External ID","tags":["Entra ID","B2B","guest invitations"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-008","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Create users and groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to create a dynamic membership group that contains only Microsoft Entra registered devices that run Windows. Which type of rule should you create?","choices":["A user rule that uses the user.companyName attribute.","A user rule that uses the user.country attribute.","A device rule that uses the deviceOwnership.department attribute.","A device rule that uses the device.deviceOSType attribute."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Device membership rules can reference only device attributes such as device.deviceOSType, and a single group cannot mix user and device members. User attributes cannot be used in a device rule.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/groups-dynamic-membership","sourceTitle":"Manage rules for dynamic membership groups in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","dynamic membership","device rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-009","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage user and group properties","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need users to be able to sign in with user principal names that use the contoso.com suffix. The tenant currently uses only the default onmicrosoft.com domain. What should you do?","choices":["Create an Azure DNS zone for contoso.com and then create the users.","Add contoso.com as a custom domain, add a TXT or MX record at the domain registrar, and then verify the domain.","Create a second Microsoft Entra tenant and federate it with the existing tenant.","Configure company branding in Microsoft Entra ID and then create the users."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adding a custom domain is a three-step process: register the domain name in Microsoft Entra ID, create the TXT or MX verification record at the public DNS registrar, and verify the domain. An Azure DNS zone is not required, a new tenant is not created, and company branding has no effect on the UPN suffix.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/add-custom-domain","sourceTitle":"Add your custom domain name to your tenant","tags":["Entra ID","custom domain","UPN"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-010","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage user and group properties","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to add a custom domain name to a Microsoft Entra tenant. Which Microsoft Entra role provides the required permissions with the least privilege?","choices":["Global Administrator","User Administrator","Domain Name Administrator","Groups Administrator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Domain Name Administrator is the dedicated least-privileged role for adding and verifying custom domain names. Global Administrator also works but is far broader, and User Administrator and Groups Administrator do not manage domain names.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/add-custom-domain","sourceTitle":"Add your custom domain name to your tenant","tags":["Entra ID","custom domain","roles"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-011","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage user and group properties","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need every new Microsoft 365 group created by users to start with the department of the user who creates the group. What should you configure in the group naming policy?","choices":["A prefix of type Attribute that uses the Department attribute.","A prefix of type String that contains the word Department.","A suffix of type String that contains the word Department.","A custom blocked word list that contains Department."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A prefix or suffix of type Attribute substitutes a value from the group creator's user profile, so the Department attribute inserts the creator's department at the start of the name. A String type applies a fixed literal value, and blocked words only prevent specific names from being used.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/groups-naming-policy","sourceTitle":"Enforce a naming policy on Microsoft 365 groups in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","group naming policy"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-012","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage user and group properties","difficulty":"easy","question":"You configure a group naming policy that contains the custom blocked word Payroll. Which statement describes how the blocked word list is evaluated?","choices":["Any group name that contains the substring Payroll anywhere is blocked.","Group names are blocked only when they end with Payroll.","Blocked words are matched case-sensitively.","An exact match between the group name and a blocked word is required to trigger a failure."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Custom blocked words require an exact match against the group name or alias; no substring search is performed, so a name such as PayrollTax is not blocked by the entry Payroll. Blocked word matching is not case-sensitive.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/groups-naming-policy","sourceTitle":"Enforce a naming policy on Microsoft 365 groups in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","group naming policy","blocked words"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-013","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage user and group properties","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to assign custom security attribute values to identity objects. Which identity types can receive custom security attribute assignments?","choices":["Azure resources such as virtual machines and storage accounts.","Microsoft Entra tenants and Azure subscriptions.","Users, security groups, and service principals.","Distribution groups and mail-enabled security groups only."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Custom security attributes can be assigned to users, security groups, and service principals, which are all Microsoft Entra identity objects. They cannot be assigned to Azure resources such as virtual machines or storage accounts.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/custom-security-attributes-manage","sourceTitle":"Manage access to custom security attributes in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","custom security attributes"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-014","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage user and group properties","difficulty":"medium","question":"User1 must assign values of existing custom security attributes to users. User1 must NOT be able to create new attribute sets or attribute definitions. Which Microsoft Entra role should you assign to User1?","choices":["Attribute Definition Administrator","Attribute Assignment Administrator","Attribute Log Administrator","Privileged Role Administrator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Attribute Assignment Administrator can assign existing custom security attributes to identity objects but cannot create attribute sets or definitions. Attribute Definition Administrator performs the opposite task, and the other two roles govern audit logs and directory role assignments.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/custom-security-attributes-manage","sourceTitle":"Manage access to custom security attributes in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","custom security attributes","roles"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-015","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage user and group properties","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to prevent standard, non-administrator users from creating security groups in Microsoft Entra ID. What should you configure?","choices":["In User settings, set 'Users can create security groups' to No.","In User settings, set 'Users can register applications' to No.","In User settings, set 'Restrict non-admin users from creating tenants' to Yes.","In External collaboration settings, set collaboration restrictions to block all domains."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 'Users can create security groups' setting under User settings directly controls whether non-admin users can create security groups. The application-registration setting governs app registrations, the tenant-creation setting governs tenants, and collaboration restrictions govern B2B invitations.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/users-default-permissions","sourceTitle":"What are the default user permissions in Microsoft Entra ID?","tags":["Entra ID","default user permissions","security groups"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-016","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage licenses in Microsoft Entra ID","difficulty":"medium","question":"Group1 is a security group that has a Microsoft 365 E5 license assigned. Group2 is a security group that is a member of Group1. What happens to the members of Group2?","choices":["They automatically receive the E5 license because membership is nested.","They receive the E5 license only after Microsoft Entra Connect synchronizes Group2.","They do not receive the E5 license because group-based licensing does not support nested groups.","They receive the E5 license but lose it after 30 days."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Group-based licensing does not propagate through nested groups, so only direct members of Group1 receive the E5 license. To license the members of Group2, the license must be assigned directly to Group2.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/licensing-groups-assign","sourceTitle":"Assign or unassign licenses to a group in the Microsoft 365 admin center","tags":["Entra ID","licensing","nested groups"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-017","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage licenses in Microsoft Entra ID","difficulty":"easy","question":"You attempt to assign a Microsoft 365 license to a cloud-only user and the assignment fails even though the account is enabled. Which requirement is most likely missing?","choices":["The user does not have a manager assigned.","The user is not a member of a security group.","The user has no mobile phone number configured.","The user does not have a usage location configured."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A license cannot be assigned until the user's location setting is specified, because some Microsoft services are not available in all locations. Manager, group membership, and mobile phone number are not prerequisites for license assignment.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/licensing-groups-assign","sourceTitle":"Assign or unassign licenses to a group in the Microsoft 365 admin center","tags":["Entra ID","licensing","usage location"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-018","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage licenses in Microsoft Entra ID","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need to move a user from an E3-licensed group to an E5-licensed group without interrupting the user's access to licensed services. What should you do first?","choices":["Remove the user from the E3-licensed group.","Add the user to the E5-licensed group.","Assign the E5 license to the user directly and then remove the E3 group membership.","Delete the user's direct license assignments."],"answer":1,"explanation":"To avoid a gap in licensing, add the user to the destination licensed group first, confirm the new license is applied, and then remove the user from the original group. Removing the user from the source group first leaves the account unlicensed until the new assignment finishes processing.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/licensing-groups-assign","sourceTitle":"Assign or unassign licenses to a group in the Microsoft 365 admin center","tags":["Entra ID","licensing","group-based licensing"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-019","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage licenses in Microsoft Entra ID","difficulty":"easy","question":"You delete a Microsoft Entra user who has a directly assigned Microsoft 365 license. What happens to that license?","choices":["The license is released and becomes available for assignment to another user.","The license remains consumed until the user is permanently purged after 30 days.","The license is transferred automatically to the user's manager.","The license is converted into a group-based license."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When a user is deleted, any licenses consumed by that user are made available for other users. The deleted account can be restored for 30 days, but the licenses are released at deletion and are not transferred to another user.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/how-to-create-delete-users","sourceTitle":"How to create, invite, and delete users","tags":["Entra ID","licensing","user deletion"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-020","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage licenses in Microsoft Entra ID","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to allow an administrator to assign and remove Microsoft 365 licenses for Microsoft Entra groups. Which role provides this ability with the least privilege?","choices":["Global Administrator","User Administrator","License Administrator","Groups Administrator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"License Administrator is the least-privileged built-in role dedicated to assigning and removing Microsoft 365 licenses. Global Administrator also works but grants far broader permissions, and Groups Administrator is intended for group management rather than license administration.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/licensing-groups-assign","sourceTitle":"Assign or unassign licenses to a group in the Microsoft 365 admin center","tags":["Entra ID","licensing","License Administrator"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-021","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage licenses in Microsoft Entra ID","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to assign a Microsoft 365 license to a group so that all members receive the license automatically. Which group type cannot be used as the target of a license assignment?","choices":["A security group that is not mail-enabled.","A Microsoft 365 group.","A mail-enabled security group.","A distribution group."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Group-based licensing supports security groups, mail-enabled groups, and Microsoft 365 groups. A distribution group is a mail-only object that is not a valid target for license assignment.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/users/licensing-groups-assign","sourceTitle":"Assign or unassign licenses to a group in the Microsoft 365 admin center","tags":["Entra ID","licensing","group types"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-022","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage external users","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to ensure that users in your tenant can send B2B collaboration invitations only to guest users whose domain is fabrikam.com. What should you configure?","choices":["In External collaboration settings, configure Collaboration restrictions to allow only fabrikam.com.","In Cross-tenant access settings, enable B2B collaboration for all organizations.","Create a Conditional Access policy that targets guest users.","Configure a group naming policy that contains fabrikam.com as a custom blocked word."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Collaboration restrictions under External collaboration settings allow or deny B2B invitations for specific domains, so an allow list containing only fabrikam.com restricts invitations to that domain. Cross-tenant access settings govern trust between tenants and cannot restrict which domains can be invited.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/external-id/external-collaboration-settings-configure","sourceTitle":"Configure external collaboration settings for B2B in Microsoft Entra External ID","tags":["Entra ID","B2B","external collaboration"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-023","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage external users","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to ensure that only users who hold the User Administrator or Guest Inviter role can invite external guest users to the tenant. Which setting should you configure?","choices":["Guest user access restrictions","Cross-tenant access settings for inbound access","Guest invite settings: 'Only users assigned to specific admin roles can invite guest users'","External user leave settings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Guest invite settings option that permits only specific admin roles allows just users with the User Administrator or Guest Inviter role to invite guests. Guest user access restrictions control what guests can see in the directory, and leave settings control whether guests can remove themselves.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/external-id/external-collaboration-settings-configure","sourceTitle":"Configure external collaboration settings for B2B in Microsoft Entra External ID","tags":["Entra ID","B2B","guest invite settings"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-024","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage external users","difficulty":"easy","question":"Your tenant is configured so that only users assigned to specific admin roles can invite guest users. A user is assigned the Guest Inviter role and nothing else. What can the user do?","choices":["Nothing, because guests can be invited only by Global Administrators.","Invite guest users, even though invitations are restricted to specific admin roles.","Invite guest users only if the user is also a member of a security group.","Invite guest users only for domains that appear on a block list."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Users assigned the Guest Inviter role can invite guests even when the option that restricts invitations to specific admin roles is selected. Global Administrator is not required, group membership is irrelevant, and a block list restricts rather than grants invitations.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/external-id/external-collaboration-settings-configure","sourceTitle":"Configure external collaboration settings for B2B in Microsoft Entra External ID","tags":["Entra ID","B2B","Guest Inviter"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-025","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage external users","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to give a group of external partners time-limited access to a security group, an enterprise application, and a SharePoint Online site as a single requestable bundle. What should you use?","choices":["A mail-enabled security group and a Conditional Access policy.","A dynamic membership group and group-based licensing.","An administrative unit and a directory role assignment.","An entitlement management access package with a policy that defines expiration."],"answer":3,"explanation":"An access package bundles groups, applications, and SharePoint sites together, and its policy defines who can request access, who approves it, and when the assignment expires. Administrative units scope administration rather than resource access, and dynamic groups cannot include applications or sites.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-governance/entitlement-management-overview","sourceTitle":"What is entitlement management?","tags":["Entra ID","entitlement management","access packages"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-026","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage external users","difficulty":"hard","question":"An external user has an entitlement management access package assignment that expires after 365 days. The external user lifecycle setting is configured to remove the user after 30 days with no active access packages. What happens on day 365?","choices":["The user is removed from the resources in the access package, but the guest account remains in the tenant.","The guest account is deleted from the tenant and all resources are removed at the same time.","The user is removed from the tenant, but the access package resources remain assigned.","Nothing happens until an administrator manually removes the assignment."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When an access package assignment expires, the user is removed from the resources in the package, such as groups and applications, but the guest account itself remains in the tenant. The guest account is deleted only after the additional grace period configured in the external user lifecycle settings.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-governance/entitlement-management-overview","sourceTitle":"What is entitlement management?","tags":["Entra ID","entitlement management","external users"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-027","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure self-service password reset (SSPR)","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to enable self-service password reset for only a subset of users so that you can test the experience before a wider rollout. Where should you configure this?","choices":["In the Authentication methods blade, set the number of methods required to reset to 1.","In the Registration blade, set 'Require users to register when signing in' to Yes.","On the Password reset Properties page, set 'Self service password reset enabled' to Selected and choose a group.","In the Notifications blade, set 'Notify all admins when other admins reset their password' to Yes."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Properties page under Password reset controls whether SSPR is enabled for None, Selected users, or All users, and it is where a specific group is chosen. The Authentication methods, Registration, and Notifications blades control methods, registration prompts, and email notifications instead.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/tutorial-enable-sspr","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Enable users to unlock their account or reset passwords using Microsoft Entra self-service password reset","tags":["Entra ID","SSPR","password reset"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-028","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure self-service password reset (SSPR)","difficulty":"medium","question":"A user who is assigned the Global Administrator role forgets their password and tries to reset it by using self-service password reset with security questions. What is the result?","choices":["The user can reset the password because administrators always have full SSPR capabilities.","The reset fails because administrator accounts are subject to a two-gate policy that prohibits security questions.","The reset succeeds, but the user must change the password again at the next sign-in.","The reset succeeds only if the tenant has a custom domain configured."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Administrator accounts are enabled for SSPR by default with a strong two-gate policy that requires two pieces of authentication data and prohibits security questions. Global Administrator is one of the roles affected by this policy, so security questions cannot be used to reset the password.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/concept-sspr-policy","sourceTitle":"Password policies and account restrictions in Microsoft Entra ID","tags":["Entra ID","SSPR","administrator policy"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-029","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure self-service password reset (SSPR)","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to configure self-service password reset settings for an organization. Which Microsoft Entra role should you use with the least privilege?","choices":["Authentication Policy Administrator","Security Administrator","User Administrator","Groups Administrator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Authentication Policy Administrator has the authority to configure authentication policies, including the SSPR settings. Security Administrator, despite its name, is not the role for SSPR configuration, and User Administrator and Groups Administrator do not manage authentication policies.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/tutorial-enable-sspr","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Enable users to unlock their account or reset passwords using Microsoft Entra self-service password reset","tags":["Entra ID","SSPR","roles"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-030","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure self-service password reset (SSPR)","difficulty":"easy","question":"You plan to enable self-service password reset for users in a Microsoft Entra tenant. Which license is required for the users who will reset their passwords?","choices":["Microsoft Entra ID Free","Microsoft 365 Business Basic","Microsoft Intune for Education","Microsoft Entra ID P1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Password reset requires at least a Microsoft Entra ID P1 license for the tenant. The Free tier does not include password reset, and the other products listed are not the licensing requirement for SSPR.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/tutorial-enable-sspr","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Enable users to unlock their account or reset passwords using Microsoft Entra self-service password reset","tags":["Entra ID","SSPR","licensing"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-031","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure self-service password reset (SSPR)","difficulty":"easy","question":"Your organization requires users to provide two authentication methods to reset their password. Which SSPR settings blade should you configure?","choices":["Properties","Registration","Authentication methods","Notifications"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Authentication methods blade is where you select the methods available to users and set the number of methods required to reset. The Properties blade enables SSPR, the Registration blade controls registration prompts, and the Notifications blade controls email notifications.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/tutorial-enable-sspr","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Enable users to unlock their account or reset passwords using Microsoft Entra self-service password reset","tags":["Entra ID","SSPR","authentication methods"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-032","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure self-service password reset (SSPR)","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to require SSPR users to reconfirm their authentication information every 180 days. Which SSPR settings blade should you configure?","choices":["Properties","Registration","Notifications","Authentication methods"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Registration blade controls whether users must register when they next sign in and how many days before their authentication information must be reconfirmed. Properties enables SSPR, Notifications controls emails, and Authentication methods controls the available methods and their counts.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/tutorial-enable-sspr","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Enable users to unlock their account or reset passwords using Microsoft Entra self-service password reset","tags":["Entra ID","SSPR","registration"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-033","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure self-service password reset (SSPR)","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need to enable SSPR for all users in the tenant and require users to answer four security questions during registration. Which two SSPR blades must you configure?","choices":["Properties and Authentication methods","Properties and Notifications","Registration and Notifications","Authentication methods and Notifications"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Enabling SSPR for all users is done on the Properties blade, while enabling security questions and setting the required question count is done on the Authentication methods blade. Notifications controls email alerts only, and Registration controls registration prompts and reconfirmation intervals.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/tutorial-enable-sspr","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Enable users to unlock their account or reset passwords using Microsoft Entra self-service password reset","tags":["Entra ID","SSPR","configuration"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-034","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage built-in Azure roles","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to grant a user the ability to deploy virtual machines and manage virtual networks in a resource group. The user must NOT be able to assign roles to other users. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Owner","User Access Administrator","Virtual Machine Contributor","Contributor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Contributor grants full management of all resource types, including virtual machines and virtual networks, but does not allow role assignment. Owner also grants role assignment, User Access Administrator manages access rather than resources, and Virtual Machine Contributor cannot manage virtual networks.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/built-in-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure built-in roles","tags":["Azure RBAC","built-in roles","Contributor"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-035","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage built-in Azure roles","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to delegate the ability to assign Azure roles to other users at a resource group scope. The delegated user must NOT be able to manage resources. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["User Access Administrator","Contributor","Reader","Resource Policy Contributor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"User Access Administrator allows a security principal to assign roles at a scope without granting the ability to manage the resources themselves. Contributor cannot assign roles, Reader only views resources, and Resource Policy Contributor manages policy rather than access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/built-in-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure built-in roles","tags":["Azure RBAC","built-in roles","User Access Administrator"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-036","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage built-in Azure roles","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to grant a user the ability to create and edit Azure Policy definitions and initiatives and to create policy assignments. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Contributor","User Access Administrator","Resource Policy Contributor","Reader"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Resource Policy Contributor includes most Azure Policy operations, including creating and modifying policy definitions, initiatives, and assignments. Contributor can trigger remediation but cannot create or update definitions and assignments, and Reader provides read-only access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Policy?","tags":["Azure Policy","built-in roles","Resource Policy Contributor"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-037","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage built-in Azure roles","difficulty":"hard","question":"You create a custom role that must allow a user to sign in to a virtual machine by using RDP. The permission string Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/login/action must be placed in which section of the role definition?","choices":["Actions, because it is a management-plane operation.","DataActions, because signing in to a virtual machine is a data-plane operation.","NotActions, because it restricts management operations.","AssignableScopes, because it controls where the role can be assigned."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Signing in to a virtual machine is a data-plane operation, so the login action belongs in the dataActions section. Actions covers only control-plane resource management, NotActions excludes permissions, and AssignableScopes limits where the role is available for assignment.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/custom-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure custom roles","tags":["Azure RBAC","custom roles","dataActions"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-038","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage built-in Azure roles","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to grant a user the ability to list and regenerate storage account access keys. Which built-in role provides this with the least privilege?","choices":["Storage Account Key Operator Service Role","Storage Account Contributor","Reader","Storage Blob Data Reader"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Storage Account Key Operator Service Role permits listing and regenerating storage account access keys without granting broader management of the account. Reader cannot access keys, Storage Blob Data Reader grants blob data access rather than key management, and Storage Account Contributor is broader than the requirement.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/built-in-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure built-in roles","tags":["Azure RBAC","built-in roles","storage keys"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-039","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage built-in Azure roles","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to grant a group the ability to create Azure Logic Apps in a specific resource group. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Logic App Operator","Contributor on the subscription","Reader","Logic App Contributor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Logic App Contributor allows a user to create and manage logic apps at the assigned scope. Logic App Operator can only read, enable, and disable existing logic apps, and Contributor on the subscription would work but is far broader than the principle of least privilege requires.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/built-in-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure built-in roles","tags":["Azure RBAC","built-in roles","Logic Apps"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-040","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage built-in Azure roles","difficulty":"hard","question":"A user is assigned the Reader role on a storage account and nothing else. The user tries to download a blob from a container in that account by using the Azure portal. What is the result?","choices":["The download succeeds because Reader grants read access to all resources in the account.","The download succeeds because Reader includes all storage data actions.","The download fails because Reader is a management-plane role and does not grant access to blob data.","The download fails because the user also needs the Storage Account Key Operator Service Role."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reader grants management-plane visibility of resources but no data-plane access, so blob contents cannot be read. Reading blob data requires a data-plane role such as Storage Blob Data Reader, and key operator permissions are not what grants data access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC)?","tags":["Azure RBAC","data plane","Reader"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-041","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"easy","question":"You assign the Contributor role to User1 at a resource group named RG1. User1 then needs to manage a virtual machine that is in RG1. What access does User1 have?","choices":["User1 can manage the virtual machine only if an explicit role assignment is created on the VM.","User1 can manage the virtual machine because role assignments at a resource group scope are inherited by all resources in the group.","User1 can manage the virtual machine only after the subscription Owner approves the access.","User1 cannot manage the virtual machine because the assignment stops at the resource group."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Role assignments are inherited downward through the scope hierarchy, so Contributor assigned at a resource group applies to every resource in that group. No additional assignment on the virtual machine is required.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Understand scope for Azure RBAC","tags":["Azure RBAC","scope","inheritance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-042","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"medium","question":"User2 is assigned the Contributor role on a management group named MG1. MG1 contains Subscription1, and Subscription1 contains RG1. Which resources can User2 manage?","choices":["Only the management group itself.","Only the subscriptions that have an explicit role assignment.","Only resource groups that are explicitly added to MG1.","All resources in Subscription1 and RG1, because the assignment inherits down the hierarchy."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A role assigned at management group scope inherits to every subscription, resource group, and resource beneath it. Because role permissions flow downward, no per-subscription assignment is required.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Understand scope for Azure RBAC","tags":["Azure RBAC","management groups","scope"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-043","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"hard","question":"User3 is assigned the Owner role on a single virtual machine named VM1. VM1 is in a resource group named RG1. User3 attempts to create a new virtual machine in RG1. What is the result?","choices":["The attempt fails because the Owner role is scoped to VM1 and cannot create resources in RG1.","The attempt succeeds because Owner includes the ability to create resources anywhere in the subscription.","The attempt succeeds because Owner on a resource grants Contributor on the parent resource group.","The attempt fails because only the subscription Owner can create virtual machines."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A role assignment grants permissions only at the assigned scope and the scopes below it, so Owner on VM1 does not extend to the parent resource group. Creating a new resource requires Contributor or Owner at the resource group scope or higher.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Understand scope for Azure RBAC","tags":["Azure RBAC","scope","least privilege"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-044","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"medium","question":"Group1 is a security group that has the Contributor role on RG1. Group2 is a security group that is a member of Group1. User4 is a member of Group2. What access does User4 have?","choices":["No access, because Azure RBAC does not process nested groups.","No access, because only direct members of Group1 inherit the role.","Contributor on RG1, because RBAC role assignments are transitive for nested security groups.","Contributor on RG1 only after User4 is added directly to Group1."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Role assignments are transitive for groups, so a user who is a member of a group that is itself a member of a group with a role assignment inherits the permissions. Adding User4 directly to Group1 is not required.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC)?","tags":["Azure RBAC","groups","inheritance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-045","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to assign an Azure RBAC role on a subscription to a group. The group must be able to contain users, devices, and service principals as members. Which group type should you use?","choices":["A Microsoft 365 group, because it supports all member types.","A security group, because it can contain users, devices, and service principals.","A distribution group, because it is mail-enabled.","A mail-enabled security group, because it must support external members."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Security groups can contain users, devices, and service principals and are used to manage access to shared resources. Microsoft 365 group membership can include only users, and distribution groups are mail-only objects used for messaging rather than access management.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/concept-learn-about-groups","sourceTitle":"Learn about group types, membership types, and access management","tags":["Entra ID","groups","security groups"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-046","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which security principals can receive an Azure RBAC role assignment?","choices":["Users and groups only.","Users, groups, and Azure resources.","Users and managed identities only.","Users, groups, service principals, and managed identities."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure RBAC role assignments can be granted to users, groups, service principals, and managed identities, which are the supported security principal types. Azure resources are the objects that access is granted to, not principals that receive role assignments.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC)?","tags":["Azure RBAC","security principals","role assignments"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-047","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"hard","question":"You create a custom role named VM Operator. The role must be available for assignment only in a resource group named RG1 in Subscription1. Which assignableScopes value should you configure?","choices":["\"/\"","\"/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}\"","\"/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resourceGroups/RG1\"","\"/providers/Microsoft.Management/managementGroups/MG1\""],"answer":2,"explanation":"The assignableScopes property lists the scopes where a custom role definition can be assigned, and a single resource group is expressed as /subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resourceGroups/{resourceGroupName}. Assigning at the subscription would make the role available in every resource group in that subscription, and the root scope is not a valid value for assignableScopes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/custom-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure custom roles","tags":["Azure RBAC","custom roles","assignableScopes"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-048","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Assign roles at different scopes","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to grant a user the ability to add and modify security rules on one specific network security group. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege. Which role assignment should you create?","choices":["Assign Network Contributor on the specific network security group.","Assign Network Contributor on the subscription.","Assign Contributor on the resource group that contains the network security group.","Assign Owner on the network security group."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assigning Network Contributor directly on the network security group grants management of that NSG only, which is the narrowest scope that satisfies the requirement. Subscription or resource group scope grants broader access than needed, and Owner adds role-assignment permissions the user does not require.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Understand scope for Azure RBAC","tags":["Azure RBAC","scope","Network Contributor"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-049","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Interpret access assignments","difficulty":"medium","question":"The following role assignments exist: User1 is Owner at the subscription scope. User2 is Owner on RG2. User3 is Owner on VM1, which is in RG1. Which users have the Owner role on VM1?","choices":["User2 and User3 only.","User1 and User2 only.","User1 and User3 only.","User1, User2, and User3."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Owner at the subscription scope inherits down to every resource, including VM1, and User3 has a direct assignment on VM1. User2's Owner assignment is on RG2, a different branch of the hierarchy, so it does not apply to VM1 in RG1.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Understand scope for Azure RBAC","tags":["Azure RBAC","scope","inheritance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-050","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Interpret access assignments","difficulty":"medium","question":"User1 is Owner at the subscription scope. User4 is Contributor on RG1. User3 is Owner on VM1, which is in RG1. Who can create a new virtual machine in RG1?","choices":["User3 only.","User1 and User4 only.","User1 only.","User1, User3, and User4."],"answer":1,"explanation":"User1 inherits Owner from the subscription, and User4 has Contributor at the resource group scope, so both can create resources in RG1. User3's assignment is scoped to VM1 only and does not permit creating new resources in the resource group.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Understand scope for Azure RBAC","tags":["Azure RBAC","scope","effective access"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-051","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Interpret access assignments","difficulty":"medium","question":"A role assignment has the scope /subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resourceGroups/pharma-sales. What access does this assignment grant?","choices":["Access to the entire subscription, including all resource groups.","Access only to the single resource group named subscriptions.","Access to every resource group whose name contains pharma-sales.","Access to the pharma-sales resource group and all resources within it."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The scope string identifies one specific resource group by subscription ID and name, and permissions apply to that resource group and everything inside it. It does not extend to other resource groups in the subscription.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Understand scope for Azure RBAC","tags":["Azure RBAC","scope","resource ID"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-052","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Interpret access assignments","difficulty":"medium","question":"User1 has the Reader role on a storage account. User2 has the Storage Blob Data Reader role on the same storage account. Which statement is correct?","choices":["User2 can read blob data, while User1 can view the storage account configuration but cannot read blob data.","User1 can read blob data, while User2 can only view the storage account configuration.","Both users can read blob data because both roles include read permissions.","Neither user can read blob data because both roles are management-plane roles."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Storage Blob Data Reader is a data-plane role that grants read and list access to containers and blobs. Reader is a management-plane role that lets the user view the resource and its configuration but does not grant access to blob contents.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/built-in-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure built-in roles","tags":["Azure RBAC","data plane","storage"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-053","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Interpret access assignments","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need to grant a group read access to blobs in a storage account only when the blobs have a specific blob index tag. Which mechanism should you use?","choices":["A stored access policy on the container.","A shared access signature scoped to the container.","A role assignment condition on a blob data role such as Storage Blob Data Reader.","A read-only resource lock on the storage account."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Attribute-based access control adds a condition to an Azure RBAC role assignment, and conditions based on blob index tags are supported for blob data roles such as Storage Blob Data Reader. Stored access policies and shared access signatures cannot filter by blob index tags, and resource locks restrict changes rather than data reads.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/storage-auth-abac","sourceTitle":"Authorize access to Azure Blob Storage using Azure role assignment conditions","tags":["Azure RBAC","ABAC","blob index tags"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-054","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Interpret access assignments","difficulty":"hard","question":"A virtual machine was deployed by using an Azure deployment stack configured with the DenyDelete deny settings mode. User1 is assigned the Owner role on the virtual machine. User1 tries to delete the virtual machine. What is the result?","choices":["The deletion succeeds because Owner grants full control over the resource.","The deletion fails because the deny assignment created by the deployment stack overrides the role assignment.","The deletion succeeds because deny assignments apply only at the management group scope.","The deletion fails only if a read-only resource lock is also present."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deny assignments block specific actions even when a role assignment grants access, and a deployment stack with DenyDelete creates a deny assignment that prevents deletion. Owner permissions are overridden by the deny assignment, and no additional resource lock is required.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/deny-assignments","sourceTitle":"List Azure deny assignments","tags":["Azure RBAC","deny assignments","deployment stacks"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-055","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to prevent users from creating storage accounts in a subscription. Which Azure Policy effect should you use?","choices":["audit","append","modify","deny"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The deny effect blocks the create or update request for resources that do not comply with the policy rule. The audit effect only records non-compliance, and append and modify add or alter properties rather than blocking resource creation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/concepts/effects","sourceTitle":"Azure Policy definitions effect basics","tags":["Azure Policy","effects","deny"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-056","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"hard","question":"You assign a built-in policy that appends the tag CostCenter to resources. Resources already exist in the subscription. What is the effect of the assignment on those existing resources?","choices":["The existing resources are not modified; the append effect applies only when a resource in scope is created or updated.","The tag is added to all existing resources immediately.","The tag is added to all existing resources after 24 hours.","The tag is added to existing resources only if they are virtual machines."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An append policy adds the tag when a resource in scope is created or updated, so resources created before the assignment keep only the tags they already have. To bring existing resources into compliance, use the modify effect with a remediation task.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/tag-policies","sourceTitle":"Assign policy definitions for tag compliance","tags":["Azure Policy","tags","append effect"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-057","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to apply an Azure Policy assignment to several subscriptions and exclude one resource group from evaluation. What should you do?","choices":["Create a separate policy definition for each subscription.","Assign the policy at the resource level of each resource that must be evaluated.","Assign the policy at a management group scope and configure an exclusion for the resource group.","Assign the policy at each subscription and delete the policy from the resource group."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A policy assignment can target a management group, subscription, resource group, or individual resource, and subscopes can be excluded from evaluation. Assigning at management group scope covers all child subscriptions, and an exclusion removes the specified resource group from evaluation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Policy?","tags":["Azure Policy","assignment scope","exclusions"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-058","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"A user must be able to trigger remediation of non-compliant resources but must NOT be able to create or update policy definitions and assignments. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Owner","Contributor","Resource Policy Contributor","User Access Administrator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Contributor has access to all read Azure Policy operations and can trigger resource remediation, but cannot create or update definitions and assignments. Resource Policy Contributor can create and modify definitions and assignments, Owner has full rights, and User Access Administrator manages access rather than policy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Policy?","tags":["Azure Policy","built-in roles","remediation"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-059","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"Resources are tagged manually at the resource group level, but users report that the individual resources do not have the resource group tags applied. Which action should you take to make new resources inherit the resource group tag?","choices":["Assign an 'Inherit a tag from the resource group' policy definition that uses the modify effect at the subscription scope.","Assign a 'Require a tag on resources' policy definition that uses the deny effect at the subscription scope.","Enable tag inheritance in the subscription's Tags blade.","Apply a Read-only lock to the resource group."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tags applied to a resource group or subscription are not inherited by the resources inside them, so inheritance must be enforced with an Azure Policy definition such as 'Inherit a tag from the resource group', which uses the modify effect. A deny policy only blocks non-compliant resources, and a lock restricts changes rather than applying tags.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/tag-resources","sourceTitle":"Use tags to organize your Azure resources and management hierarchy","tags":["Azure Policy","tags","tag inheritance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-060","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which statement describes the difference between Azure Policy and Azure RBAC?","choices":["Azure Policy controls who can perform actions, while Azure RBAC controls resource configuration.","Azure Policy applies only to new resources, while Azure RBAC applies to existing resources.","Azure Policy is evaluated only at the management group scope, while Azure RBAC is evaluated at every scope.","Azure Policy evaluates resource properties and state regardless of who made the change, while Azure RBAC manages user actions at different scopes."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Policy ensures that resources comply with business rules by examining resource properties, without concern for who made the change. Azure RBAC instead focuses on which user or principal can perform which actions at which scope, and the two work together.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Policy?","tags":["Azure Policy","Azure RBAC","governance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-061","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"hard","question":"A deny policy that restricts allowed locations to westus is assigned to a subscription. A second deny policy that restricts allowed locations to eastus is assigned to a resource group in that subscription. What is the net effect for new resources in that resource group?","choices":["The resource group policy overrides the subscription policy, so resources can be created in eastus.","The most restrictive policy applies, so new resources in the resource group are blocked in both locations.","The subscription policy blocks all new resources in the resource group regardless of location.","No policy is enforced because the two assignments conflict."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each policy assignment is evaluated independently and the net result of layering policy definitions is cumulative most restrictive, so a resource must satisfy every applicable assignment. A narrower assignment at the resource group does not override a broader deny assigned at the subscription.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/concepts/effects","sourceTitle":"Azure Policy definitions effect basics","tags":["Azure Policy","effects","layering"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-062","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"You are preparing to enforce a new Azure Policy that requires resources to use approved SKUs. You want to understand the impact before blocking deployments. Which effect should you use first?","choices":["deny","modify","audit","deployIfNotExists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Microsoft recommends starting with the audit or auditIfNotExists effect to track how a policy definition affects resources before enforcement. Enforcement effects such as deny, modify, and deployIfNotExists change or block behavior and can disrupt existing automation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Policy?","tags":["Azure Policy","effects","audit"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-063","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Implement and manage Azure Policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to group several policy definitions so that they can be assigned and tracked together as a single item. What should you create?","choices":["An initiative definition, also called a policySet.","A policy exemption.","A resource lock.","A management group."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An initiative definition, also known as a policySet, is a collection of policy definitions that share an overarching goal and can be assigned as one item. Definitions added to the initiative later also become part of its existing assignments.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/policy/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Policy?","tags":["Azure Policy","initiative","policySet"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-064","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure resource locks","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to prevent users from deleting a storage account while still allowing them to modify its configuration. Which lock type should you apply?","choices":["ReadOnly","Delete","Deny assignment","CanNotDelete"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A CanNotDelete lock, shown as Delete in the portal, lets authorized users read and modify a resource but blocks deletion. ReadOnly would also prevent modifications, and a deny assignment is created by Azure or by a deployment stack rather than applied manually as a lock.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/lock-resources","sourceTitle":"Lock your Azure resources to protect your infrastructure","tags":["resource locks","CanNotDelete","governance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-065","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure resource locks","difficulty":"easy","question":"You apply a ReadOnly lock to a virtual machine. What can authorized users do with that virtual machine?","choices":["Read the virtual machine, but not delete or update it.","Read and modify the virtual machine, but not delete it.","Delete and recreate the virtual machine, but not modify it.","Nothing, because a ReadOnly lock removes all access."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A ReadOnly lock lets authorized users read a resource but prevents deletion and updates, which is similar to restricting all users to the permissions that the Reader role provides. CanNotDelete is the lock type that still allows modifications.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/lock-resources","sourceTitle":"Lock your Azure resources to protect your infrastructure","tags":["resource locks","ReadOnly","governance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-066","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure resource locks","difficulty":"medium","question":"You apply a CanNotDelete lock to a resource group that contains several resources. What happens to the resources in the group and to resources added later?","choices":["Only the resource group is protected; resources inside it are not affected.","Only the resources that existed when the lock was applied are protected.","The lock is inherited by all resources in the group, including resources added later.","The lock applies only to virtual machines in the group."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When a lock is applied at a parent scope, all resources within that scope inherit the same lock, and resources added later inherit it as well. The most restrictive lock in the inheritance chain takes precedence.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/lock-resources","sourceTitle":"Lock your Azure resources to protect your infrastructure","tags":["resource locks","inheritance","governance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-067","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure resource locks","difficulty":"medium","question":"User1 is assigned the Owner role on a subscription. A CanNotDelete lock is applied to a storage account in that subscription. User1 attempts to delete the storage account. What is the result?","choices":["The deletion succeeds because Owner grants full control over all resources.","The deletion fails because the lock overrides RBAC permissions, including Owner.","The deletion succeeds because locks apply only at the resource group scope.","The deletion fails only if User1 is also a User Access Administrator."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Management locks apply a restriction across all users and roles, so a lock overrides role-based access control permissions. The Owner role does not bypass a lock; the lock must be removed before the resource can be deleted.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/lock-resources","sourceTitle":"Lock your Azure resources to protect your infrastructure","tags":["resource locks","Azure RBAC","Owner"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-068","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure resource locks","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to protect a hierarchy of Azure resources from accidental deletion. At which scope can a resource lock NOT be applied?","choices":["Subscription","Resource group","Individual resource","Management group"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Locks can be applied to a subscription, a resource group, or an individual resource, but a lock cannot be added to a management group. Lock inheritance flows down from the scopes that support locks.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/lock-resources","sourceTitle":"Lock your Azure resources to protect your infrastructure","tags":["resource locks","management groups","scope"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-069","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure resource locks","difficulty":"hard","question":"A resource group named RG1 contains several virtual machines. A ReadOnly lock is applied to RG1. You need to move one of the virtual machines to RG2. What happens?","choices":["The move is blocked because a ReadOnly lock on the resource group prevents moving resources in or out of that group.","The move succeeds because locks do not affect move operations.","The move succeeds because the lock is applied to the group and not to the virtual machine.","The move is blocked only if RG2 also has a lock."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A ReadOnly lock on a resource group prevents moving existing resources in or out of that resource group. Resources also cannot be moved if a ReadOnly lock exists on the source, destination resource group, or subscription.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/lock-resources","sourceTitle":"Lock your Azure resources to protect your infrastructure","tags":["resource locks","ReadOnly","move operations"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-070","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Apply and manage tags on resources","difficulty":"easy","question":"You apply the tag CostCenter = 1234 to a resource group. A virtual machine already exists in that resource group and has no tags of its own. What tag does the virtual machine have?","choices":["CostCenter = 1234, because tags are inherited from the resource group.","CostCenter = 1234, because tags are inherited from the subscription.","No CostCenter tag, because resources do not inherit tags from a resource group.","CostCenter with no value, because the value is populated from the subscription."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Resources do not inherit the tags that are applied to a resource group or a subscription, so the virtual machine keeps only tags applied directly to it. Tag inheritance must be enforced with Azure Policy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/tag-resources","sourceTitle":"Use tags to organize your Azure resources and management hierarchy","tags":["tags","tag inheritance","governance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-071","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Apply and manage tags on resources","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which resource hierarchy scopes support tag assignment?","choices":["Resources and resource groups only.","Resources, resource groups, and subscriptions.","Resources, resource groups, subscriptions, and management groups.","All scopes, including the tenant root group."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tags can be applied to Azure resources, resource groups, and subscriptions. Management groups and the tenant root group do not support tags, so a requirement to tag at management group scope cannot be satisfied with tags.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/tag-resources","sourceTitle":"Use tags to organize your Azure resources and management hierarchy","tags":["tags","scope","management groups"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-072","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Apply and manage tags on resources","difficulty":"medium","question":"User1 must be able to manage tags on entities but must NOT have access to the entities themselves. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Contributor","Reader","User Access Administrator","Tag Contributor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Tag Contributor lets a user manage tags on entities without providing access to the entities themselves. Contributor grants full access to the resources, Reader cannot modify anything, and User Access Administrator manages role assignments rather than tags.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/built-in-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure built-in roles","tags":["tags","built-in roles","Tag Contributor"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-073","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Apply and manage tags on resources","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which statement correctly describes tag case handling in Azure?","choices":["Tag names are case-insensitive for operations, but tag values are case-sensitive.","Tag names are case-sensitive, but tag values are case-insensitive.","Both tag names and tag values are case-insensitive.","Both tag names and tag values are case-sensitive."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An operation updates or retrieves a tag by name regardless of the casing used, although the resource provider might keep the casing that you supply. Tag values, by contrast, are case-sensitive.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/tag-resources","sourceTitle":"Use tags to organize your Azure resources and management hierarchy","tags":["tags","tag names","tag values"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-074","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Apply and manage tags on resources","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to group Azure billing data so that costs can be reported per department. What should you use?","choices":["A resource lock on each department's resource group.","An Azure Policy definition that denies untagged resources.","Tags applied to the resources, with a key that identifies the department.","A separate management group for each department."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tags can be used to group billing data, for example to categorize usage by cost center or department, and tagged resources appear in the Tags column of the usage file. Management groups and locks organize or protect resources but do not categorize billing data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/tag-resources","sourceTitle":"Use tags to organize your Azure resources and management hierarchy","tags":["tags","cost management","billing"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-075","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Apply and manage tags on resources","difficulty":"medium","question":"An application team wants to store a database connection string as a tag value on the resources that the application uses. What should you do?","choices":["Create the tag with the connection string as the value because tags are encrypted at rest.","Do not store the value in a tag, because tags are stored as plain text and can be exposed.","Create the tag and then apply a ReadOnly lock to the resource.","Create the tag and mark the value as hidden in the tag definition."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tags are stored as plain text and can be exposed through cost reports, deployment histories, exported templates, and monitoring logs, so sensitive values must not be placed in tags. A resource lock controls changes to the resource and does not make tag values confidential.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/tag-resources","sourceTitle":"Use tags to organize your Azure resources and management hierarchy","tags":["tags","security","sensitive data"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-076","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage resource groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to organize resources so that they can be deployed, updated, and deleted together as a unit. What should you use?","choices":["A management group","A subscription","An administrative unit","A resource group"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A resource group is a container that holds related resources for an Azure solution, and resources that share the same lifecycle should be placed in the same resource group. Management groups and subscriptions are governance scopes, and administrative units scope Microsoft Entra administration.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/manage-resource-groups-portal","sourceTitle":"Use the Azure portal and Azure Resource Manager to manage resource groups","tags":["resource groups","lifecycle","governance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-077","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage resource groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to move a resource group and all of its resources to a different Azure subscription. What should you do?","choices":["Move all the resources in the resource group to a resource group in the other subscription, because a resource group cannot be moved to another subscription.","Select the resource group and use the Move to a new subscription option.","Delete the resource group and recreate it in the other subscription.","Change the subscription ID property of the resource group."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A resource group cannot be moved to a new subscription, but the resources inside it can be moved to a resource group in another subscription. Tags, role assignments, and policies do not transfer automatically and must be reapplied in the destination.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/move-resource-group-and-subscription","sourceTitle":"Move Azure resources to a new resource group or subscription","tags":["resource groups","subscriptions","move operations"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-078","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage resource groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to move several resources from a resource group in Subscription1 to a resource group in Subscription2. What is a requirement for this move?","choices":["Both subscriptions must be in the same Azure region.","Both subscriptions must belong to the same management group.","Both subscriptions must exist in the same Microsoft Entra tenant.","Both subscriptions must use the same billing account."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Moving resources across subscriptions requires that both the source and the destination subscriptions are in the same Microsoft Entra tenant. Region, management group membership, and billing account are not requirements for the move operation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/move-resource-group-and-subscription","sourceTitle":"Move Azure resources to a new resource group or subscription","tags":["resource groups","move operations","subscriptions"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-079","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage resource groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You apply a CanNotDelete lock to one resource inside a resource group. You then try to delete the resource group. What is the result?","choices":["Only the resource group is deleted and the locked resource remains.","The whole delete operation is blocked and no partial deletion occurs.","The resource group and all its resources are deleted because the lock is only inherited by child resources.","The deletion succeeds because the lock applies only to individual resources, not to groups."],"answer":1,"explanation":"If a delete lock exists on a resource in the resource group, deleting the resource group is blocked entirely, even when the resource group and other resources in it are unlocked. A partial deletion is not possible.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/lock-resources","sourceTitle":"Lock your Azure resources to protect your infrastructure","tags":["resource groups","resource locks","deletion"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-080","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage resource groups","difficulty":"hard","question":"You move a virtual machine that has an active Azure role assignment to a different resource group. What happens to the role assignment?","choices":["The role assignment does not move and becomes orphaned, so it must be created again after the move.","The role assignment moves automatically and continues to apply at the new scope.","The role assignment is converted into a deny assignment at the destination.","The role assignment blocks the move until it is converted into a policy exemption."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A role assignment does not move with the resource and becomes orphaned, so it must be created again after the move. The system removes the orphaned assignment automatically, although removing it before the move is recommended.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/move-resource-group-and-subscription","sourceTitle":"Move Azure resources to a new resource group or subscription","tags":["resource groups","move operations","role assignments"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-081","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to give a user access to manage resources across 15 Azure subscriptions by using the fewest role assignments. What should you do?","choices":["Place the subscriptions in a management group and assign the role at the management group scope.","Assign the role separately on each of the 15 subscriptions.","Assign the role on every resource group in each subscription.","Create a custom role and assign it separately in each subscription."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moving multiple subscriptions under a management group lets you create a single Azure role assignment on the management group that inherits to all of the subscriptions. Assigning the role separately in each subscription works but requires far more assignments.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","Azure RBAC","subscriptions"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-082","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"medium","question":"In a management group hierarchy, how many parent management groups can a single subscription have?","choices":["Up to six","Up to ten","Exactly one","Unlimited"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each management group and subscription can support only one parent, while each management group can have many children. The depth limit applies to the number of levels in the tree, not to the number of parents a subscription may have.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","hierarchy","subscriptions"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-083","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to move a subscription from one management group to another management group in the same tenant. What must be true?","choices":["The subscription must be moved to the root management group first.","You need write permissions on the subscription and management group write access on both the current and the target management groups.","The subscription must contain no resource groups.","The subscription must be deleted and recreated in the target management group."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Moving a subscription to another management group requires management group write permissions and role assignment write permissions on the subscription, plus management group write access on the current parent and the target parent. No root group step or recreation is involved.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","subscriptions","move operations"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-084","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"medium","question":"By default, who has access to the root management group of a Microsoft Entra tenant?","choices":["Every user who has access to any subscription in the tenant.","Every Global Administrator, automatically and permanently.","Every user assigned the Owner role on any management group.","No one; Global Administrators must elevate their access to gain access."],"answer":3,"explanation":"No one has default access to the root management group, and Microsoft Entra Global Administrators are the only users who can elevate themselves to gain access. After elevating, they can assign any Azure role to other users to manage the hierarchy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","root group","elevate access"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-085","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"hard","question":"A policy that denies a specific resource type is assigned at the tenant root group. A child management group has a policy that allows that resource type. A subscription in the child management group tries to create the resource. What is the result?","choices":["The creation is denied, because the deny policy inherited from the tenant root group still applies.","The creation succeeds, because the child management group policy is more specific.","The creation succeeds because deny assignments do not inherit to child scopes.","The creation is allowed, but the resource is marked non-compliant."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each policy assignment is evaluated independently, so a deny inherited from the tenant root group applies to all descendant subscriptions regardless of a narrower assignment at a child management group. An allow policy at a lower scope does not override a deny above it.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","Azure Policy","inheritance"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-086","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which statement about the root management group is correct?","choices":["It can be renamed and moved to another directory.","It can be deleted after all subscriptions are moved out of it.","It can be renamed, but it cannot be moved or deleted.","It is created manually the first time an administrator assigns a role."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The root management group's display name is Tenant root group by default and can be changed by a user who has the Owner or Contributor role on it, but it cannot be moved or deleted. It is built into the hierarchy rather than created manually.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","root group","tenant"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-087","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage costs by using alerts, budgets, and Azure Advisor recommendations","difficulty":"easy","question":"You configure a monthly budget with a threshold of 90 percent and an email alert. Costs reach 100 percent of the budget. What happens?","choices":["All resources in the subscription are stopped automatically.","An alert is generated and emailed, but resources are not affected and consumption is not stopped.","The subscription is disabled until the budget amount is increased.","The resources are deallocated automatically and the budget is reset."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Budgets notify you when spending reaches or exceeds a threshold, but resources are not affected and consumption is not stopped. Budgets never stop or deallocate resources, regardless of whether the budget is exceeded.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-acm-create-budgets","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Create and manage budgets","tags":["cost management","budgets","alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-088","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage costs by using alerts, budgets, and Azure Advisor recommendations","difficulty":"medium","question":"At which scopes can an Azure budget be created?","choices":["Individual resources only.","Management groups only.","Subscriptions only.","Management groups, subscriptions, and resource groups."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Budgets are supported for Azure RBAC scopes including management group, subscription, and resource group, as well as Enterprise Agreement and Microsoft Customer Agreement scopes. A budget cannot be scoped to an individual resource.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-acm-create-budgets","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Create and manage budgets","tags":["cost management","budgets","scope"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-089","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage costs by using alerts, budgets, and Azure Advisor recommendations","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which of the following is a type of Cost Management alert?","choices":["Credit alert","Deny alert","Resource lock alert","Compliance alert"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cost Management generates budget alerts, credit alerts, and department spending quota alerts, and it can also generate cost anomaly alerts. Deny, resource lock, and compliance alerts are not Cost Management alert types.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/cost-mgt-alerts-monitor-usage-spending","sourceTitle":"Use cost alerts to monitor usage and spending","tags":["cost management","cost alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-090","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage costs by using alerts, budgets, and Azure Advisor recommendations","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to review recommendations that can reduce your overall Azure spending and improve reliability and security. Into how many categories does Azure Advisor divide its recommendations?","choices":["Two: cost and security","Three: cost, security, and performance","Five: reliability, security, performance, cost, and operational excellence","Six: cost, security, performance, reliability, compliance, and networking"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Azure Advisor divides recommendations into five categories: reliability, security, performance, cost, and operational excellence. The Advisor dashboard displays personalized recommendations for all subscriptions within those categories.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/advisor/advisor-overview","sourceTitle":"Introduction to Azure Advisor","tags":["Azure Advisor","cost optimization","recommendations"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-091","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage costs by using alerts, budgets, and Azure Advisor recommendations","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to allow a user to view costs and create budgets and exports, without granting full Contributor access to Azure resources. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Owner","Cost Management Contributor","Reader","Cost Management Reader"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cost Management Contributor can view costs and manage cost configuration such as budgets and exports without the broader resource-management permissions that Contributor grants. Cost Management Reader can only view cost data and configuration.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/built-in-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure built-in roles","tags":["cost management","built-in roles","least privilege"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-092","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage costs by using alerts, budgets, and Azure Advisor recommendations","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need advance warning when spending trends are likely to exceed a budget before the budget amount is actually reached. What should you configure?","choices":["A forecasted budget alert threshold on the budget.","A credit alert.","A department spending quota alert.","A resource lock on the subscription."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forecasted budget alerts use forecasted cost predictions and are generated when the projected cost exceeds the configured threshold, which provides advance notification. Credit alerts trigger at fixed percentages of Azure Prepayment, quota alerts apply to department spending, and locks have no cost function.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-acm-create-budgets","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Create and manage budgets","tags":["cost management","budgets","forecasted alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-093","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure management groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You plan to organize several Azure subscriptions into a management group. What must be true about those subscriptions?","choices":["They must all use the same billing currency.","They must all be in the same Azure region.","They must all be owned by the same account.","They must all trust the same Microsoft Entra tenant."],"answer":3,"explanation":"All subscriptions within a single management group must trust the same Microsoft Entra tenant. Billing currency, region, and ownership by a single account are not requirements of the management group hierarchy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","subscriptions","Entra tenant"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-094","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure management groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to restrict the regions in which virtual machines can be created across all subscriptions in a management group. What should you do?","choices":["Apply a resource lock to the management group.","Assign an Azure Policy that restricts allowed locations at the management group scope.","Assign the Reader role on each subscription.","Create a budget at the management group scope."],"answer":1,"explanation":"An Azure Policy assigned at a management group scope cascades by inheritance to all associated subscriptions and their resources, and the resource or subscription owner cannot alter it. Locks cannot be applied to management groups, and role assignments and budgets do not restrict resource locations.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","Azure Policy","allowed locations"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-095","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure management groups","difficulty":"hard","question":"You create a custom role that includes dataActions and you want it to be usable across many subscriptions that share a management group. Which statement about management group scope is correct?","choices":["The custom role can be assigned at the management group scope without restrictions.","A custom role can never include both assignableScopes and dataActions.","A custom role with dataActions cannot be assigned at the management group scope, but it can be assigned at the subscription scope within that management group.","A custom role with dataActions can be assigned only at an individual resource scope."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Custom roles with dataActions cannot be assigned at the management group scope. You can still include a single management group in the assignableScopes and then assign the role on subscriptions within that management group.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/custom-roles","sourceTitle":"Azure custom roles","tags":["custom roles","dataActions","management groups"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-096","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure management groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"A Global Administrator needs to gain access to the root management group so that they can assign roles to other users across the entire hierarchy. What should the administrator do?","choices":["Elevate access to manage all Azure subscriptions and management groups.","Assign the Owner role on every subscription in the tenant.","Create a new management group above the root management group.","Convert the Global Administrator role into the User Access Administrator role."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Elevating access grants the User Access Administrator role at the root management group, and Microsoft Entra Global Administrators can do this themselves. After elevating, they can assign any Azure role to other users or groups to manage the hierarchy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","root group","elevate access"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-097","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure management groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"How many management group hierarchies can exist in a single Microsoft Entra directory?","choices":["One per subscription","Exactly one","Up to ten","Unlimited"],"answer":1,"explanation":"All subscriptions and management groups are within a single hierarchy in each directory, and all of them fold up into one root management group. This single hierarchy lets administrators apply global access and policies that other users in the directory cannot bypass.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","hierarchy","root group"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-098","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Configure management groups","difficulty":"hard","question":"You move a subscription so that it becomes a child of the root management group. Which permission requirement applies?","choices":["You need the Owner role on the root management group.","You need Management Group Contributor on the root management group and Contributor on the subscription.","You need Azure Policy Contributor on every management group in the hierarchy.","No permission is required on the root management group because it is the default landing spot."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Because the root management group is the default landing spot for all new management groups and subscriptions, permission requirements do not apply to it when it is the target or the existing parent. Permissions are still required on the item being moved and on the other parent in the hierarchy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","permissions","root group"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-099","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"hard","question":"User1 has the Owner role on Subscription1, and that role was inherited from the current management group. User1 wants to move Subscription1 to a management group where User1 has only the Contributor role. What is the result?","choices":["The move succeeds because Contributor on the destination management group is sufficient.","The move succeeds because Owner on the subscription always allows any move.","The move fails, because an inherited Owner role limits the move to management groups where User1 also has Owner.","The move fails because subscriptions can never be moved between management groups."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the Owner role on a subscription is inherited from the current management group, the move targets are limited to management groups where the user also has the Owner role, because Contributor would mean losing ownership. If Owner were assigned directly to the subscription, a move to a management group where the user has Contributor would be allowed.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","subscriptions","move operations"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D1-100","domain":1,"domainName":"Identities and governance","subtopic":"Manage subscriptions","difficulty":"easy","question":"A new Azure subscription is created in your tenant. Where does it appear in the management group hierarchy?","choices":["In the root management group of the directory by default.","In the first management group that was created in the directory.","In no management group until an administrator moves it.","In a management group named after the subscription."],"answer":0,"explanation":"New subscriptions automatically default to the root management group when they are created. All management groups and subscriptions fold up into the single root management group for the directory.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure management groups?","tags":["management groups","subscriptions","root group"],"sourceFile":"domain-1-identities-and-governance.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-001","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage firewalls and virtual networks","difficulty":"medium","question":"You have a storage account that is configured to permit access only from selected virtual networks. A virtual machine running on a subnet that is not listed attempts to read a blob. What is the result?","choices":["The request succeeds because resources in the same Azure subscription are automatically trusted.","The request fails with error 404 (Not Found) because the storage endpoint is hidden from unlisted subnets.","The request fails with error 403 (Forbidden) because traffic to a subnet is blocked by default until a rule allows it.","The request succeeds for read operations but fails for write operations."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When network rules are configured, only traffic from explicitly allowed sources can reach the public endpoint; requests from other subnets receive a 403 (Forbidden) error until a virtual network rule is added. Being in the same subscription does not grant access, and virtual network rules restrict both read and write operations equally.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-network-security","sourceTitle":"Azure Storage firewall rules and network access","tags":["storage firewall","virtual network rule","subnet","access denied"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-002","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage firewalls and virtual networks","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to let a subnet in an Azure virtual network reach a storage account over the Azure backbone, while the storage account keeps its public endpoint. What should you configure?","choices":["A private endpoint on the storage account.","A NAT gateway on the subnet.","A Microsoft.Storage virtual network service endpoint on the subnet, plus a virtual network rule on the storage account.","An application security group on the subnet."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A service endpoint extends the identity of the virtual network to the Azure Storage service so that traffic from the subnet travels over the Azure backbone, and the matching virtual network rule authorizes that subnet at the storage account. A private endpoint would instead assign a private IP address and change DNS resolution, and a NAT gateway controls outbound internet addressing rather than service access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-network-security","sourceTitle":"Azure Storage firewall rules and network access","tags":["service endpoint","virtual network rule","azure backbone","storage firewall"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-003","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage firewalls and virtual networks","difficulty":"hard","question":"An on-premises server that has the public IP address 203.0.113.10 must upload data to a storage account whose firewall uses a default action of Deny. Which type of network rule should you create?","choices":["A virtual network rule.","A private endpoint.","A resource instance rule.","An IP network rule for 203.0.113.10."],"answer":3,"explanation":"IP network rules enable traffic from specific public IP address ranges and are the supported way to grant access to clients that are not located in an Azure virtual network, such as an on-premises server. A virtual network rule requires a subnet rather than a public IP address, and a resource instance rule applies only to specific Azure resource instances in the same tenant.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-network-security","sourceTitle":"Azure Storage firewall rules and network access","tags":["ip network rule","on-premises","storage firewall","public ip"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-004","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage firewalls and virtual networks","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need Azure Backup to protect unmanaged disks that are stored in a storage account restricted to selected networks. What should you configure on the storage account?","choices":["The exception that allows trusted Microsoft services to access the storage account.","A service endpoint for Microsoft.Storage on the Azure Backup subnet.","A resource instance rule for the Recovery Services vault.","An IP network rule for the public IP ranges that Azure Backup uses."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A network security exception for trusted Azure services lets first-party services such as Azure Backup reach the storage account even when virtual network and IP rules would otherwise block them, and those services authenticate with strong authentication. There is no Azure Backup subnet or published IP range that you can add to a firewall rule, so the other options cannot work.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-network-security","sourceTitle":"Azure Storage firewall rules and network access","tags":["trusted services","azure backup","network security exception","firewall"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-005","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage firewalls and virtual networks","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to block all access to a storage account through its public endpoint while keeping access available through a private endpoint. Which storage account setting should you change?","choices":["Set Allow Blob public access to Disabled.","Set Public network access to Disabled.","Set the minimum TLS version to 1.2.","Set Allow storage account key access to Disabled."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Setting public network access to Disabled removes reachability through the public endpoint while private endpoint connections remain usable. Allow Blob public access controls only anonymous read access to containers and blobs, and disabling storage account key access affects authorization, not network reachability.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-network-security","sourceTitle":"Azure Storage firewall rules and network access","tags":["public network access","private endpoint","network security"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-006","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage firewalls and virtual networks","difficulty":"medium","question":"A subnet has a Microsoft.Storage service endpoint and the storage account has a matching virtual network rule. Which statement about IP network rules on that storage account is correct?","choices":["IP network rules that permit that subnet's public IP address continue to apply as before.","You must also create an IP network rule for the subnet, or traffic is denied.","Traffic from the subnet uses a private IP address as its source, so IP network rules that permit traffic from that subnet no longer have an effect.","IP network rules are evaluated before virtual network rules and always take precedence."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When you enable a service endpoint for a subnet, traffic from that subnet no longer uses a public IP address to communicate with the storage account; the source IP becomes a private IP. As a result, any IP network rule that allowed the subnet's former public address is no longer needed and no longer has an effect.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-network-security","sourceTitle":"Azure Storage firewall rules and network access","tags":["service endpoint","ip network rule","source ip","storage firewall"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-007","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to grant time-limited access to blobs in a container and be able to revoke that access without regenerating the storage account keys. Which type of shared access signature should you use?","choices":["An account SAS.","A user delegation SAS.","A service SAS that is associated with a stored access policy.","An ad hoc account SAS with a short expiry time."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A stored access policy is defined on the container and can be modified or deleted to revoke the signatures associated with it, which lets you revoke a service SAS without regenerating the account keys. Stored access policies are not supported for account SAS or user delegation SAS tokens, so neither of those can be revoked in this way.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-sas-overview","sourceTitle":"Grant limited access to data with shared access signatures (SAS) - Azure Storage","tags":["service sas","stored access policy","revoke","sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-008","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"medium","question":"A storage account has its Allow storage account key access setting set to Disabled. Which type of shared access signature still authorizes requests to Blob Storage?","choices":["An account SAS.","A service SAS.","A user delegation SAS.","All three SAS types continue to work."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A user delegation SAS is authorized with Microsoft Entra ID rather than the account key, so requests that use it are permitted when Shared Key authorization is disallowed. Service SAS and account SAS tokens are both authorized with Shared Key and are denied for all Azure Storage services when key access is disabled.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/shared-key-authorization-prevent","sourceTitle":"Prevent authorization with Shared Key - Azure Storage","tags":["user delegation sas","shared key","allow shared key access","sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-009","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which type of shared access signature does Microsoft recommend because it is secured with Microsoft Entra credentials instead of the storage account key?","choices":["A user delegation SAS.","An account SAS.","A service SAS.","An ad hoc account SAS."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Microsoft recommends using a user delegation SAS whenever possible because it is signed with a user delegation key that was created with Microsoft Entra credentials, so the account key does not need to be stored in your code. Service SAS and account SAS tokens are both signed with the storage account key, which provides weaker security.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-sas-overview","sourceTitle":"Grant limited access to data with shared access signatures (SAS) - Azure Storage","tags":["user delegation sas","microsoft entra id","best practice","sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-010","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"medium","question":"You generate a service SAS with the start time set to the current time. Users report intermittent authorization failures during the first few minutes. What should you change?","choices":["Regenerate the primary storage account access key.","Set the SAS start time to at least 15 minutes in the past.","Change the SAS protocol from HTTPS to HTTP.","Extend the SAS expiry time to 24 hours."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Intermittent failures immediately after a SAS becomes active are caused by clock skew between machines, which can be up to around 15 minutes in either direction. Microsoft recommends setting the start time at least 15 minutes in the past, or omitting it so that the SAS is valid immediately; switching to HTTP would weaken security rather than fix the problem.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-sas-overview","sourceTitle":"Grant limited access to data with shared access signatures (SAS) - Azure Storage","tags":["sas start time","clock skew","troubleshooting","sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-011","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must create a SAS token that lets a client list the blobs in a container and download those blobs, and nothing else. Which permissions should you grant?","choices":["Add, Create, Write","Delete, List, Write","List, Read","Process, Read, Update"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Listing the blobs in a container requires the List permission and downloading blob content requires the Read permission, so the least-privilege combination is List and Read. Permissions such as Create, Write and Delete are not needed and would grant more privileges than the scenario requires.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-sas-overview","sourceTitle":"Grant limited access to data with shared access signatures (SAS) - Azure Storage","tags":["sas permissions","least privilege","list","read"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-012","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"hard","question":"An application that runs as a managed identity must create a user delegation SAS for blob access. Which additional requirement applies to that identity?","choices":["The identity must be assigned the Owner role on the storage account.","The identity must have access to the storage account access keys.","The identity must be granted a role that includes the Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices/generateUserDelegationKey action.","The identity must be a member of the Storage Account Key Operator Service Role."],"answer":2,"explanation":"To obtain the user delegation key and then create the SAS, a Microsoft Entra security principal must hold an Azure role that includes the Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices/generateUserDelegationKey action. The Owner role or the Storage Account Key Operator Service Role is not required, and access to the account keys is deliberately not needed because a user delegation SAS is not signed with the account key.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-sas-overview","sourceTitle":"Grant limited access to data with shared access signatures (SAS) - Azure Storage","tags":["user delegation sas","generateuserdelegationkey","managed identity","rbac"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-013","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"hard","question":"A service SAS is associated with a stored access policy. You change the expiry time of the stored access policy to a value in the past. What is the effect on the SAS?","choices":["Every shared access signature associated with that policy immediately expires.","Only signatures created after the change are affected.","The policy is removed, but existing signatures remain valid until their own expiry time.","The signatures remain valid because an ad hoc SAS is not bound by the policy."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Setting the expiry time of a stored access policy to a value in the past causes all associated signatures to expire, and existing signatures inherit the constrained start time, expiry time and permissions from the policy. Altering or deleting the policy therefore immediately affects every service SAS that references it.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/define-stored-access-policy","sourceTitle":"Define a stored access policy - Azure Storage","tags":["stored access policy","revoke","expiry","service sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-014","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and use shared access signature (SAS) tokens","difficulty":"medium","question":"The security team wants users to receive a warning whenever they generate a service SAS or an account SAS with a validity interval longer than 24 hours. What should you configure?","choices":["A SAS expiration policy on the storage account.","An Azure Policy assignment scoped to the resource group.","A lifecycle management rule.","A resource lock on the storage account."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A SAS expiration policy specifies a recommended interval for SAS validity and applies to service SAS and account SAS tokens; when a user generates a token with a longer interval, a warning is displayed. An Azure Policy assignment, a lifecycle rule or a resource lock does not govern the validity interval of generated SAS tokens.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-sas-overview","sourceTitle":"Grant limited access to data with shared access signatures (SAS) - Azure Storage","tags":["sas expiration policy","storage account configuration","sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-015","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure stored access policies","difficulty":"medium","question":"A blob container already has two stored access policies defined. How many additional stored access policies can you create on that container?","choices":["Three","Five","Seven","None"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A maximum of five access policies can be set on a container, table, queue or file share at one time, and setting more causes the service to return 400 (Bad Request). Because two policies already exist, only three more can be created.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/define-stored-access-policy","sourceTitle":"Define a stored access policy - Azure Storage","tags":["stored access policy","container limit","sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-016","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure stored access policies","difficulty":"easy","question":"On which resources can a stored access policy be defined?","choices":["Blob containers only.","Blob containers and file shares only.","Blob containers, file shares, queues, and tables.","Blob containers, file shares, and Azure managed disks."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stored access policies are supported on blob containers, file shares, queues and tables, and a policy on a container or share can constrain a SAS that grants access to that resource or to the objects inside it. Azure managed disks do not support stored access policies.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/define-stored-access-policy","sourceTitle":"Define a stored access policy - Azure Storage","tags":["stored access policy","blob container","file share","queue"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-017","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure stored access policies","difficulty":"medium","question":"You issued a service SAS to a partner and associated it with a stored access policy. You must revoke access immediately without regenerating the storage account keys. Which action should you take?","choices":["Delete the blob container that the SAS grants access to.","Delete the stored access policy or change its signed identifier.","Change the storage account's default access tier to Archive.","Disable blob soft delete on the storage account."],"answer":1,"explanation":"To revoke a stored access policy you can delete it, rename it by changing the signed identifier, or set its expiry time to a value in the past; deleting or modifying the policy immediately affects all signatures associated with it. Deleting the container would destroy the data rather than simply revoking the token, and access tier or soft delete settings have no effect on an issued SAS.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/define-stored-access-policy","sourceTitle":"Define a stored access policy - Azure Storage","tags":["stored access policy","revoke sas","signed identifier"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-018","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure stored access policies","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which types of shared access signature support association with a stored access policy?","choices":["The service SAS only.","The account SAS only.","The service SAS and the account SAS.","The service SAS, the account SAS, and the user delegation SAS."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stored access policies are not supported for the user delegation SAS or the account SAS, so only a service SAS can be associated with one. A user delegation SAS and an account SAS must always be ad hoc SAS tokens.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/define-stored-access-policy","sourceTitle":"Define a stored access policy - Azure Storage","tags":["stored access policy","service sas","ad hoc sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-019","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure stored access policies","difficulty":"medium","question":"When you associate a service SAS with a stored access policy, where are the start time, expiry time, and permissions specified?","choices":["On the SAS token and on the stored access policy, so that the service can compare the two sets of values.","On the SAS token only, because the policy supplies only the resource types.","On the SAS token, because the policy stores only the signed identifier.","On the stored access policy, which the SAS token references by its signed identifier."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The start time, expiry time and permissions may all be specified on the stored access policy with none of them on the signature URI, or all of them on the URI with none on the policy; specifying a parameter in both places is not allowed. When the policy carries the parameters, the SAS token references the policy through its signed identifier.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/define-stored-access-policy","sourceTitle":"Define a stored access policy - Azure Storage","tags":["stored access policy","signed identifier","sas parameters"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-020","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage access keys","difficulty":"easy","question":"How many access keys does a newly created storage account have, and what is their size?","choices":["Two 512-bit access keys.","One 256-bit access key.","Two 256-bit access keys.","Four 512-bit access keys."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When you create a storage account, Azure generates two 512-bit storage account access keys. The two keys exist so that you can rotate keys without interrupting access, because one key remains usable while the other is regenerated.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-keys-manage","sourceTitle":"Manage account access keys - Azure Storage","tags":["access keys","key rotation","storage account"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-021","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage access keys","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to delegate the ability to list and regenerate storage account access keys to an operator, without granting that operator Microsoft Entra ID access to blob data. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Storage Blob Data Contributor","Storage Account Key Operator Service Role","Reader","Storage Blob Data Owner"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/listkeys/action and Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/regeneratekey/action permissions are included in the Owner, Contributor and Storage Account Key Operator Service Role roles, so the key operator role grants key management only. Storage Blob Data Contributor and Storage Blob Data Owner grant data-plane access to blobs through Microsoft Entra ID and do not include key management permissions.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-keys-manage","sourceTitle":"Manage account access keys - Azure Storage","tags":["built-in role","access keys","rbac","key operator"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-022","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage access keys","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need the storage account access keys to be rotated on a schedule without interrupting applications that read the current key. What should you configure?","choices":["Register the storage account with Azure Key Vault and let Key Vault manage the key rotation.","An Azure Policy assignment that audits key age.","A lifecycle management rule that expires keys.","Infrastructure encryption on the storage account."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Microsoft recommends using Azure Key Vault to manage and rotate storage account access keys, because Key Vault can regenerate the active key on a schedule and applications retrieve the current key from the vault without being modified. Azure Policy can only audit or report on key rotation, and lifecycle management and infrastructure encryption have no role in key rotation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-keys-manage","sourceTitle":"Manage account access keys - Azure Storage","tags":["key rotation","azure key vault","managed storage account"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-023","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage access keys","difficulty":"hard","question":"You regenerate the primary access key of a storage account. Which tokens stop working as a result?","choices":["User delegation SAS tokens only.","No tokens are affected because tokens are independent of keys.","Microsoft Entra ID access tokens issued for managed identities.","Account SAS and service SAS tokens that were signed with the previous key."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Rotating or regenerating an access key revokes the shared access signatures that were generated from that key, so account SAS and service SAS tokens must be regenerated afterwards. User delegation SAS tokens are secured with Microsoft Entra credentials and are not affected by key rotation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-keys-manage","sourceTitle":"Manage account access keys - Azure Storage","tags":["key rotation","sas revocation","user delegation sas"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-024","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage access keys","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must prevent all Shared Key authorization against a storage account so that Microsoft Entra Conditional Access policies can be applied to it. Which setting should you configure?","choices":["Set the minimum TLS version to 1.2.","Set Allow storage account key access to Disabled.","Set Allow Blob public access to Disabled.","Set Public network access to Disabled."],"answer":1,"explanation":"To protect an Azure Storage account with Microsoft Entra Conditional Access policies you must disallow Shared Key authorization, which is controlled by the Allow storage account key access setting. The minimum TLS version, blob public access and public network access settings do not affect Shared Key authorization.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/shared-key-authorization-prevent","sourceTitle":"Prevent authorization with Shared Key - Azure Storage","tags":["shared key","conditional access","allow shared key access"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-025","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure identity-based access for Azure Files","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which statement about identity sources for Azure Files SMB access is correct?","choices":["You can enable on-premises AD DS and Microsoft Entra Kerberos at the same time on one storage account.","Cloud-only identities can authenticate to a share when on-premises AD DS is the identity source.","You can enable only one identity source per storage account, and it applies to all file shares in the account.","Azure Files does not support identity-based authentication for SMB shares."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Azure Files supports three identity sources for SMB access, but only one identity source can be enabled on a storage account at a time, and it applies to all file shares in that account. Cloud-only identities cannot authenticate when the identity source is on-premises AD DS, because those users have no corresponding AD DS account.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-active-directory-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview - Azure Files Identity-Based Authentication","tags":["identity source","azure files","smb","ad ds"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-026","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure identity-based access for Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"Users who exist only in Microsoft Entra ID must access an SMB file share. Their clients are Microsoft Entra-joined, and they have no connectivity to any domain controller. Which identity source should you configure?","choices":["On-premises AD DS.","Microsoft Entra Domain Services.","Microsoft Entra Kerberos.","Storage account key authentication on each client."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Microsoft Entra Kerberos is designed for cloud-only or hybrid environments with Microsoft Entra-joined or Microsoft Entra hybrid-joined clients, and it does not require the clients to have domain-controller connectivity. AD DS requires the clients to reach domain controllers, and Microsoft Entra Domain Services requires the clients to be joined to the managed domain, so neither fits this scenario.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-identity-auth-hybrid-identities-enable","sourceTitle":"Microsoft Entra Kerberos Authentication for Azure Files","tags":["microsoft entra kerberos","cloud-only identity","azure files","smb"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-027","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure identity-based access for Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"A storage account has on-premises AD DS configured as the identity source for Azure Files. A user who was created directly in the Microsoft Entra admin center and has no AD DS account tries to access the share. What is the result?","choices":["Authentication fails because only hybrid identities that exist in AD DS and are synchronized to Microsoft Entra ID can be authorized.","The user is authenticated as an anonymous identity with read-only access.","The user is authenticated with the storage account key instead.","The user is authenticated as a Microsoft Entra guest account."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the identity source is on-premises AD DS, the share-level permission is assigned to the Microsoft Entra identity while the file and directory permission is enforced with the AD DS account, so only users that exist in both directories can authenticate and be authorized. A cloud-only user has no AD DS counterpart and therefore cannot access the share; that user requires Microsoft Entra Domain Services or Microsoft Entra Kerberos.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-active-directory-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview - Azure Files Identity-Based Authentication","tags":["ad ds","hybrid identity","cloud-only identity","azure files"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-028","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure identity-based access for Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which Azure built-in role grants read, write, delete, and modify-ACLs access on files and directories in Azure Files?","choices":["Storage File Data SMB Share Reader","Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor","Storage Blob Data Contributor","Storage File Data SMB Share Elevated Contributor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Storage File Data SMB Share Elevated Contributor grants read, write, delete and modify-ACLs access on files and directories in Azure Files, which is similar to a change ACL on a Windows file server. Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor grants read, write and delete but not modify-ACLs, and Storage Blob Data Contributor applies to blob data rather than file shares.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-identity-assign-share-level-permissions","sourceTitle":"Assign Share-Level Permissions for Azure Files","tags":["azure files","rbac role","smb share","elevated contributor"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-029","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure identity-based access for Azure Files","difficulty":"hard","question":"You want to assign the Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor role to a user for a specific file share. Which configuration must be completed on the storage account first?","choices":["A private endpoint for the file service.","The Allow Blob public access setting must be enabled.","An identity source for identity-based authentication must be enabled on the storage account.","A stored access policy must be created on the share."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Share-level Azure Files roles such as Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor take effect only after identity-based data access is enabled at the storage account level by configuring an identity source, such as AD DS, Microsoft Entra Domain Services or Microsoft Entra Kerberos. Without that prerequisite the role assignment exists in IAM but has no functional effect when a user connects over SMB.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-identity-assign-share-level-permissions","sourceTitle":"Assign Share-Level Permissions for Azure Files","tags":["azure files","identity-based access","prerequisite","rbac"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-030","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure identity-based access for Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"A storage account uses on-premises AD DS as the identity source for Azure Files. What must be true for a hybrid user to access the share?","choices":["The user account must exist only in Microsoft Entra ID.","The user must exist in on-premises AD DS and be synchronized to Microsoft Entra ID, and the share-level permission must be assigned to the Microsoft Entra ID identity.","The user must possess the storage account access key.","The user must be assigned the Owner role on the storage account."],"answer":1,"explanation":"With AD DS authentication the user must exist in both on-premises AD DS and Microsoft Entra ID, because the share-level permission is configured against the identity represented in Microsoft Entra ID while the directory and file-level permission is enforced in AD DS. The account access key and the Owner role are not part of identity-based Azure Files access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-active-directory-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview - Azure Files Identity-Based Authentication","tags":["ad ds","hybrid identity","share-level permission","azure files"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-031","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure storage accounts","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which storage account type supports Blob Storage, Azure Files, Queue Storage, and Table Storage?","choices":["Standard general-purpose v2","Premium block blobs","Premium file shares","Premium page blobs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A standard general-purpose v2 account supports Azure Blob Storage, Azure Files, Queue Storage and Table Storage, which makes it the recommended account type for most scenarios. The premium account types are specialized: premium block blobs supports only blob data, premium file shares supports only file shares, and premium page blobs supports only page blobs.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-overview","sourceTitle":"Storage Account Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["general-purpose v2","storage account type","storage services"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-032","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure storage accounts","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which storage account setting cannot be changed after the account is created?","choices":["The default access tier.","The storage account type, for example standard general-purpose v2.","The redundancy option.","The firewall and virtual network rules."],"answer":1,"explanation":"You cannot change a storage account to a different type after it is created; to move data to another account type you must create a new account and copy the data. The default access tier, the redundancy option and the network rules can all be modified on an existing account.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-overview","sourceTitle":"Storage Account Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["storage account type","immutable settings","account kind"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-033","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure storage accounts","difficulty":"medium","question":"Traffic from internet clients must be handed off to the transit ISP at the point of presence closest to the storage account in order to lower networking costs. Which setting should you change?","choices":["The storage account's minimum TLS version.","The default action of the storage account firewall.","The storage account's redundancy option.","The network routing preference, set to Internet routing."],"answer":3,"explanation":"By default, clients outside Azure reach a storage account over the Microsoft global network, entering at the point of presence closest to the client. Setting the network routing preference to Internet routing directs both inbound and outbound traffic through the point of presence closest to the storage account, minimizing traversal of the Microsoft global network and lowering networking costs.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/network-routing-preference","sourceTitle":"Network routing preference - Azure Storage","tags":["routing preference","internet routing","network costs"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-034","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure storage accounts","difficulty":"medium","question":"A storage account must support Azure Data Lake Storage and provide a hierarchical namespace for analytics workloads. What should you configure?","choices":["Enable the NFS 3.0 protocol on a premium block blob account.","Add a private endpoint to the storage account.","Configure object replication to a second account.","Enable the hierarchical namespace on a standard general-purpose v2 storage account."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Data Lake Storage capabilities and features such as the NFS 3.0 protocol require blobs to be organized into a hierarchical namespace, which you enable when you create a general-purpose v2 storage account. A private endpoint or object replication changes networking or data distribution and does not provide a hierarchical namespace.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/network-file-system-protocol-support","sourceTitle":"Network File System 3.0 Support for Azure Blob Storage - Azure Storage","tags":["hierarchical namespace","data lake storage","nfs 3.0"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-035","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure storage accounts","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which storage account name is valid?","choices":["contosostorage01","ContosoStorage","contoso_storage_01","contoso-storage-01"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Storage account names must be between 3 and 24 characters long and can contain only numbers and lowercase letters. Names containing uppercase letters, underscores or hyphens are rejected, and the name must also be unique across Azure.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-overview","sourceTitle":"Storage Account Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["storage account name","naming rules","constraints"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-036","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure storage accounts","difficulty":"hard","question":"You must ensure that data in a new storage account is encrypted twice, with two different encryption algorithms and two different keys. Which option must you enable when the account is created?","choices":["Customer-managed keys stored in Azure Key Vault.","A default encryption scope for the account.","The minimum TLS version set to 1.2.","Infrastructure encryption."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Infrastructure encryption adds a second layer of 256-bit AES encryption at the storage infrastructure level with a different key and algorithm from the service-level encryption, which results in double encryption of the data. Customer-managed keys and encryption scopes only change which key protects the single service-level encryption layer, and the TLS version protects data in transit rather than at rest.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/infrastructure-encryption-enable","sourceTitle":"Enable infrastructure encryption for double encryption of data - Azure Storage","tags":["infrastructure encryption","double encryption","data at rest"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-037","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure storage accounts","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to create an SMB file share in a new storage account. Which account type should you use?","choices":["Premium block blobs","Standard general-purpose v2","Premium page blobs","A legacy Blob Storage account"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A standard general-purpose v2 account supports Azure Files together with blobs, queues and tables, so an SMB file share can be created in it. Premium block blobs accounts support only block and append blobs, premium page blobs accounts support only page blobs, and legacy Blob Storage accounts do not support Azure Files.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-overview","sourceTitle":"Storage Account Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["azure files","storage account type","smb file share"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-038","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which redundancy option replicates data synchronously across three or more Azure availability zones in the primary region?","choices":["Locally redundant storage (LRS)","Geo-redundant storage (GRS)","Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)","Read-access geo-redundant storage (RA-GRS)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ZRS copies data synchronously across three or more availability zones in the primary region, so data remains available for read and write operations if a zone becomes unavailable. LRS replicates within a single datacenter, and GRS and RA-GRS replicate to a secondary region but use LRS within the primary region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-redundancy","sourceTitle":"Data redundancy - Azure Storage","tags":["zrs","availability zone","redundancy"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-039","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"easy","question":"An application must be able to read data from the secondary region while the primary region is fully healthy. Which redundancy option should you configure?","choices":["Read-access geo-redundant storage (RA-GRS)","Locally redundant storage (LRS)","Geo-redundant storage (GRS)","Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With GRS and GZRS the data in the secondary region is not available for read or write access unless a failover occurs, so only RA-GRS or RA-GZRS permit reads from the secondary region at any time. LRS and ZRS keep data in the primary region only.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-redundancy","sourceTitle":"Data redundancy - Azure Storage","tags":["ra-grs","secondary region","read access","redundancy"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-040","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need protection against the failure of an availability zone in the primary region and also protection against a regional disaster. Which redundancy option should you choose?","choices":["Locally redundant storage (LRS)","Geo-redundant storage (GRS)","Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)","Geo-zone-redundant storage (GZRS)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GZRS copies data synchronously across three or more availability zones in the primary region and also replicates it asynchronously to a secondary region, so it protects against both a zone failure and a regional disaster. GRS and ZRS each provide only one of those two protections, and LRS protects data only within a single datacenter.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-redundancy","sourceTitle":"Data redundancy - Azure Storage","tags":["gzrs","zone failure","regional disaster","redundancy"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-041","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which redundancy option replicates data only within a single physical datacenter in the primary region?","choices":["Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)","Locally redundant storage (LRS)","Geo-redundant storage (GRS)","Geo-zone-redundant storage (GZRS)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"LRS replicates your data to a single physical datacenter in the primary region, which protects against drive, server and rack failures but not against the loss of the datacenter itself. ZRS spreads data across availability zones, and GRS and GZRS also replicate to a secondary region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-redundancy","sourceTitle":"Data redundancy - Azure Storage","tags":["lrs","single datacenter","redundancy"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-042","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"easy","question":"You are designing redundancy for an Azure Files workload that requires high availability, and file share clients must not have to remount if an availability zone becomes unavailable. Which redundancy option does Microsoft recommend?","choices":["Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)","Locally redundant storage (LRS)","Read-access geo-redundant storage (RA-GRS)","Premium page blobs with LRS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Microsoft recommends ZRS for Azure Files workloads, and with ZRS no remounting of Azure file shares from connected clients is required if a zone becomes unavailable. LRS offers no zone protection, and Azure Files does not support read-access geo-redundant storage.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-redundancy","sourceTitle":"Data redundancy - Azure Storage","tags":["zrs","azure files","high availability","remount"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-043","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"medium","question":"A storage account configured for GRS has data in a secondary region. The primary region becomes unavailable and you need to restore write access and promote the secondary region. What should you do?","choices":["Initiate a storage account failover.","Change the redundancy setting to LRS.","Change the redundancy setting to ZRS.","Regenerate both storage account access keys."],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the primary region becomes unavailable, you can fail over to the secondary region; the failover updates the DNS entry so that the secondary region endpoints become the new primary endpoints, after which you can read and write data. Changing the redundancy setting does not promote the secondary region, and regenerating keys has no effect on regional availability.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-initiate-account-failover","sourceTitle":"Initiate a storage account failover - Azure Storage","tags":["failover","disaster recovery","secondary region","grs"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-044","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"hard","question":"You plan to move blobs to the archive access tier. Which redundancy options support that tier?","choices":["ZRS only","GZRS and RA-GZRS only","All redundancy options","LRS, GRS, and RA-GRS only"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Only storage accounts configured for LRS, GRS or RA-GRS support moving blobs to the archive tier; the archive tier is not supported for ZRS, GZRS or RA-GZRS accounts. To change the redundancy configuration of an account that already contains archived blobs, you must first rehydrate those blobs to an online tier.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["archive tier","redundancy","lrs","gzrs"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-045","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure Azure Storage redundancy","difficulty":"hard","question":"For a storage account configured with geo-redundant storage, how is data replicated within the secondary region?","choices":["Across three or more availability zones using ZRS.","Asynchronously, using geo-redundant replication.","It is not replicated within the secondary region.","Synchronously, using locally redundant storage."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The primary difference between GRS and GZRS is how data is replicated in the primary region; within the secondary region, data is always replicated synchronously by using LRS. That LRS layer protects the secondary copy against hardware failures but not against the loss of a datacenter in the secondary region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-redundancy","sourceTitle":"Data redundancy - Azure Storage","tags":["grz","secondary region","lrs","synchronous replication"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-046","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure object replication","difficulty":"easy","question":"What does Azure Storage object replication do?","choices":["Synchronously copies blobs between two accounts in the same region.","Asynchronously copies block blobs from a source storage account to a destination storage account.","Replicates Azure file shares between two regions.","Replicates the storage account configuration between subscriptions."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Object replication asynchronously copies block blobs between a source storage account and a destination account according to the rules you configure. It does not apply to Azure file shares, queues or tables, and it does not replicate account configuration.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/object-replication-overview","sourceTitle":"Object replication overview - Azure Storage","tags":["object replication","block blob","asynchronous"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-047","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure object replication","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which storage account features must be enabled before you can configure object replication?","choices":["Container soft delete on the destination account only.","Static website hosting on both accounts.","Change feed on the source account, and blob versioning on both the source and destination accounts.","Lifecycle management on both accounts."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Object replication requires the change feed to be enabled on the source account so that Azure Storage can detect which blobs changed, and blob versioning to be enabled on both the source and destination accounts. Soft delete, static website hosting and lifecycle management are not prerequisites.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/object-replication-overview","sourceTitle":"Object replication overview - Azure Storage","tags":["object replication","change feed","blob versioning","prerequisites"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-048","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure object replication","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which storage account types are supported as the destination of an object replication policy?","choices":["Any storage account type.","General-purpose v2 or premium block blob accounts.","Legacy Blob Storage accounts only.","Accounts with a hierarchical namespace enabled."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Object replication supports general-purpose v2 storage accounts and premium block blob accounts, and both the source and destination accounts must be one of those two types. Accounts with a hierarchical namespace enabled are not supported for object replication.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/object-replication-overview","sourceTitle":"Object replication overview - Azure Storage","tags":["object replication","account type","general-purpose v2","destination"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-049","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure object replication","difficulty":"hard","question":"Which blob types does object replication support?","choices":["Block blobs only; append blobs and page blobs are not supported.","Block blobs and page blobs.","All blob types, including append and page blobs.","Page blobs only."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Object replication supports block blobs only and does not support append blobs or page blobs. It also does not replicate blob snapshots, although it does copy the blob's metadata and properties, and it can copy index tags when tag replication is configured.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/object-replication-overview","sourceTitle":"Object replication overview - Azure Storage","tags":["object replication","block blob","append blob","page blob"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-050","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure object replication","difficulty":"medium","question":"An object replication policy is active for a destination container. What happens when an application attempts a write operation to that destination container?","choices":["The write succeeds and is replicated back to the source container.","The write succeeds only if the blob names match an existing source blob.","The write is redirected to the source container automatically.","The write fails with error 409 (Conflict) until replication is disabled for that container."],"answer":3,"explanation":"After a replication policy is created, write operations to the destination container are not permitted and any attempt fails with error code 409 (Conflict). To write to the destination container you must first disable replication by deleting that rule or removing the entire replication policy, although read and delete operations on the destination container are permitted while the policy is active.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/object-replication-overview","sourceTitle":"Object replication overview - Azure Storage","tags":["object replication","destination container","write blocked","409"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-051","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage account encryption","difficulty":"easy","question":"Can Azure Storage service-side encryption be disabled for a storage account?","choices":["Yes, by turning off encryption in the storage account configuration.","No, Azure Storage encryption is always enabled and cannot be disabled.","Yes, but only for premium performance tiers.","Yes, but only for blobs stored in the archive tier."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Storage encryption is enabled for all storage accounts and cannot be disabled; data is encrypted and decrypted transparently by using 256-bit AES encryption. Data is encrypted regardless of performance tier or access tier, so there is no account type or blob tier for which encryption can be turned off.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-service-encryption","sourceTitle":"Azure Storage encryption for data at rest","tags":["service-side encryption","aes-256","data at rest"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-052","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage account encryption","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which key types can be used as customer-managed keys for Azure Storage encryption?","choices":["RSA and RSA-HSM keys only.","Elliptic curve (EC) and EC-HSM keys.","AES and 3DES symmetric keys.","8192-bit RSA keys only."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Azure Storage encryption supports RSA and RSA-HSM keys with sizes of 2048, 3072 and 4096 bits, which must be stored in Azure Key Vault or Key Vault Managed HSM. Elliptic curve keys and symmetric algorithms such as AES or 3DES are not supported, and 8192-bit RSA keys are not among the supported sizes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-encryption-keys-portal","sourceTitle":"Configure customer-managed keys in the same tenant for an existing storage account - Azure Storage","tags":["customer-managed key","rsa","key vault","rsa-hsm"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-053","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage account encryption","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which settings must be enabled on the Azure key vault that stores the customer-managed keys for a storage account?","choices":["Firewall rules that allow access from all networks.","A stored access policy on the key vault.","Diagnostic settings that export key usage logs.","Soft delete and purge protection."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Using customer-managed keys with Azure Storage encryption requires that both soft delete and purge protection be enabled for the key vault, which prevents accidental or malicious deletion of the key. Network access rules, stored access policies and diagnostic settings are unrelated to this requirement.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-encryption-keys-portal","sourceTitle":"Configure customer-managed keys in the same tenant for an existing storage account - Azure Storage","tags":["key vault","soft delete","purge protection","customer-managed key"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-054","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage account encryption","difficulty":"medium","question":"To which resources can an encryption scope be applied?","choices":["All services in the storage account, including files, queues, and tables.","Blob containers and the blobs within them only.","Azure Files and Blob Storage within the same account.","Queues and tables only."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encryption scopes are a Blob storage feature and are used to manage encryption with a key scoped to a container or an individual blob, creating secure boundaries between data in the same account. Azure Files, queues and tables are encrypted with the account-level key and do not support encryption scope assignment.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/encryption-scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Encryption scopes for Blob storage - Azure Storage","tags":["encryption scope","blob container","key management"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-055","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage account encryption","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need one container in a storage account to be encrypted with a key that is different from the rest of the account. What must you create before you create the container?","choices":["An encryption scope in the storage account.","A second storage account in the same region.","A stored access policy on the container.","A customer-provided key that is supplied on every blob upload."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A default encryption scope for a container must be specified at the time the container is created, and the scope must be defined in the storage account first, associated with the key from Key Vault. A scope can only be used within the account where it was created, and a stored access policy governs authorization rather than encryption.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/encryption-scope-overview","sourceTitle":"Encryption scopes for Blob storage - Azure Storage","tags":["encryption scope","container","customer-managed key","order of operations"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-056","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage data by using Azure Storage Explorer and AzCopy","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which operating systems are supported for AzCopy v10?","choices":["Windows, Linux, and macOS.","Windows only.","Windows and Linux only.","Linux only."],"answer":0,"explanation":"AzCopy v10 is supported on versions of Windows, Linux and macOS that are officially maintained. On Linux you can install AzCopy by using a package manager, while on other operating systems you download a portable binary.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-use-azcopy-v10","sourceTitle":"Copy or move data to Azure Storage by using AzCopy v10","tags":["azcopy","operating system","platform support"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-057","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage data by using Azure Storage Explorer and AzCopy","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to create a new blob container by using AzCopy. Which command should you use?","choices":["azcopy copy","azcopy sync","azcopy make","azcopy list"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The azcopy make command creates a container or file share, and the URL must use the correct service endpoint, such as https://account.blob.core.windows.net/containername for a blob container. The azcopy copy command transfers data, azcopy sync replicates a source location to a destination, and azcopy list enumerates entities.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-use-azcopy-v10","sourceTitle":"Copy or move data to Azure Storage by using AzCopy v10","tags":["azcopy make","blob container","azcopy commands"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-058","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage data by using Azure Storage Explorer and AzCopy","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to transfer only the files that have changed since the last transfer between a local directory and a blob container. Which AzCopy command should you use?","choices":["azcopy copy","azcopy make","azcopy bench","azcopy sync"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The azcopy sync command replicates the source location to the destination location and transfers only the content that differs, which makes it suitable for ongoing synchronization. The azcopy copy command performs a straight copy, azcopy make creates a container or share, and azcopy bench runs a performance benchmark.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-use-azcopy-v10","sourceTitle":"Copy or move data to Azure Storage by using AzCopy v10","tags":["azcopy sync","incremental transfer","azcopy commands"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-059","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage data by using Azure Storage Explorer and AzCopy","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must copy a local folder and all of its subdirectories to a blob container by using AzCopy. Which option should you include in the command?","choices":["--snapshot","--blob-tags","--s2s-preserve-access-tier","--recursive"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The --recursive option copies all files and subdirectories from the specified local directory, so omitting it copies only the top level of the folder. The --blob-tags option adds index tags, and --s2s-preserve-access-tier controls tier handling in server-to-server copies rather than directory recursion.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-use-azcopy-v10","sourceTitle":"Copy or move data to Azure Storage by using AzCopy v10","tags":["azcopy copy","recursive","local folder upload"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-060","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Manage data by using Azure Storage Explorer and AzCopy","difficulty":"hard","question":"You plan to copy blobs between two storage accounts by using AzCopy with Microsoft Entra authorization for both the source and destination. Which condition must be satisfied?","choices":["Both accounts must share the same access key.","Both accounts must be located in the same Azure region.","Both accounts must belong to the same Microsoft Entra tenant.","Both accounts must have Shared Key access disabled."],"answer":2,"explanation":"If you use Microsoft Entra authorization for both source and destination, both accounts must belong to the same Microsoft Entra tenant, and the client must have network access to both accounts. The accounts may be in different regions or subscriptions, and they may use different access keys or have Shared Key enabled.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-use-azcopy-blobs-copy","sourceTitle":"Copy blobs between Azure storage accounts with AzCopy v10","tags":["azcopy","microsoft entra id","cross-account copy","tenant"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-061","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a file share in Azure Files","difficulty":"easy","question":"Users must map a drive to an Azure file share from computers that are outside Azure. Which outbound TCP port must be open on the client for the SMB protocol to work?","choices":["Port 22","Port 443","Port 445","Port 3389"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The SMB protocol requires TCP port 445 to be open and connections fail if port 445 is blocked, which is a common problem with some corporate firewalls and ISPs. If you cannot open port 445 from outside Azure, you can tunnel SMB traffic over a point-to-site VPN instead; port 443 is used for HTTPS and port 3389 for RDP.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-how-to-use-files-windows","sourceTitle":"Mount SMB Azure File Share on Windows","tags":["azure files","smb","port 445","drive mapping"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-062","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a file share in Azure Files","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which UNC path correctly references a file share named data in a storage account named contosostorage?","choices":["\\\\contosostorage.blob.core.windows.net\\data","\\\\data.contosostorage.file.core.windows.net","\\\\contosostorage.file.core.windows.net\\data","\\\\contosostorage.file.core.windows.net.blob\\data"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An Azure file share is referenced as \\\\<storage-account-name>.file.core.windows.net\\<file-share-name>, so the storage account name comes first, followed by the file service endpoint and then the share name. Using blob.core.windows.net addresses Blob Storage rather than Azure Files, so that path would not work for a file share.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-how-to-use-files-windows","sourceTitle":"Mount SMB Azure File Share on Windows","tags":["azure files","unc path","file endpoint","net use"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-063","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a file share in Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to create file shares that support both the SMB protocol and the NFS protocol. Which storage account type should you use?","choices":["Premium block blobs","Premium page blobs","Premium file shares","A legacy Blob Storage account"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Premium file shares is the premium storage account type for file shares only, and it is the account type to use when you need a storage account that supports both SMB and NFS file shares. Premium block blobs supports only block and append blobs, premium page blobs supports only page blobs, and Blob Storage accounts do not support Azure Files at all.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-overview","sourceTitle":"Storage Account Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["premium file shares","smb","nfs","storage account type"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-064","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a file share in Azure Files","difficulty":"hard","question":"An application that runs on an Azure virtual machine must access an Azure file share over SMB without using the storage account key. What should you configure?","choices":["A stored access policy on the share.","Anonymous access on the share.","A shared access signature scoped to the share and renewed daily.","A managed identity for the application, with an Azure role assignment granting access to the storage account."],"answer":3,"explanation":"For applications and Azure compute workloads that need keyless SMB access, Microsoft recommends using a managed identity, which is separate from the three user identity sources and can coexist with them on the same account. Stored access policies and SAS tokens are used with REST access rather than SMB, and anonymous access is not an identity-based mechanism.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-active-directory-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview - Azure Files Identity-Based Authentication","tags":["managed identity","keyless smb","azure files","rbac"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-065","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a file share in Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"In addition to a share-level Azure Files role, which role must a user be assigned to read and access the file share by using Azure Storage Explorer?","choices":["Storage Blob Data Reader","Reader and Data Access","Storage Account Contributor","Storage File Data SMB Admin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Several built-in roles grant share-level permissions for Azure Files, and if you are using Azure Storage Explorer you also need the Reader and Data Access role to read and access the file share. Storage Blob Data Reader applies to blob data rather than to file shares, and Storage File Data SMB Admin grants administrative SMB access equivalent to the account key rather than browsing access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-identity-assign-share-level-permissions","sourceTitle":"Assign Share-Level Permissions for Azure Files","tags":["azure storage explorer","reader and data access","file share rbac"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-066","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a file share in Azure Files","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to create an Azure file share. Which prerequisite must be satisfied?","choices":["The file share must be created in a storage account that supports Azure Files, such as a standard general-purpose v2 account or a premium file shares account.","The file share must be created in a Blob Storage account.","The file share must be created in a premium block blobs account.","The file share must be created in an Azure App Service plan."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Azure file shares can only be created in storage account types that support Azure Files, such as standard general-purpose v2 or premium file shares. Blob Storage accounts and premium block blobs accounts support only blob data, so a file share cannot be created in them.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-overview","sourceTitle":"Storage Account Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["azure files","file share","storage account type","prerequisite"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-067","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a container in Azure Blob Storage","difficulty":"medium","question":"A regulatory requirement states that blobs written to a container must not be modified or deleted for a defined period. Which container setting should you configure?","choices":["The container's access tier.","A time-based retention immutability policy on the container.","Blob soft delete with a long retention period.","The container's anonymous access level."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Immutable storage for Blob Storage stores data in a write once, read many state, and a time-based retention policy prevents modification or deletion of objects for a user-specified interval. Soft delete retains deleted data for recovery but does not prevent deletion, while access tier and anonymous access settings control cost and read authorization respectively.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/storage-blob-immutable-storage","sourceTitle":"Immutable Storage for Blob Data Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["immutability","time-based retention","worm","container"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-068","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a container in Azure Blob Storage","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need blobs in a single container to be readable by clients that do not send an Authorization header. What must you configure?","choices":["Anonymous access must be allowed on the storage account, and the container's anonymous access level must be set to Blob or Container.","Only the container's anonymous access level must be set; the storage account setting has no effect.","A stored access policy must be created on the container.","Shared Key authorization must be disabled on the storage account."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two settings affect anonymous access: the account-level setting that allows or disallows anonymous access, and the container's anonymous access setting, which is disabled by default. Blob data is never available for anonymous access unless a user with appropriate permissions also takes the extra step of configuring the container's anonymous access setting.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/anonymous-read-access-configure","sourceTitle":"Configure anonymous read access for containers and blobs - Azure Storage","tags":["anonymous access","blob container","public access level"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-069","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a container in Azure Blob Storage","difficulty":"medium","question":"A container's anonymous access level is set to Blob, but anonymous access is disallowed at the storage account level. What is the result for clients that send no Authorization header?","choices":["Blobs in the container can be read anonymously.","Only the container listing is publicly readable.","Clients receive a 401 error because anonymous requests are challenged for credentials.","No anonymous access is permitted, because the storage account setting overrides the container setting."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disallowing anonymous access for the storage account overrides the access settings for all containers in that account, so no anonymous access is possible regardless of the container configuration. Clients using the anonymous bearer challenge receive a 403 (Forbidden) rather than a 401 response in this situation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/anonymous-read-access-configure","sourceTitle":"Configure anonymous read access for containers and blobs - Azure Storage","tags":["anonymous access","account level setting","container override","403"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-070","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a container in Azure Blob Storage","difficulty":"hard","question":"Legal counsel requires that blobs added to a container stay unmodifiable for exactly one year and that the interval cannot be shortened afterwards. What should you configure?","choices":["Blob soft delete with a 365-day retention period.","Blob versioning together with container soft delete.","An Azure Resource Manager lock on the storage account.","A time-based retention immutability policy on the container, and then lock the policy."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A time-based retention immutability policy protects blobs in a container from modification and deletion for the configured interval, and a locked policy cannot be deleted and its retention period can only be extended, never decreased. Soft delete merely retains deleted data for recovery, versioning preserves previous states of overwritten blobs, and a resource lock protects the account rather than the blob data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/storage-blob-immutable-storage","sourceTitle":"Immutable Storage for Blob Data Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["immutability","locked policy","retention interval","worm"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-071","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a container in Azure Blob Storage","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which immutability policy keeps blobs in a write once, read many state until it is explicitly cleared, without a defined retention period?","choices":["A time-based retention policy.","An Azure Resource Manager lock.","A legal hold.","Blob soft delete."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A legal hold stores blob data in a WORM format until the hold is explicitly cleared and has no retention period, which makes it suitable when the period for which data must be kept is unknown. A time-based retention policy uses a fixed interval, and soft delete only retains deleted data for a configured retention window.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/storage-blob-immutable-storage","sourceTitle":"Immutable Storage for Blob Data Overview - Azure Storage","tags":["legal hold","worm","immutability","container"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-072","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Create and configure a container in Azure Blob Storage","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to categorize blobs with key-value pairs so that lifecycle rules and queries can target them by category. What should you use?","choices":["Blob metadata.","Blob index tags.","Container properties.","The access tier of the container."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Blob index tags are key-value pairs that can be used to categorize and filter blobs, and a lifecycle management policy can use blob index tag conditions in its filters. Metadata describes the object but is not searchable in the same way, and access tiers control storage cost rather than classification.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management overview - Azure Blob Storage","tags":["blob index tags","classification","lifecycle filter"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-073","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage tiers","difficulty":"easy","question":"Blobs contain compliance data that is rarely accessed and can tolerate hours of retrieval latency. In which access tier should you store the blobs to minimize storage cost?","choices":["Hot","Cool","Cold","Archive"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The archive tier is an offline tier for data that is rarely accessed and that has flexible latency requirements, and it offers the lowest storage cost while having the highest access and transaction costs. The hot, cool and cold tiers are all online tiers with millisecond read latency and higher capacity costs than archive.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["archive tier","access tier selection","cost optimization"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-074","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage tiers","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the minimum recommended data retention period for a blob in the cool tier of a general-purpose v2 storage account?","choices":["30 days","60 days","90 days","180 days"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Data in the cool tier of a general-purpose v2 account should be stored for a minimum of 30 days, and a blob deleted, overwritten or moved to a different tier before that period elapses is subject to a prorated early deletion penalty. The cold tier uses a 90-day minimum and the archive tier a 180-day minimum.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["cool tier","minimum retention","early deletion"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-075","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage tiers","difficulty":"easy","question":"A blob is currently in the archive tier. What must you do before you can read its content?","choices":["Rehydrate the blob to an online tier such as hot, cool, or cold.","Change the storage account redundancy to ZRS.","Enable blob versioning on the storage account.","Enable blob soft delete on the storage account."],"answer":0,"explanation":"While a blob is in the archive tier it cannot be read or modified, and you must first rehydrate it to an online tier, a process that can take up to 15 hours depending on the rehydration priority you choose. Versioning and soft delete are data protection features, and changing redundancy does not make archived blob data readable.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["archive tier","rehydration","offline tier","latency"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-076","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage tiers","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which statement about blob access tiers is correct?","choices":["The access tier can be set on page blobs and append blobs.","Setting the access tier is only allowed on block blobs.","The archive tier can be set as the default access tier of a storage account.","A blob in the archive tier can be read directly without changing its tier."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Setting an access tier is supported only for block blobs and is not supported for page blobs or append blobs. The archive tier also cannot be used as a default access tier for a storage account, and archived blobs cannot be read until they are rehydrated to an online tier.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["access tier","block blob","default access tier","archive"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-077","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage tiers","difficulty":"hard","question":"A blob was moved to the archive tier 45 days ago and is now deleted. What is the billing effect?","choices":["No charge applies because archive blobs have no minimum storage duration.","Only transaction charges apply for the delete operation.","The blob is exempt from charges because soft delete is enabled on the account.","An early deletion charge applies for the remaining 135 days of the 180-day minimum."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Data must remain in the archive tier for at least 180 days or be subject to an early deletion charge, and the charge is prorated for the days the blob did not remain. Deleting a blob 45 days after it entered the archive tier therefore incurs a charge equivalent to 135 days of archive storage.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["archive tier","early deletion penalty","180 days","billing"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-078","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage tiers","difficulty":"medium","question":"A blob has been in the cool tier for only 10 days and must now be moved to the hot tier. Which approach should you use to avoid an early deletion penalty?","choices":["Call the Set Blob Tier operation.","Delete the blob and upload it again to the hot tier.","Copy the blob to a new blob in the hot tier by using the Copy Blob operation.","Leave the blob in the cool tier for the full 30 days before moving it."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Copying a blob lets you avoid the early deletion penalty when the required storage interval for the source blob has not yet elapsed, although it results in capacity charges for both the source and destination blobs. Set Blob Tier is recommended for moving to a cooler tier, and deleting the blob would itself trigger the penalty.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["copy blob","early deletion penalty","rehydration","tier change"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-079","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure storage tiers","difficulty":"hard","question":"Which mechanism cannot be used to rehydrate a blob from the archive tier to an online tier?","choices":["Calling the Copy Blob operation.","Calling Set Blob Tier to move the blob to hot, cool, or cold.","Using the change tier option in the Azure portal.","Using a lifecycle management policy."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A lifecycle management policy cannot be used to rehydrate a blob from the archive tier to an online tier, which is an explicit documented limitation of lifecycle management. Rehydration is instead performed by calling Set Blob Tier or Copy Blob, both of which can be invoked from the Azure portal and other tools.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview","sourceTitle":"Access tiers for blob data - Azure Storage","tags":["rehydration","lifecycle management","archive tier","set blob tier"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-080","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure soft delete for blobs and containers","difficulty":"easy","question":"What does blob soft delete protect against?","choices":["Deletion of the entire storage account.","Accidental deletion or overwrite of a blob, by retaining the deleted data for a specified period.","Permanent data loss caused by the failure of an Azure region.","Accidental modification of file share permissions."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Blob soft delete keeps a deleted or overwritten blob, snapshot or version in the system for a specified retention period, during which the object can be restored. It does not protect the storage account itself from deletion, which requires a resource lock, and it does not protect against regional outages, which is the role of redundancy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/soft-delete-blob-overview","sourceTitle":"Soft Delete for Blobs to Recover Data - Azure Storage","tags":["soft delete","data protection","restore","blob"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-081","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure soft delete for blobs and containers","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which retention period can you configure for blob soft delete on a storage account?","choices":["Between 1 and 30 days","Between 1 and 365 days","Between 7 and 365 days","Between 1 and 100 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When you enable blob soft delete you specify a retention period for deleted or overwritten objects of between 1 and 365 days, and the clock starts as soon as the object is deleted or overwritten. During the retention period you can restore the object by calling the Undelete Blob operation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/soft-delete-blob-overview","sourceTitle":"Soft Delete for Blobs to Recover Data - Azure Storage","tags":["soft delete","retention period","blob recovery"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-082","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure soft delete for blobs and containers","difficulty":"medium","question":"A container and all of the blobs inside it were deleted by mistake and must be recoverable within seven days. Which feature must you enable?","choices":["Blob versioning.","Blob soft delete only.","An immutability policy on the container.","Container soft delete."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Container soft delete lets you restore a deleted container and its contents within the retention period, and Microsoft recommends enabling it for all storage accounts with a minimum retention interval of seven days. Blob soft delete can restore an individual blob but not a deleted container, and immutability policies protect data from modification rather than enabling recovery.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/data-protection-overview","sourceTitle":"Data protection overview - Azure Storage","tags":["container soft delete","restore container","data protection"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-083","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure soft delete for blobs and containers","difficulty":"medium","question":"You shorten the blob soft delete retention period of a storage account. How is the change applied?","choices":["The new period applies only to data deleted after the change; data deleted earlier keeps the retention period that was in effect when it was deleted.","The new period applies retroactively to all soft-deleted data.","The retention period cannot be changed once soft delete is enabled.","Changing the retention period permanently deletes all soft-deleted objects."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An updated retention period applies only to data deleted after the retention period was changed, so any data deleted before the change remains subject to the retention period that was in effect when it was deleted. The retention period can be changed at any time, and changing it does not permanently delete existing soft-deleted objects.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/soft-delete-blob-overview","sourceTitle":"Soft Delete for Blobs to Recover Data - Azure Storage","tags":["soft delete","retention period change","blob recovery"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-084","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure soft delete for blobs and containers","difficulty":"hard","question":"Blob soft delete and blob versioning are both enabled on a storage account. An application overwrites an existing blob. What happens?","choices":["A soft-deleted snapshot is created and is removed when the retention period expires.","The previous state of the blob is discarded and cannot be recovered.","A new previous version is created that reflects the blob's state before the write, and that version is not soft-deleted.","Nothing changes, because overwrite operations are not protected by either feature."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When both versioning and soft delete are enabled, overwriting a blob automatically creates a new previous version that reflects the state before the write; that version is not soft-deleted and is not removed when the soft delete retention period expires. No soft-deleted snapshots are created in this configuration, so the snapshot behaviour described in the other option applies only when versioning is not enabled.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/versioning-overview","sourceTitle":"Blob Versioning in Azure Storage - Azure Storage","tags":["blob versioning","soft delete","overwrite","previous version"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-085","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure snapshots and soft delete for Azure Files","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is an Azure Files share snapshot?","choices":["A point-in-time, read-only copy of the file share.","A writable clone of the file share that can be modified independently.","A copy of a single file at a point in time.","A backup of the file share stored in a Recovery Services vault."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A share snapshot captures the share state at that point in time and is a read-only copy, so after you create it you can read, copy or delete it but you cannot modify it. Snapshots are incremental, so only the data that changed since the most recent snapshot is saved.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-snapshots-files","sourceTitle":"Use Azure Files Share Snapshots","tags":["share snapshot","azure files","point in time","read only"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-086","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure snapshots and soft delete for Azure Files","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the default retention period for Azure file share soft delete?","choices":["1 day","30 days","7 days","365 days"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The default retention period for Azure file shares is 7 days, although you can specify any retention period between 1 and 365 days. The retention clock starts when the file share is deleted, and you can change the retention period at any time.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-enable-soft-delete","sourceTitle":"Azure File Share Soft Delete","tags":["azure files","soft delete","default retention","file share"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-087","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure snapshots and soft delete for Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"An individual file was deleted from an Azure file share in a storage account where file share soft delete is enabled. How can the file be restored?","choices":["By using file share soft delete, because it covers individual files as well as shares.","By using blob soft delete on the same storage account.","By using container soft delete on the same storage account.","By restoring from a share snapshot, because file share soft delete works only at the file share level."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Soft delete for Azure files works only at the file share level, so it recovers deleted shares rather than deleted files. To restore a deleted file you must use share snapshots or Azure file share backup; blob and container soft delete apply to Blob Storage and cannot recover data in a file share.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-files-enable-soft-delete","sourceTitle":"Azure File Share Soft Delete","tags":["azure files","soft delete scope","share snapshot","file recovery"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-088","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure snapshots and soft delete for Azure Files","difficulty":"hard","question":"What is the maximum number of snapshots that Azure Files supports per file share?","choices":["100","500","1,000","200"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Files supports up to 200 share snapshots per share, and after reaching that limit you must delete older share snapshots before creating new ones. Snapshots can be retained for up to 10 years, and they do not count towards the maximum share size limit.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-snapshots-files","sourceTitle":"Use Azure Files Share Snapshots","tags":["share snapshot","limit","azure files","retention"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-089","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure snapshots and soft delete for Azure Files","difficulty":"medium","question":"You delete an Azure file share that has three share snapshots. What happens to the snapshots?","choices":["They are retained for 30 days after the share is deleted.","They are converted into soft-deleted files in another share.","They are deleted automatically as part of the delete operation.","They remain available as read-only orphaned snapshots."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Share snapshots persist until you explicitly delete them or until the file share is deleted, and the delete workflow automatically deletes the snapshots when you delete the share. It is not possible to delete a file share and keep its snapshots.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/files/storage-snapshots-files","sourceTitle":"Use Azure Files Share Snapshots","tags":["share snapshot","delete share","azure files"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-090","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob lifecycle management","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which lifecycle management policy action moves blobs to the lowest-cost access tier?","choices":["tierToArchive","tierToCool","tierToHot","delete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The archive tier has the lowest storage cost of the Azure Blob Storage access tiers, so the tierToArchive action is the one that moves blobs to the lowest-cost tier. The delete action removes blobs entirely rather than moving them to a cheaper tier, and tierToHot is not a valid action for reducing cost.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management overview - Azure Blob Storage","tags":["lifecycle management","tierToArchive","archive tier","cost"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-091","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob lifecycle management","difficulty":"easy","question":"A lifecycle rule must move blobs that have not been modified for 45 days to the cool tier. Which condition should you use in the rule?","choices":["daysAfterCreationGreaterThan","daysAfterLastAccessTimeGreaterThan","daysAfterLastTierChangeGreaterThan","daysAfterModificationGreaterThan"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Conditions are based on creation time, last modified time and last accessed time, and a requirement about blobs that have not been updated or modified maps to the last modified time, so daysAfterModificationGreaterThan is correct. daysAfterCreationGreaterThan measures age since creation and daysAfterLastAccessTimeGreaterThan measures reads and writes but requires access time tracking to be enabled.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management overview - Azure Blob Storage","tags":["lifecycle management","rule condition","daysAfterModificationGreaterThan"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-092","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob lifecycle management","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to scope a lifecycle rule to all blobs whose names start with a specific string. Which filter property should you use?","choices":["prefixMatch in the filters section of the rule.","blobIndexMatch in the filters section of the rule.","Setting blobTypes to the container name.","Creating a separate lifecycle policy for each storage account."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Filters limit rule actions to a subset of blobs by using path prefixes and blob tags, so prefixMatch targets blobs by name prefix. blobIndexMatch targets blobs by their index tag values instead, and blobTypes selects which blob types the rule applies to rather than matching a name.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management overview - Azure Blob Storage","tags":["lifecycle management","filters","prefixMatch","scope"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-093","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob lifecycle management","difficulty":"easy","question":"You want a lifecycle rule to act only on blobs that carry a specific index tag value. Which filter property should you use?","choices":["prefixMatch","blobIndexMatch","blobTypes","objectType"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Filters can limit rule actions by using path prefixes and blob tags, and blobIndexMatch is the property that matches blobs by their blob index tag conditions. prefixMatch matches blobs by name prefix instead, and blobTypes restricts the rule to particular blob types such as blockBlob.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management overview - Azure Blob Storage","tags":["lifecycle management","blobIndexMatch","index tags","filters"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-094","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob lifecycle management","difficulty":"medium","question":"You add a new rule to an existing lifecycle management policy. How long can it take before the change takes effect and the first policy run starts?","choices":["The change takes effect immediately.","Up to 5 minutes.","Up to 24 hours.","Up to 7 days."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When you add or edit the rules of a lifecycle policy, it can take up to 24 hours for the changes to go into effect and for the first execution to start. An active policy then processes objects periodically, and a run can also be interrupted if changes are made to the policy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management overview - Azure Blob Storage","tags":["lifecycle management","policy execution","24 hours"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-095","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob lifecycle management","difficulty":"hard","question":"A lifecycle management rule must apply to a subset of blobs within a storage account. To what can a lifecycle management policy be applied?","choices":["Only one container per policy, with no further filtering.","Only blobs that already have an explicit access tier set.","An entire storage account, selected containers, or a subset of blobs identified by name prefix or blob index tag.","Only blobs stored in the archive tier."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A lifecycle management policy can apply rules to an entire storage account, to selected containers, or to a subset of blobs by using name prefixes or blob index tags as filters. Lifecycle management does not affect system containers such as $logs or $web, and it can act on blobs regardless of whether an access tier was explicitly set.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Blob Storage lifecycle management overview - Azure Blob Storage","tags":["lifecycle management","scope","prefix","index tags"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-096","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob versioning","difficulty":"easy","question":"What does blob versioning provide?","choices":["Protection against deletion of the storage account.","Protection against deletion of a container.","Protection against a regional outage.","Automatic previous versions of a blob, so that the effects of an accidental overwrite can be reversed."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Blob versioning automatically maintains previous versions of an object, and a new version is created each time a blob is created or modified, which lets you recover data if it is modified or deleted. Versioning cannot help you recover from the accidental deletion of a storage account or container, which requires a resource lock.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/versioning-overview","sourceTitle":"Blob Versioning in Azure Storage - Azure Storage","tags":["blob versioning","accidental overwrite","data recovery"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-097","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob versioning","difficulty":"easy","question":"How many current versions can a single blob have when blob versioning is enabled?","choices":["One for each write operation performed on the blob.","Only one current version at a time.","One current version per container.","An unlimited number of current versions."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A blob can have only one current version at a time; when the blob is modified, the current version becomes a previous version and a new version is created to capture the updated state. A version ID can identify either the current version or a previous version, and each version has a unique version ID that stays the same for the lifetime of the version.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/versioning-overview","sourceTitle":"Blob Versioning in Azure Storage - Azure Storage","tags":["blob versioning","current version","version id"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-098","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob versioning","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which storage accounts support blob versioning?","choices":["Only accounts that have a hierarchical namespace enabled.","Standard general-purpose v2, premium block blob, and legacy Blob Storage accounts; accounts with a hierarchical namespace are not supported.","All storage account types without exception.","Premium page blob accounts only."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Blob versioning is available for standard general-purpose v2, premium block blob and legacy Blob Storage accounts, while accounts with a hierarchical namespace enabled for Azure Data Lake Storage are not currently supported. Versioning also requires REST API version 2019-10-10 or higher.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/versioning-overview","sourceTitle":"Blob Versioning in Azure Storage - Azure Storage","tags":["blob versioning","supported accounts","hierarchical namespace"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-099","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob versioning","difficulty":"hard","question":"Blob versioning and blob soft delete are both enabled. An application deletes a blob without specifying a version ID. What is the result?","choices":["The current version is permanently deleted immediately.","The current version becomes a previous version and there is no longer a current version; all previous versions are retained.","A soft-deleted snapshot is created and expires when the retention period elapses.","The blob is automatically restored to its previous state."],"answer":1,"explanation":"When a blob is deleted without a version ID, the current version becomes a previous version and no current version remains, while all existing previous versions are preserved. Because versioning is enabled, no soft-deleted snapshots are created, and previous versions are retained until they are explicitly deleted or removed by a lifecycle policy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/versioning-overview","sourceTitle":"Blob Versioning in Azure Storage - Azure Storage","tags":["blob versioning","delete operation","previous version","soft delete"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D2-100","domain":2,"domainName":"Storage","subtopic":"Configure blob versioning","difficulty":"medium","question":"You enable blob versioning on a storage account that stores frequently updated blobs. What is the effect of enabling this feature?","choices":["Previous versions are created only when a blob is deleted, not when it is overwritten.","Every write operation creates a new version, which can result in extra costs, so you should use a lifecycle policy to delete old versions.","Versions are automatically stored in the archive tier to reduce cost.","Versioning is free because version data is not billed."],"answer":1,"explanation":"After you enable blob versioning, every write operation to a blob in that account results in the creation of a new version, and for this reason enabling versioning might result in extra costs. Microsoft recommends using a lifecycle management policy to automatically delete old versions, and versions are not automatically moved to the archive tier.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/versioning-overview","sourceTitle":"Blob Versioning in Azure Storage - Azure Storage","tags":["blob versioning","write operation","cost","lifecycle policy"],"sourceFile":"domain-2-storage.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-001","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Interpret an Azure Resource Manager template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"In an Azure Resource Manager (ARM) template, which section defines values that can be supplied by the person or process that runs the deployment?","choices":["The parameters section","The variables section","The outputs section","The resources section"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Parameters are resolved before the deployment starts, which lets the same template be reused for different environments. Variables are computed inside the template and cannot be supplied at deployment time, and the outputs section only returns values after the deployment finishes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/template-parameters","sourceTitle":"Parameters in templates","tags":["ARM template","parameters","deployment"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-002","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Interpret an Azure Resource Manager template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"An ARM template contains a copy loop where the count property is set to 3 and the resource name is \"[concat('disk', copyIndex())]\". Which resource names are created?","choices":["disk0, disk1, disk2 and disk3","disk1 and disk2","disk1, disk2 and disk3","disk0, disk1 and disk2"],"answer":3,"explanation":"copyIndex() is zero-based, so a loop with a count of 3 iterates with indexes 0, 1 and 2 and creates disk0, disk1 and disk2. Because the loop runs exactly three times, four names are never produced.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/copy-resources","sourceTitle":"Resource iteration in ARM templates","tags":["copy loop","copyIndex","ARM template"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-003","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Interpret an Azure Resource Manager template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need a storage account defined in an ARM template to be deployed only after the virtual network that it uses exists. Which element should you add to the storage account resource?","choices":["\"variables\"","\"condition\"","\"outputs\"","\"dependsOn\""],"answer":3,"explanation":"The dependsOn element declares an explicit deployment dependency and Resource Manager then deploys the resources in dependent order. The condition element only controls whether a resource is deployed at all, and outputs and variables do not control deployment ordering.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/resource-dependency","sourceTitle":"Set deployment order for resources","tags":["dependsOn","ARM template","deployment order"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-004","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Interpret an Azure Resource Manager template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"hard","question":"A Bicep file declares a virtual network with name: 'VNET1' and location: resourceGroup().location. What is the name of the virtual network that is deployed?","choices":["The name of the resource group that contains the virtual network","The Azure region of the resource group that contains the virtual network","A name that combines VNET1 with the region name","VNET1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The name property is the literal string VNET1, so the virtual network always has that name. resourceGroup().location only supplies the Azure region, which is an independent field and is never used to build the resource name.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Bicep?","tags":["Bicep","resource name","resourceGroup().location"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-005","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Interpret an Azure Resource Manager template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"hard","question":"In an ARM template, which expression should you use inside a dependsOn array to reference a network interface that is declared in the same template?","choices":["resourceId('Microsoft.Network/networkInterfaces', 'VM1-nic')","reference('Microsoft.Network/networkInterfaces', 'VM1-nic')","union('Microsoft.Network/networkInterfaces', 'VM1-nic')","copyIndex('Microsoft.Network/networkInterfaces', 'VM1-nic')"],"answer":0,"explanation":"resourceId() returns the fully qualified resource ID that the dependsOn array expects. reference() retrieves runtime property values of an already deployed resource, union() merges objects, and copyIndex() returns the current loop iteration index.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/template-functions","sourceTitle":"Template functions - Azure Resource Manager","tags":["resourceId","dependsOn","template functions"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-006","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Interpret an Azure Resource Manager template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which ARM template section should you use to reuse a value, such as a concatenated naming string, in several places without asking the user for input?","choices":["The parameters section","The resources section","The outputs section","The variables section"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The variables section holds values that are computed inside the template and reused throughout it. Parameters require a value from the deployment request, outputs only return values to the caller after the deployment, and resources declares the resources themselves.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/overview","sourceTitle":"Templates overview - Azure Resource Manager","tags":["variables","ARM template","reuse"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-007","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Azure Resource Manager template","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need the storage account name in an existing ARM template to be supplied by the person who runs the deployment. Which change should you make to the template?","choices":["Move the name to the outputs section","Add the value to the allowedValues list of a variable","Add a copy loop to the storage account resource","Move the name from the variables section to the parameters section"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Moving the value into the parameters section makes it a value that the deployment request supplies. Variables are computed by the template and cannot be supplied at deployment time, and allowedValues only constrains the values that an existing parameter accepts.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/template-parameters","sourceTitle":"Parameters in templates","tags":["parameters","variables","template modification"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-008","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Azure Resource Manager template","difficulty":"medium","question":"An ARM template must create five identically configured storage accounts, each with a unique name. Which element should you add to the resource?","choices":["A condition element","A dependsOn array","Five separate resource blocks","A copy element whose name expression uses copyIndex()"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A copy loop with a count of 5 creates the five instances, and including copyIndex() in the name expression makes every name unique. Five separate resource blocks would duplicate the configuration, and dependsOn and condition do not create extra instances.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/copy-resources","sourceTitle":"Resource iteration in ARM templates","tags":["copy loop","copyIndex","storage account"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-009","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Azure Resource Manager template","difficulty":"easy","question":"An ARM template must deploy a virtual machine only when a parameter named deployVm is set to true. Which element should you add to the virtual machine resource?","choices":["\"condition\": \"[parameters('deployVm')]\"","\"copy\": { \"count\": \"[parameters('deployVm')]\" }","\"dependsOn\": \"[parameters('deployVm')]\"","\"outputs\": \"[parameters('deployVm')]\""],"answer":0,"explanation":"The condition element controls whether a resource is deployed. A copy element controls how many instances are deployed, dependsOn controls deployment ordering, and outputs cannot be declared inside a resource block.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/copy-resources","sourceTitle":"Resource iteration in ARM templates","tags":["condition","ARM template","parameters"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-010","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Azure Resource Manager template","difficulty":"easy","question":"An ARM template must accept a database password that must never be displayed in the deployment history or the portal. Which parameter type should you use?","choices":["array","int","string","secureString"],"answer":3,"explanation":"secureString parameters are treated as secrets, so their values are not shown in deployment history or logs. A string parameter has no such protection, and array and int cannot hold a text secret.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/template-parameters","sourceTitle":"Parameters in templates","tags":["secureString","parameters","secrets"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-011","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Bicep file","difficulty":"medium","question":"A Bicep file must create a resource group together with other subscription-scoped resources. Which property should you add or modify in the file?","choices":["targetScope = 'subscription'","output location string","scope: 'resourceGroup'","location: resourceGroup().location"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The targetScope property sets the scope that the Bicep file deploys to, and subscription scope is required to create resource groups. The location property only sets the region of individual resources and does not change the deployment scope.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/file","sourceTitle":"Bicep file structure and syntax","tags":["Bicep","targetScope","subscription scope"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-012","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Bicep keyword declares a value that the person running the deployment supplies?","choices":["var","output","module","param"],"answer":3,"explanation":"param declares a parameter whose value is supplied at deployment time. var declares a value that is computed inside the file, output returns a value to the caller, and module references another Bicep file.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/file","sourceTitle":"Bicep file structure and syntax","tags":["Bicep","param","declaration"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-013","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Bicep construct should you use to deploy the set of resources that are defined in another Bicep file?","choices":["module","output","var","targetScope"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A module declaration reuses another Bicep file and its resources, and can pass parameter values to it. A var holds a local value, an output returns a value to the caller, and targetScope only declares the deployment scope of the current file.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/file","sourceTitle":"Bicep file structure and syntax","tags":["Bicep","module","code reuse"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-014","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Modify an existing Bicep file","difficulty":"medium","question":"You deploy the same Bicep file twice to the same resource group without changing the file. What is the result of the second deployment?","choices":["The deployment recreates every resource that the file defines","The deployment deletes the resources that the file does not define","The deployment fails because the resources already exist","The deployment succeeds and only the differences are applied"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Bicep deployments are idempotent: Azure compares the desired state with the current state and applies only the changes. Existing resources are not recreated, and unrelated resources are not deleted because incremental is the default deployment mode.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Bicep?","tags":["Bicep","idempotent","redeployment"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-015","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy resources by using an ARM template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"You must deploy a template to a resource group and delete every resource in that group that is not defined in the template. Which deployment mode should you use?","choices":["What-if","Incremental","Validate","Complete"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Complete mode deletes the resources in the target resource group that are not specified in the template. Incremental mode, which is the default, leaves those resources unchanged, and Validate and What-if make no changes at all.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/deployment-modes","sourceTitle":"Deployment modes - Azure Resource Manager","tags":["deployment mode","complete","resource group"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-016","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy resources by using an ARM template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which deployment mode does Azure Resource Manager use by default when you do not specify one?","choices":["Complete","Validate","What-if","Incremental"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Incremental is the default mode and the recommended one. Complete removes the resources that are missing from the template, and Validate and What-if are operations that check a template rather than deployment modes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/deployment-modes","sourceTitle":"Deployment modes - Azure Resource Manager","tags":["deployment mode","incremental","default"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-017","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy resources by using an ARM template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"hard","question":"An ARM template contains a resource of type Microsoft.Resources/resourceGroups. Which Azure PowerShell cmdlet should you use to deploy the template?","choices":["New-AzDeployment","New-AzResourceGroupDeployment","New-AzManagementGroupDeployment","New-AzTenantDeployment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A template that creates resource groups must be deployed at subscription scope, which uses New-AzDeployment. New-AzResourceGroupDeployment requires an existing resource group, and the management group and tenant cmdlets target other scopes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/deploy-powershell","sourceTitle":"Deploy resources with PowerShell and template","tags":["New-AzDeployment","subscription scope","PowerShell"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-018","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy resources by using an ARM template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"You use the Azure CLI to deploy a local template file into an existing resource group. Which command should you use?","choices":["az deployment mg create","az deployment tenant create","az deployment sub create","az deployment group create"],"answer":3,"explanation":"az deployment group create targets an existing resource group and is the correct scope for resources that are deployed into a group. az deployment sub create is used for subscription-scope deployments such as creating resource groups.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/deploy-cli","sourceTitle":"Azure deployment templates with Azure CLI","tags":["az deployment group create","Azure CLI","scope"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-019","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy resources by using an ARM template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to see which resources a template deployment will create, modify or delete before any change is made. Which operation should you use?","choices":["The Validate operation","A deployment in Complete mode","A what-if operation","A redeployment in Incremental mode"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The what-if operation shows the changes that the deployment would make without applying them, and it is recommended before deploying in Complete mode. Validate only checks that the template is valid and does not show the planned changes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/deployment-modes","sourceTitle":"Deployment modes - Azure Resource Manager","tags":["what-if","validation","deployment"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-020","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy resources by using an ARM template or a Bicep file","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to deploy a template that is stored at a publicly available URL into an existing resource group by using Azure PowerShell. Which parameter should you use?","choices":["-TemplateFile","-TemplateParameterFile","-Location","-TemplateUri"],"answer":3,"explanation":"-TemplateUri passes the URL of the template to deploy. -TemplateFile is used for a template that is stored on the local computer, -Location is required only for subscription-scope deployments, and -TemplateParameterFile supplies parameter values rather than the template.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/deploy-powershell","sourceTitle":"Deploy resources with PowerShell and template","tags":["TemplateUri","PowerShell","deployment"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-021","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Export a deployment as an ARM template or convert to a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"You have an existing JSON ARM template and want to convert it to a Bicep file. Which command should you run?","choices":["az bicep decompile --file main.json","az bicep build --file main.json","az bicep install","az deployment group validate --template-file main.json"],"answer":0,"explanation":"az bicep decompile converts an ARM template JSON file into a Bicep file. az bicep build performs the opposite conversion by compiling Bicep into JSON, and the validate command only checks a template without converting it.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/decompile","sourceTitle":"Decompile a JSON Azure Resource Manager template to Bicep","tags":["Bicep","decompile","ARM template"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-022","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Export a deployment as an ARM template or convert to a Bicep file","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to recover the template that was used for a deployment that ran against a resource group six months ago. Where should you look in the Azure portal?","choices":["The subscription's Access control (IAM) blade","The resource group's Locks blade","The resource group's Tags blade","The resource group's Deployments blade, where the deployment history and its template are shown"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Every deployment is recorded in the deployment history of its scope, and the Azure portal exposes the stored template on the Deployments blade. Tags, IAM and Locks hold governance metadata and never store template bodies.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/export-template-portal","sourceTitle":"Export template in Azure portal","tags":["deployment history","template export","portal"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-023","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Export a deployment as an ARM template or convert to a Bicep file","difficulty":"easy","question":"In the Azure portal, which two ways can you export a template for existing resources?","choices":["From a Recovery Services vault or from a Backup vault","From a management group or from a tenant","From a resource group or resource, and from the deployment history","From a policy definition or from a blueprint"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The portal can generate a template from the current state of a resource group or of selected resources, and it can also export the template that is recorded in the deployment history. Vaults, management groups and policy definitions are not export points for resource templates.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/export-template-portal","sourceTitle":"Export template in Azure portal","tags":["export template","resource group","deployment history"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-024","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create a virtual machine","difficulty":"easy","question":"You create a Linux virtual machine in the Azure portal and you want to avoid using a password for authentication. Which authentication type should you select?","choices":["Managed identity","Storage account key","Password","SSH public key"],"answer":3,"explanation":"SSH public key authentication uses a key pair, so no password is sent to the virtual machine. A managed identity and a storage account key are used by Azure services to access other resources, not to sign in interactively to a Linux virtual machine.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of virtual machines in Azure","tags":["virtual machine","SSH","authentication"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-025","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create a virtual machine","difficulty":"easy","question":"By default, in which resource group are the network interface, operating system disk, and public IP address that support a new virtual machine created?","choices":["The same resource group as the virtual machine","The subscription's default resource group","A resource group that is named after the region","The resource group that hosts the virtual network"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By default the supporting resources are created in the same resource group as the virtual machine. The other options describe resource groups that are not created automatically for a new virtual machine.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of virtual machines in Azure","tags":["virtual machine","resource group","supporting resources"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-026","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create a virtual machine","difficulty":"easy","question":"For a virtual machine, what does the location (region) setting specify?","choices":["The region where the virtual network must be created","The region where the deployment metadata is stored","The region where the virtual hard disks are stored","The region that is closest to the user who connects to the virtual machine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For a virtual machine the location specifies where the virtual hard disks are stored. The region of the virtual network and the location of the deployment metadata are separate settings.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of virtual machines in Azure","tags":["virtual machine","region","location"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-027","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create a virtual machine","difficulty":"medium","question":"You deploy a virtual machine that must accept inbound SSH connections on port 22 and block all other inbound internet traffic. Which resource should you configure?","choices":["A user-defined route","A network security group","An Azure Firewall policy","A private endpoint"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A network security group manages the traffic to and from a virtual machine, so a rule that allows TCP 22 and denies the rest meets the requirement. User-defined routes control routing, and a private endpoint provides private inbound access rather than internet filtering.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of virtual machines in Azure","tags":["network security group","NSG","inbound rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-028","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure encryption at host for Azure virtual machines","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which encryption method encrypts the temporary disk, the disk caches, and the data that flows between the virtual machine host and Azure Storage?","choices":["Azure Disk Encryption","Server-side encryption with platform-managed keys","Encryption at host","Confidential disk encryption"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Encryption at host starts at the virtual machine host itself, so the temporary disk and the disk caches are encrypted and the data flows encrypted to the Storage service. Azure Disk Encryption protects data inside the guest OS, and server-side encryption does not cover the temporary disk.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disk-encryption","sourceTitle":"Server-side encryption of Azure managed disks","tags":["encryption at host","temporary disk","cache"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-029","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure encryption at host for Azure virtual machines","difficulty":"hard","question":"Which condition must be met before you can enable encryption at host on a virtual machine?","choices":["The virtual machine must use Ultra Disks","The virtual machine must be in a zone-redundant deployment","The virtual machine must be in an availability set","The virtual machine must never have had Azure Disk Encryption enabled"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Encryption at host cannot be enabled on a virtual machine or scale set that currently has, or ever had, Azure Disk Encryption enabled, and Azure Disk Encryption cannot be enabled on a disk that has encryption at host enabled. Availability sets, disk type and zone redundancy are not prerequisites.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disk-encryption","sourceTitle":"Server-side encryption of Azure managed disks","tags":["encryption at host","Azure Disk Encryption","restrictions"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-030","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure encryption at host for Azure virtual machines","difficulty":"medium","question":"You enable encryption at host on an existing virtual machine that is already running. What must you do so that the existing data is encrypted by the feature?","choices":["Restart the virtual machine from the guest operating system","Detach and then reattach the operating system disk","Deallocate the virtual machine and then start it again","Attach a disk encryption set to the virtual machine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Existing virtual machines must be deallocated and reallocated before encryption at host applies to them. A guest restart, a disk detach, or attaching a disk encryption set does not reallocate the virtual machine to a host that performs host encryption.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disk-encryption","sourceTitle":"Server-side encryption of Azure managed disks","tags":["encryption at host","deallocate","reallocate"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-031","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure encryption at host for Azure virtual machines","difficulty":"hard","question":"You enable encryption at host on an existing Virtual Machine Scale Set. Which instances are encrypted by the feature?","choices":["Only the instances in the first fault domain","None until the scale set is deleted and recreated","All existing instances and all new instances","Only the new instances that are created after encryption is enabled"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Encryption can be enabled on an existing scale set, but only new virtual machines that are created afterwards are automatically encrypted. Existing instances keep running without host encryption until they are recreated.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disk-encryption","sourceTitle":"Server-side encryption of Azure managed disks","tags":["encryption at host","scale set","new instances"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-032","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Move a virtual machine to another resource group, subscription, or region","difficulty":"easy","question":"What happens to a virtual machine's physical region when you move the virtual machine to a different resource group?","choices":["The virtual machine is moved to a region that is paired with the original region","The virtual machine is moved to the region of the destination resource group","The region cannot be determined from the move operation","The physical region does not change; only the resource group association changes"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Moving a resource only changes its associated resource group or subscription and does not alter the physical region of the resource. A move between regions requires a different mechanism, such as Azure Resource Mover.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/move-resource-group-and-subscription","sourceTitle":"Move Azure resources to a new resource group or subscription","tags":["move","resource group","region"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-033","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Move a virtual machine to another resource group, subscription, or region","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must move a virtual machine from the East US region to the West Europe region. Which service should you use?","choices":["Azure Resource Mover","The resource group move option","The subscription move option","Azure Migrate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Azure Resource Mover is the service that helps you move Azure resources between Azure regions. Moving a resource between resource groups or subscriptions changes only the management association and does not change the region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/resource-mover/overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Resource Mover?","tags":["Azure Resource Mover","region","virtual machine"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-034","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Move a virtual machine to another resource group, subscription, or region","difficulty":"medium","question":"During a move of a virtual machine to another resource group, what is the state of the source and destination resource groups?","choices":["The source resource group is locked and the destination stays writable","Neither resource group is affected because only metadata changes","Both resource groups are deleted and recreated","Both resource groups are locked, but the existing resources keep running"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both the source and the target resource groups are locked during the move, so resources cannot be created, deleted or updated in them, while the existing resources remain fully operational. The operations are blocked rather than the resource groups being replaced.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/move-resource-group-and-subscription","sourceTitle":"Move Azure resources to a new resource group or subscription","tags":["move","resource group lock","availability"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-035","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Move a virtual machine to another resource group, subscription, or region","difficulty":"hard","question":"You plan to move a virtual machine and its network interface to a different subscription. The virtual machine is currently associated with a Standard SKU public IP address. What must you do?","choices":["Convert the public IP address to a Basic SKU address","Move the virtual network to the new subscription first","Disassociate the public IP address from the network interface before the move","Delete the network interface and create a new one after the move"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A resource that is associated with a Standard SKU public IP address cannot be moved across subscriptions, so the public IP address must be disassociated first. Changing the SKU or deleting the network interface would break the virtual machine's connectivity.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/move-limitations/networking-move-limitations","sourceTitle":"Move Azure Networking resources to new subscription or resource group","tags":["cross-subscription move","public IP","network interface"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-036","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine sizes","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which setting determines the maximum number of data disks that can be attached to a virtual machine?","choices":["The virtual machine size","The disk type","The guest operating system","The virtual network"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The number of data disks that can be attached depends on the virtual machine size that you select. Disk type and guest operating system affect performance and compatibility, but they do not change the number of attachable data disks.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/windows/expand-disks","sourceTitle":"Expand Virtual Hard Disks Attached to a Windows VM in Azure","tags":["VM size","data disks","limits"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-037","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine sizes","difficulty":"medium","question":"You try to resize a running virtual machine to a larger size and the operation fails because the new size is not available on the hardware cluster that currently hosts the machine. What should you do?","choices":["Resize the virtual machine to an intermediate size and then retry","Shut down the virtual machine from the guest operating system and retry","Create a proximity placement group and resize again","Deallocate the virtual machine, and then resize it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Deallocating the virtual machine releases it from its current host, so Azure can allocate it to any host in the region that supports the new size. A proximity placement group constrains placement further, and stopping from inside the guest OS does not release the compute allocation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/resize-vm","sourceTitle":"Resize a virtual machine","tags":["resize","deallocate","VM size"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-038","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine sizes","difficulty":"easy","question":"What happens to a running virtual machine when you change its size without deallocating it first?","choices":["It restarts","It keeps running without interruption","It is converted to a Spot virtual machine","Its operating system disk is recreated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Changing the size of a running virtual machine causes it to restart, so resizing should be treated as a disruptive operation. The operating system and data disks are not recreated, and no Spot pricing is applied.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/resize-vm","sourceTitle":"Resize a virtual machine","tags":["resize","restart","downtime"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-039","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine sizes","difficulty":"medium","question":"A virtual machine that has a dynamically assigned public IP address is deallocated so that it can be resized. What happens to the public IP address?","choices":["It is retained and stays with the virtual machine","It is converted to a static IP address","It is reassigned to another virtual machine of the same size","It is released and might change when the virtual machine starts"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Deallocating a virtual machine also releases any dynamic IP addresses that are assigned to it. The operating system and data disks are unaffected, but the public IP address might change after the virtual machine starts again.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/resize-vm","sourceTitle":"Resize a virtual machine","tags":["deallocate","public IP address","dynamic"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-040","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine sizes","difficulty":"hard","question":"A workload requires a high memory-to-vCPU ratio, such as a large in-memory database. Which virtual machine size category should you select?","choices":["General purpose","Compute optimized","Memory optimized","Storage optimized"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memory optimized sizes offer a high memory-to-vCPU ratio for memory-intensive workloads such as in-memory databases. Compute optimized sizes favour CPU performance and storage optimized sizes favour disk throughput and capacity.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/sizes","sourceTitle":"Virtual machine sizes overview","tags":["VM size","memory optimized","workload"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-041","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine disks","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which managed disk type cannot be used as an operating system disk and does not support disk caching?","choices":["Premium SSD","Standard SSD","Ultra Disk","Premium SSD v2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ultra Disks cannot be used as an operating system disk, do not support disk caching, and also cannot be used with availability sets. Premium SSD, Standard SSD and Premium SSD v2 support the common virtual machine disk configurations.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disks-types","sourceTitle":"Select a disk type for Azure IaaS VMs - managed disks","tags":["Ultra Disk","OS disk","disk caching"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-042","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine disks","difficulty":"easy","question":"By default, what protects the data on an Azure managed disk at rest?","choices":["Encryption at host","A customer-managed key that is stored in a key vault","BitLocker encryption inside the guest operating system","Server-side encryption with platform-managed keys"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Storage encryption automatically encrypts the data on managed disks at rest with platform-managed keys by default. A customer-managed key must be configured explicitly through a disk encryption set, and encryption at host and BitLocker are separate mechanisms.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disk-encryption","sourceTitle":"Server-side encryption of Azure managed disks","tags":["managed disk","server-side encryption","at rest"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-043","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine disks","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to reduce the size of a managed data disk from 1 TiB to 512 GiB. What should you do?","choices":["Resize the disk in the Azure portal and confirm the change","Detach the disk, reduce its size, and then reattach it","Shrinking an existing disk is not supported; create a smaller disk and migrate the data","Convert the disk to a Standard HDD and then reduce its size"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shrinking an existing managed disk is not supported and might result in data loss, so the supported approach is to create a new smaller disk and migrate the data to it. The portal and the detach and reattach workflow cannot perform a shrink either.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/windows/expand-disks","sourceTitle":"Expand Virtual Hard Disks Attached to a Windows VM in Azure","tags":["managed disk","shrink","resizing"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-044","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine disks","difficulty":"hard","question":"You plan to encrypt managed disks with customer-managed keys. Which requirement applies?","choices":["The managed disks and the key store must be in the same region","The key vault must be in a different subscription from the disks","The disk encryption set must be in the same availability zone as the disks","The key vault must use the Premium pricing tier"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Managed disks and the key store that holds the customer-managed keys must be in the same region, and customer-managed keys also depend on managed identities, so tenant moves can break key access. The key vault tier and the availability zone are not requirements.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disk-encryption","sourceTitle":"Server-side encryption of Azure managed disks","tags":["customer-managed key","disk encryption set","region"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-045","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage virtual machine disks","difficulty":"hard","question":"What is the maximum capacity of an Azure managed operating system disk?","choices":["1 TiB","2 TiB","4,095 GiB","64 TiB"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An operating system disk has a maximum capacity of 4,095 GiB. Many operating systems are partitioned with MBR, which limits the usable size to 2 TiB, but that is a partitioning limit and not the managed disk limit.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/windows/expand-disks","sourceTitle":"Expand Virtual Hard Disks Attached to a Windows VM in Azure","tags":["OS disk","capacity","limits"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-046","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy virtual machines to availability zones and availability sets","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the maximum number of fault domains and update domains that an availability set can have?","choices":["3 fault domains and 20 update domains","2 fault domains and 5 update domains","5 fault domains and 3 update domains","20 fault domains and 3 update domains"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An availability set can have up to 3 fault domains and 20 update domains, and those settings cannot be changed after the set is created. Two fault domains and five update domains are the default values, not the maximums.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/availability-set-overview","sourceTitle":"Availability sets overview","tags":["availability set","fault domain","update domain"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-047","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy virtual machines to availability zones and availability sets","difficulty":"easy","question":"Against which type of event does an update domain in an availability set protect virtual machines?","choices":["An outage of an entire Azure region","A network security group misconfiguration","Unplanned hardware failure of a rack","Planned maintenance events"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Update domains protect against planned maintenance, and only one update domain is restarted at a time. Fault domains, not update domains, protect against unplanned hardware failures such as power or network switch failures.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/availability-set-overview","sourceTitle":"Availability sets overview","tags":["availability set","update domain","planned maintenance"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-048","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy virtual machines to availability zones and availability sets","difficulty":"easy","question":"In a region that supports them, what is an availability zone?","choices":["A logical grouping of one or more physically separate datacenters within a region","A single rack inside a datacenter","A pair of Azure regions that are geographically distant from each other","A group of virtual machines that share the same fault domain"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An availability zone is a logical grouping of one or more physically separate datacenters within a region, each with independent power, cooling and networking. A pair of regions is a region pair, and a group of virtual machines in one fault domain is an availability set.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/reliability/availability-zones-overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure Availability Zones?","tags":["availability zone","datacenter","resiliency"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-049","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy virtual machines to availability zones and availability sets","difficulty":"medium","question":"You deploy two virtual machines into the same availability set. Which connectivity service-level agreement does this configuration provide?","choices":["99.99%","99.999%","99.9%","99.95%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Using two or more virtual machines in an availability set helps keep applications highly available and meets the 99.95% service-level agreement. A single virtual machine and a deployment that spans availability zones are covered by different agreements.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/availability-set-overview","sourceTitle":"Availability sets overview","tags":["availability set","SLA","high availability"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-050","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy virtual machines to availability zones and availability sets","difficulty":"hard","question":"An availability set is configured with two fault domains and contains eight virtual machines. If one fault domain fails, how many virtual machines could be unavailable at the same time?","choices":["1","2","4","8"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Azure distributes the virtual machines as evenly as possible across the fault domains, so eight virtual machines across two fault domains means four in each. A failure of one fault domain can therefore make four virtual machines unavailable at the same time.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/availability-set-overview","sourceTitle":"Availability sets overview","tags":["fault domain","distribution","availability"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-051","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy and configure an Azure Virtual Machine Scale Set","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Virtual Machine Scale Set orchestration mode should you choose if you need to mix virtual machine types and use Spot and on-demand virtual machines together?","choices":["Uniform","Flexible","Classic","Automatic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flexible orchestration provides a unified experience across the Azure virtual machine ecosystem and lets a scale set mix virtual machine types and use Spot and on-demand instances together. Uniform orchestration uses a single model for all instances.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machine-scale-sets/overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets overview","tags":["scale set","flexible orchestration","Spot"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-052","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy and configure an Azure Virtual Machine Scale Set","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to change an existing Virtual Machine Scale Set from Uniform orchestration to Flexible orchestration. What should you do?","choices":["Update the orchestration mode in the scale set properties","Convert the scale set with Azure CLI","Create a new scale set that uses the required orchestration mode","Scale the set to zero instances and update the orchestration mode"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The orchestration mode is defined when the scale set is created and cannot be changed or updated later, so a new scale set is required. Scaling to zero or converting with tooling does not change the orchestration mode.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machine-scale-sets/overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets overview","tags":["scale set","orchestration mode","recreate"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-053","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy and configure an Azure Virtual Machine Scale Set","difficulty":"hard","question":"A scale set autoscale profile defines a minimum of two instances and a maximum of ten instances. Four instances are running when a scale-in rule that removes four instances is triggered. How many instances are running afterwards?","choices":["0","2","4","6"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The result of the scale-in action is clamped to the minimum number of instances that the profile defines, so four minus four becomes two rather than zero. The minimum and maximum instance limits always bound the outcome of an autoscale action.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/autoscale/autoscale-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of autoscale in Azure Monitor","tags":["autoscale","scale in","minimum instances"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-054","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Deploy and configure an Azure Virtual Machine Scale Set","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which autoscale feature uses machine learning to manage Virtual Machine Scale Sets that have cyclical workload patterns?","choices":["Predictive autoscale","Schedule-based scaling","Custom metric scaling","Manual scaling"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Predictive autoscale forecasts the overall CPU load of a scale set from historical usage and scales out in time to meet the predicted demand. Schedule-based scaling triggers on fixed times rather than on a forecast, and manual scaling requires an administrator to set the instance count.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/autoscale/autoscale-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of autoscale in Azure Monitor","tags":["autoscale","predictive","scale set"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-055","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create and manage an Azure Container Registry","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Azure Container Registry pricing tier is required to replicate a registry across multiple Azure regions?","choices":["Basic","Standard","Premium","Developer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Geo-replication is a Premium tier feature that manages a single registry across multiple regions. Basic and Standard registries provide the same programmatic capabilities as Premium but without geo-replication, private endpoints or higher limits.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-registry/container-registry-skus","sourceTitle":"Azure Container Registry SKU Features and Limits","tags":["container registry","geo-replication","Premium"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-056","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create and manage an Azure Container Registry","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which Azure Container Registry feature must you enable so that the registry can store and serve signed container images?","choices":["Anonymous pull access","Content trust","A dedicated data endpoint","Repository-scoped tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Content trust uses Docker Content Trust to sign and verify image layers, and it is available only on the Premium tier. Anonymous pull access controls who can read images, a dedicated data endpoint changes the data traffic endpoint, and repository-scoped tokens grant access permissions.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-registry/container-registry-skus","sourceTitle":"Azure Container Registry SKU Features and Limits","tags":["container registry","content trust","signed images"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-057","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create and manage an Azure Container Registry","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which Azure Container Registry authentication option uses a single registry-wide account that has push and pull access and is not recommended when multiple users need access?","choices":["A managed identity","A Microsoft Entra service principal","The admin user account","A repository-scoped token"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The admin user account is a single account per registry with a high level of access, so it is not recommended when several users or systems need access. A managed identity, a service principal and repository-scoped tokens each allow per-identity or per-repository permissions.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-registry/container-registry-authentication","sourceTitle":"Azure Container Registry Authentication Options Explained","tags":["container registry","admin user","authentication"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-058","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create and manage an Azure Container Registry","difficulty":"easy","question":"After signing in with az login, which command should you use to authenticate the Docker CLI session to a registry that is named contosoacr?","choices":["docker login contosoacr","az acr login --name contosoacr","az acr credential show --name contosoacr","az acr build --registry contosoacr"],"answer":1,"explanation":"az acr login --name uses the token that az login created and sets it in the Docker configuration, so later docker commands in the session are authenticated. Showing credentials or starting a build does not authenticate the current session.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-registry/container-registry-authentication","sourceTitle":"Azure Container Registry Authentication Options Explained","tags":["container registry","az acr login","Docker"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-059","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create and manage an Azure Container Registry","difficulty":"medium","question":"An Azure-hosted container app must pull images from a private registry without storing registry credentials in its configuration. What should you use?","choices":["The registry admin user account","A managed identity that has the AcrPull role on the registry","A personal access token that is stored in an application setting","Anonymous pull access on the registry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A managed identity removes the need to store and rotate credentials, and the AcrPull role grants read access to the registry. The admin user account requires a password, and anonymous pull access would expose the registry images publicly.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-registry/container-registry-authentication","sourceTitle":"Azure Container Registry Authentication Options Explained","tags":["container registry","managed identity","AcrPull"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-060","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Instances","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Azure Container Instances resource is the top-level resource whose containers share a lifecycle, a local network and storage volumes?","choices":["The container group","The container registry","The container app environment","The pod security policy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The container group is the top-level resource in Azure Container Instances, and the containers in a group share a lifecycle, resources, a local network and storage volumes, similar to a Kubernetes pod. A container registry stores images, and a container app environment is a Container Apps resource.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-container-groups","sourceTitle":"Introduction to container groups","tags":["Azure Container Instances","container group","lifecycle"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-061","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Instances","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to deploy a container group that contains a web container and a logging container that both write to the same Azure file share. Which operating system is supported for this deployment?","choices":["Windows only","Linux only","Both Windows and Linux","Neither; multi-container groups require a container registry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multi-container container groups currently support only Linux containers, and Azure file shares can be mounted only to Linux containers in Azure Container Instances. For Windows containers, the service supports the deployment of a single container instance.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-volume-azure-files","sourceTitle":"Mount Azure Files volume to container group","tags":["Azure Container Instances","Azure Files","Linux"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-062","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Instances","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to pass a database password to a container when it starts, without exposing the value in logs or in the Azure portal. What should you configure?","choices":["A resource tag that contains the password","A secure environment variable on the container","An empty directory volume that contains the password","A customer-managed key for the container group"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Azure Container Instances supports secure environment variables whose values are not exposed in output or logs. Tags are resource metadata, and a customer-managed key encrypts the container group's data at rest rather than providing a startup value.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-environment-variables","sourceTitle":"Set environment variables in container instance","tags":["Azure Container Instances","secrets","environment variables"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-063","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Instances","difficulty":"medium","question":"A container in an Azure Container Instances container group exits with exit code 0. The container group uses the default restart policy. What happens?","choices":["The container is restarted","The container group is deleted","The container remains stopped until it is started manually","The container image is pulled again and the container is not restarted"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Always is the default restart policy, so the containers in the group are always restarted, even after a successful exit. OnFailure would restart only after a failure, and Never would leave the container stopped.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-restart-policy","sourceTitle":"Restart policy for run-once tasks","tags":["Azure Container Instances","restart policy","Always"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-064","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Instances","difficulty":"hard","question":"You run a batch job in Azure Container Instances and want the container to restart only when the process fails. Which restart policy should you configure?","choices":["Always","OnFailure","Never","OnSuccess"],"answer":1,"explanation":"OnFailure restarts the container only when the process exits with a failure, which suits batch jobs that should retry on failure and stop cleanly on success. Always restarts the container even after a successful exit, and OnSuccess is not a valid restart policy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-restart-policy","sourceTitle":"Restart policy for run-once tasks","tags":["Azure Container Instances","restart policy","OnFailure"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-065","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Apps","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which scaling technology does Azure Container Apps use for its declarative scaling rules?","choices":["Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets autoscale","KEDA (Kubernetes Event-driven Autoscaling)","Azure Automation runbooks","Service Fabric autoscaling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Container Apps uses KEDA to scale replicas against HTTP requests, queue messages, CPU and memory load, and many event sources. The other options are autoscaling mechanisms for different Azure compute services.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/scale-app","sourceTitle":"Scaling in Azure Container Apps","tags":["Azure Container Apps","KEDA","scaling"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-066","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Apps","difficulty":"medium","question":"An Azure Container App scales on CPU load. To which value can the app scale?","choices":["It can scale to zero replicas","It can scale down to one replica but not to zero","It can scale only up to the number of revisions","It can scale to zero only when a queue trigger is configured"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Applications that scale on CPU or memory load cannot scale to zero, so they must keep at least one replica. Applications that scale on HTTP traffic or on event triggers can scale to zero because a new replica can be created when a request or event arrives.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Container Apps overview","tags":["Azure Container Apps","scale to zero","CPU"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-067","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Apps","difficulty":"medium","question":"A container app must retrieve secrets from an Azure Key Vault. Which mechanism should you configure so that the app authenticates without storing credentials?","choices":["A repository-scoped token for the key vault","A managed identity for the container app","A service principal password in an environment variable","A shared access signature for the key vault"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A managed identity lets the container app authenticate to Key Vault without any credentials in the app's configuration, and access is granted with an Azure role assignment. A service principal password would have to be stored and rotated, and shared access signatures are for storage services.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/managed-identity","sourceTitle":"Managed identities in Azure Container Apps","tags":["Azure Container Apps","managed identity","Key Vault"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-068","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Apps","difficulty":"hard","question":"You deploy Azure Container Apps into a custom virtual network by using the default workload profiles environment. What is the minimum subnet size that the environment requires?","choices":["/23","/27","/24","/28"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The workload profiles environment type requires a dedicated subnet of at least /27. The larger /23 requirement applies to the legacy consumption-only environment type.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/networking","sourceTitle":"Networking in an Azure Container Apps Environment","tags":["Azure Container Apps","subnet","workload profiles"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-069","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision a container by using Azure Container Apps","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which type of change creates a new revision of an Azure Container App?","choices":["Adding or editing a scaling rule","Adding a resource tag","Changing the container app's resource group","Enabling a managed identity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Adding or editing scaling rules creates a new revision, and a revision is an immutable snapshot of the container app. Tags, resource group membership and identity configuration are management settings that do not create a revision.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/scale-app","sourceTitle":"Scaling in Azure Container Apps","tags":["Azure Container Apps","revision","scaling rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-070","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage sizing and scaling for containers, including ACI and ACA","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Azure container hosting service does not provide built-in automatic scaling of the number of running containers?","choices":["Azure Container Apps","Azure Container Instances","Azure App Service","Azure Kubernetes Service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Container Instances runs individual container groups on demand and has no built-in autoscaling. Azure Container Apps and Azure App Service both scale the number of instances automatically when the load changes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Container Apps overview","tags":["Azure Container Instances","autoscale","container hosting"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-071","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage sizing and scaling for containers, including ACI and ACA","difficulty":"easy","question":"You create a container group with two containers, and each container requests one CPU. How much CPU is allocated to the container group?","choices":["One CPU","Two CPUs","Four CPUs","The CPU allocation is set by the largest container request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Container Instances adds the resource requests of the container instances in the group, so two containers that each request one CPU produce a group that is allocated two CPUs. The allocation is the sum of the requests, not the largest request.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-container-groups","sourceTitle":"Introduction to container groups","tags":["Azure Container Instances","CPU request","container group"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-072","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage sizing and scaling for containers, including ACI and ACA","difficulty":"hard","question":"Two containers run in the same container group. Container2 has a CPU limit that is so high that Container1 is starved of CPU. What should you do?","choices":["Remove the CPU limit from Container1","Decrease the CPU limit of Container2","Increase the CPU request of Container2","Move Container1 to a different container group in another region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The CPU limit of a container instance caps how much of the group's total CPU allocation it can consume, so lowering Container2's limit leaves CPU available for Container1. Increasing Container2's request would reserve even more of the group's resources for it.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-container-groups","sourceTitle":"Introduction to container groups","tags":["Azure Container Instances","CPU limit","resource starvation"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-073","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage sizing and scaling for containers, including ACI and ACA","difficulty":"hard","question":"In a container group, what bounds the maximum CPU resource limit that you can set for an individual container instance?","choices":["The total resources that are allocated to the container group","The CPU request of the smallest container in the group","The number of containers in the group multiplied by two CPUs","The virtual network address space of the container group"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The maximum resource limit that you can set for a container instance is the total resources that are allocated to the container group. A container's limit must be greater than or equal to its own request, and the group total is the upper bound.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-instances/container-instances-container-groups","sourceTitle":"Introduction to container groups","tags":["Azure Container Instances","resource limit","container group"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-074","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage sizing and scaling for containers, including ACI and ACA","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which two Azure services can automatically scale the number of container instances that run an application?","choices":["Azure Container Instances and Azure Container Apps","Azure Container Apps and Azure App Service","Azure Container Instances and Azure App Service","Azure App Service and Azure Functions only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Container Apps scales replicas with KEDA-based rules, and Azure App Service scales the instances of a plan that hosts a containerized app with rules-based autoscale. Azure Container Instances has no built-in autoscaling.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/autoscale/autoscale-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of autoscale in Azure Monitor","tags":["autoscale","Azure Container Apps","App Service"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-075","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Manage sizing and scaling for containers, including ACI and ACA","difficulty":"easy","question":"You want to be sure that at least one replica of an Azure Container App revision is always running. Which setting should you configure?","choices":["Set the minimum number of replicas to one or higher","Set the maximum number of replicas to one","Enable a CPU scaling rule with a scale-to-zero behaviour","Set the revision mode to single"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Setting the minimum number of replicas to one or higher keeps an instance of the revision always running. Setting the maximum to one still allows the app to scale to zero, and the revision mode controls how many revisions can be active rather than how many replicas run.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/scale-app","sourceTitle":"Scaling in Azure Container Apps","tags":["Azure Container Apps","replicas","minimum"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-076","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision an App Service plan","difficulty":"easy","question":"You plan to deploy ten web apps that all use the same region and the same operating system. What is the minimum number of App Service plans that can host them?","choices":["One","Two","Five","Ten"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A single App Service plan can host multiple web apps that share its compute resources, so one plan is sufficient when the region and operating system requirements match. Creating one plan per app would multiply the cost without adding capability.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview-hosting-plans","sourceTitle":"Azure App Service Plans","tags":["App Service plan","multiple apps","cost"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-077","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision an App Service plan","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which App Service setting defines the region, the operating system, and the number of virtual machine instances that run your web app?","choices":["The web app's deployment slot","The App Service plan","The web app's application settings","The web app's custom domain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every app runs in an App Service plan, and the plan defines the operating system, region, and the number and size of the virtual machine instances. Slots, app settings and custom domains are configured on the app rather than on the plan.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview-hosting-plans","sourceTitle":"Azure App Service Plans","tags":["App Service plan","region","operating system"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-078","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Provision an App Service plan","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which App Service plan tier runs apps on dedicated Azure virtual machines in dedicated Azure virtual networks and provides network isolation in addition to compute isolation?","choices":["Basic","Standard","IsolatedV2","Shared"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The IsolatedV2 tier runs dedicated virtual machines on dedicated Azure virtual networks, which adds network isolation to compute isolation. Basic and Standard use dedicated compute without a dedicated virtual network, and Shared runs apps on virtual machines that are shared with other customers.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview-hosting-plans","sourceTitle":"Azure App Service Plans","tags":["App Service plan","Isolated v2","network isolation"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-079","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure scaling for an App Service plan","difficulty":"medium","question":"An App Service plan is already configured with Azure Monitor metric rules that scale the instance count. Which scale-out method is in use?","choices":["Manual scaling","Automatic (Azure-managed) scaling","Rules-based scaling","Vertical scaling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rules-based scaling uses Azure Monitor rules that define metric thresholds and scale actions. Manual scaling requires an administrator to set the instance count, and autoscale does not perform vertical scaling.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/autoscale/autoscale-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of autoscale in Azure Monitor","tags":["App Service","scale out","rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-080","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure scaling for an App Service plan","difficulty":"hard","question":"What is the maximum number of autoscale rules that you can define in a single autoscale profile?","choices":["5","10","20","100"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An autoscale profile supports up to 10 rules for both scale-in and scale-out actions. An autoscale setting supports up to 20 profiles, which are also called scale conditions.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/autoscale/autoscale-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of autoscale in Azure Monitor","tags":["autoscale","rules","profile"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-081","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure scaling for an App Service plan","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which three values does an autoscale profile define to bound the number of instances that can run?","choices":["Minimum, maximum and default","Target, threshold and step","Baseline, ceiling and burst","Initial, final and cooldown"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each scale condition, or profile, defines the default, minimum and maximum number of instances that can run under that profile. Thresholds and cooldown periods are part of the individual rules rather than the instance limits.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/autoscale/autoscale-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of autoscale in Azure Monitor","tags":["autoscale","instance limits","profile"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-082","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create an App Service","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which runtime stacks can an Azure App Service web app run, and on which operating systems?","choices":[".NET and Java on Windows only","Python and PHP on Linux only",".NET, Java, Node.js, Python and PHP, on both Windows and Linux","Node.js only, on both Windows and Linux"],"answer":2,"explanation":"App Service supports .NET, Java (Java SE, Tomcat and JBoss), Node.js, Python and PHP on both Windows and Linux, and containerized apps can be deployed as a custom container. The other options describe subsets of that support.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of Azure App Service","tags":["App Service","runtime stacks","Windows","Linux"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-083","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to host a containerized web application in Azure App Service. What should you deploy?","choices":["A managed certificate for the container image","A virtual machine scale set that pulls the image","The image as a custom container in a web app","A file share that contains the image layers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"App Service can run a containerized app by deploying the image as a custom container in a web app. A managed certificate secures a custom domain rather than hosting a workload, and a file share cannot run containers.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of Azure App Service","tags":["App Service","custom container","web app"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-084","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Create an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"You plan to host a containerized web application in Azure App Service. Which host resources must match the plan that hosts the app?","choices":["Only the operating system","Only the region","Both the operating system and the region","Neither; App Service resolves both automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A web app must be placed in an App Service plan that matches its operating system and its region, so both requirements have to be satisfied. App Service does not create a plan in a matching region automatically.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview-hosting-plans","sourceTitle":"Azure App Service Plans","tags":["App Service","plan","region","operating system"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-085","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure certificates and TLS for an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need a free TLS certificate for a custom domain that Azure creates and renews automatically. What should you use?","choices":["An App Service managed certificate","A public certificate that you upload","A self-signed certificate that you generate on the web server","A certificate that you import from a Key Vault without a private key"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The App Service managed certificate is free, fully managed by App Service and renewed automatically as long as the prerequisites stay the same. Uploading a public certificate does not secure a custom domain, and a certificate without a private key cannot be used for a TLS binding.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/configure-ssl-certificate","sourceTitle":"Install a TLS/SSL Certificate for Your App","tags":["App Service","managed certificate","TLS"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-086","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure certificates and TLS for an App Service","difficulty":"hard","question":"You upload a private certificate to App Service so that it can be used for a TLS binding. Which requirement must the file meet?","choices":["It must be a password-protected PFX file","It must be a self-signed certificate","It must be a public certificate without a private key","It must not contain the intermediate certificates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An uploaded private certificate must be exported as a password-protected PFX file that contains all intermediate certificates and the root certificate. For a TLS binding it must also be signed by a trusted certificate authority and include the server authentication extended key usage.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/configure-ssl-certificate","sourceTitle":"Install a TLS/SSL Certificate for Your App","tags":["App Service","certificate","PFX","TLS"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-087","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure certificates and TLS for an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to secure several custom domains that share the same inbound IP address by using multiple TLS certificates. Which binding type should you use?","choices":["IP-based SSL","Server Name Indication (SNI) SSL","A managed certificate in the production slot only","A client certificate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SNI SSL allows multiple TLS certificates to secure multiple domains on the same IP address. IP-based SSL allows only one certificate per dedicated public IP address and is supported only in the Standard tier and above.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/app-service-web-tutorial-custom-domain","sourceTitle":"Set Up an Existing Custom Domain Name for Your App","tags":["App Service","SNI","TLS binding"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-088","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Map an existing custom DNS name to an App Service","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to add the root domain contoso.com to an Azure web app. Which DNS record type should you create at the domain registrar?","choices":["A CNAME record","An A record","An MX record","An SRV record"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A root, or apex, domain must be mapped with an A record because a CNAME record cannot be used for the root domain. A CNAME record is the recommended record for a subdomain such as www.contoso.com.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/app-service-web-tutorial-custom-domain","sourceTitle":"Set Up an Existing Custom Domain Name for Your App","tags":["App Service","custom domain","A record"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-089","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Map an existing custom DNS name to an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to map the subdomain www.contoso.com to an Azure web app. Which DNS record type should you create?","choices":["An A record only","A TXT record only","A CNAME record","An MX record"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A CNAME record maps the subdomain to the app's default hostname, which is less likely to change than the app's IP address. An A record could be used for a subdomain, but the CNAME is the recommended record because the IP address can change.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/app-service-web-tutorial-custom-domain","sourceTitle":"Set Up an Existing Custom Domain Name for Your App","tags":["App Service","custom domain","CNAME"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-090","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Map an existing custom DNS name to an App Service","difficulty":"hard","question":"How many DNS records are required for each custom domain that you add to an App Service app?","choices":["One record of any type","Two records: a CNAME or A record and a TXT record for validation","Three records: CNAME, A and MX","No records, because App Service issues the record automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For each custom domain you need two DNS records: a CNAME record for a subdomain or an A record for a root domain, plus a TXT record that proves domain ownership. Only the routing record would leave the domain unverified.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/app-service-web-tutorial-custom-domain","sourceTitle":"Set Up an Existing Custom Domain Name for Your App","tags":["App Service","custom domain","TXT verification"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-091","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure backup for an App Service","difficulty":"easy","question":"What must you create before you can configure a custom backup for an Azure App Service app?","choices":["A Recovery Services vault","An Azure Storage account","A Backup vault","A managed disk snapshot"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A custom App Service backup writes the backup to a blob container in an Azure Storage account, so the storage account must exist first. Recovery Services and Backup vaults are used by other Azure backup services, not by App Service backup.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/manage-backup","sourceTitle":"Back Up an App","tags":["App Service","backup","storage account"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-092","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure backup for an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to exclude several folders from an App Service backup. What should you do?","choices":["Add the folder paths to a _backup.filter file in the app's wwwroot folder","Add the folder paths to the backup policy retention settings","Create a read-only lock on the folders that you want to exclude","Configure the exclusions in the deployment slot's application settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Folders and files are excluded from future backups by listing them in a _backup.filter file in the app's wwwroot folder. Retention settings control how long backups are kept, and locks and application settings have no effect on the backup scope.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/manage-backup","sourceTitle":"Back Up an App","tags":["App Service","backup","exclusions"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-093","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure backup for an App Service","difficulty":"hard","question":"An App Service plan uses the Basic tier. Which statement about backup is correct?","choices":["Backups can be restored to any deployment slot","Only the production slot can be backed up and restored","Backups require the Standard tier or above","Backups are not supported in the Basic tier"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Back up and restore is supported in the Basic, Standard, Premium and Isolated tiers, but in the Basic tier only the production slot can be backed up and restored. Higher tiers, and a separate configuration for each slot, are required for other slots.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/manage-backup","sourceTitle":"Back Up an App","tags":["App Service","backup","Basic tier","slots"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-094","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure networking settings for an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"An Azure web app must connect to a virtual machine that is hosted on an Azure virtual network. Which App Service feature should you configure?","choices":["Virtual network integration","A private endpoint","Virtual network peering on the app","An external load balancer in front of the app"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Virtual network integration gives the app outbound access to resources in the virtual network, which is what a connection to a virtual machine requires. A private endpoint provides inbound private access to the app, and peering and a load balancer do not connect the app to the network.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview-vnet-integration","sourceTitle":"Integrate your app with an Azure virtual network","tags":["App Service","VNet integration","outbound"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-095","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure networking settings for an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which type of connectivity does App Service virtual network integration provide?","choices":["Inbound access to the app from the virtual network only","Both inbound and outbound access to the app","Outbound access from the app into the virtual network","Access to the virtual network only when an App Service Environment is used"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Virtual network integration is used to make outbound calls from the app into the virtual network, and it does not grant inbound private access to the app. Inbound private access requires a private endpoint.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview-vnet-integration","sourceTitle":"Integrate your app with an Azure virtual network","tags":["App Service","VNet integration","inbound","outbound"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-096","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure networking settings for an App Service","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need an Azure web app to be reachable only from inside a virtual network and never from the public internet. What should you configure?","choices":["Virtual network integration on the integration subnet","A private endpoint for the app","An IP restriction on the app's public endpoint","A network security group on the app's integration subnet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A private endpoint gives the app inbound private access so that it can be reached only from the virtual network. Virtual network integration is outbound only, and an NSG on the integration subnet does not control inbound traffic to the app's public endpoint.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview-vnet-integration","sourceTitle":"Integrate your app with an Azure virtual network","tags":["App Service","private endpoint","inbound access"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-097","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure deployment slots for an App Service","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which App Service plan tiers support deployment slots?","choices":["Free, Shared and Basic","Basic and Standard","Standard, Premium and Isolated","Premium and Isolated only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deployment slots are available in the Standard, Premium and Isolated tiers of an App Service plan. The Free, Shared and Basic tiers do not provide deployment slots.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/deploy-staging-slots","sourceTitle":"Set Up Staging Environments","tags":["App Service","deployment slots","tiers"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-098","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure deployment slots for an App Service","difficulty":"easy","question":"How many deployment slots does the Standard tier of an App Service plan support?","choices":["One","Three","Five","Ten"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Standard tier supports five deployment slots. Because of this limit, an app that uses more than five slots cannot be scaled down to the Standard tier.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/deploy-staging-slots","sourceTitle":"Set Up Staging Environments","tags":["App Service","deployment slots","Standard tier"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-099","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure deployment slots for an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must deploy a new version of a web app without causing downtime for users. Which approach should you use?","choices":["Stop the production app, deploy the new version and then start the app","Deploy the new version to a deployment slot, warm it up, and then swap it into production","Deploy the new version directly to production and restart the app","Scale out the plan to the maximum number of instances before deploying"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A swap redirects traffic seamlessly after the slot has been warmed up, so no requests are dropped. Stopping the app or deploying directly to production causes downtime, and scaling out does not remove the interruption that a direct deployment causes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/deploy-staging-slots","sourceTitle":"Set Up Staging Environments","tags":["App Service","deployment slots","zero downtime"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D3-100","domain":3,"domainName":"Compute","subtopic":"Configure deployment slots for an App Service","difficulty":"medium","question":"After a slot swap, a problem is discovered in production. What is the fastest way to restore the previous version?","choices":["Restore the most recent App Service backup","Redeploy the previous build from the source repository","Swap the slots again","Scale the plan down and then up again"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After a swap, the other slot contains the previous production version, so performing the same swap again immediately returns the last known good version in seconds. Restoring a backup or redeploying the app takes far longer and causes an interruption.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/deploy-staging-slots","sourceTitle":"Set Up Staging Environments","tags":["App Service","slot swap","rollback"],"sourceFile":"domain-3-compute.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-001","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to create a private network in Azure that isolates your Azure resources, such as virtual machines and virtual machine scale sets, from other networks. Which Azure resource should you create first?","choices":["A virtual network","A network security group","A public IP address","A private endpoint"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An Azure virtual network is the fundamental building block of a private network in Azure and must exist before you can deploy resources such as virtual machines and virtual machine scale sets. A network security group only filters traffic to and from resources and cannot create a network. A public IP address and a private endpoint are both associated with resources that already reside in a virtual network.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Virtual Network?","tags":["virtual network","isolation","networking fundamentals"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-002","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"easy","question":"You plan to deploy a virtual network with the address space 10.0.0.0/16. The subnet must provide at least 500 usable IP addresses for virtual machines. What is the smallest subnet address range that meets the requirement?","choices":["10.0.0.0/24","10.0.0.0/23","10.0.0.0/25","10.0.0.0/29"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure reserves five addresses in every subnet: the first address, the last address, and three addresses for Azure service use. A /23 range contains 512 addresses, leaving 507 usable addresses, which is the smallest range here that satisfies the 500-address requirement. A /24 leaves 251 usable addresses and a /25 leaves 123, so both are too small.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["subnet sizing","CIDR","IP addressing"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-003","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"medium","question":"You have a virtual network that contains a subnet named Subnet1. You need to give all resources in Subnet1 outbound internet connectivity that uses a dedicated, predictable set of public IP addresses. What should you configure?","choices":["A public IP address on the network interface of each virtual machine","A network security group rule that allows outbound internet traffic","A NAT gateway associated with Subnet1","An Azure Bastion host deployed to Subnet1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Associating a NAT gateway with a subnet provides network address translation for the resources in that subnet and uses a dedicated set of public IP addresses for outbound connections. Assigning a public IP address to every network interface is per-resource overhead and does not scale. A network security group only filters traffic and does not perform translation, and Azure Bastion provides remote access rather than outbound connectivity.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["NAT gateway","outbound connectivity","subnet"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-004","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to deploy Azure Application Gateway into a virtual network. Which naming requirement applies to the subnet that will host the gateway?","choices":["The subnet name must begin with a letter.","The subnet must be named GatewaySubnet.","The subnet name must be prefixed with the resource group name.","The subnet name must begin with a number."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For maximum compatibility with Azure services, a subnet name should start with a letter. Azure Application Gateway cannot be deployed into a subnet whose name starts with a number. GatewaySubnet is reserved for virtual network gateways, not for Application Gateway, and no prefix derived from the resource group name is required.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["subnet naming","application gateway","subnet"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-005","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to create a virtual network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses for the resources deployed in its subnets. What should you configure first?","choices":["Add an IPv6 address space to the virtual network.","Enable a service endpoint on each subnet.","Associate a NAT gateway with each subnet.","Create a public IP address prefix."],"answer":0,"explanation":"You create a dual-stack virtual network that supports both IPv4 and IPv6 by adding an IPv6 address space to the virtual network, then defining subnets from that space. Service endpoints and NAT gateways change how traffic is routed or translated but do not add an address family. A public IP address prefix is a separate resource used for contiguous public addresses.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["IPv6","dual-stack","virtual network"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-006","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"medium","question":"A virtual network uses the address space 10.0.0.0/16 and contains a subnet named Subnet1 with the address range 10.0.1.0/24. Subnet1 currently hosts running virtual machines. You need to change the address range of Subnet1. What must you do first?","choices":["Delete the virtual network peering that is associated with Subnet1.","Move the resources to another subnet or delete them.","Delete and recreate the entire virtual network.","Disable all service endpoints on Subnet1."],"answer":1,"explanation":"You can change a subnet address range only when the subnet contains no resources. If resources exist, you must first move them to another subnet or delete them, and the steps vary by resource type. Peerings and service endpoints do not have to be removed to change a subnet address range, and the whole virtual network does not need to be recreated.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["subnet","address range","change subnet"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-007","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to connect two virtual networks that are in the same Azure region so that resources in each network can communicate directly and privately. The virtual networks are in different Azure subscriptions. What should you configure?","choices":["A site-to-site VPN connection","An ExpressRoute circuit","Virtual network peering","An Azure Route Server"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Virtual network peering connects virtual networks for low-latency, private communication over the Microsoft backbone, and it supports different subscriptions, Microsoft Entra tenants, and regions. A site-to-site VPN requires a gateway in each network and sends traffic over the public internet. ExpressRoute connects an on-premises network to Azure rather than two virtual networks to each other.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["peering","cross-subscription","connectivity"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-008","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"medium","question":"You have three virtual networks named VNet1, VNet2, and VNet3. VNet1 is peered with VNet2, and VNet2 is peered with VNet3. No other peerings exist. A virtual machine in VNet1 must communicate with a virtual machine in VNet3. What should you do?","choices":["Create a user-defined route that points from VNet1 to VNet3.","Enable gateway transit on VNet2.","Remove the peering between VNet1 and VNet2.","Create a virtual network peering between VNet1 and VNet3."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Virtual network peering is not transitive, so a virtual machine in VNet1 cannot reach VNet3 through VNet2's peerings alone. You must create a direct peering between VNet1 and VNet3, or redesign the topology so that traffic passes through a network virtual appliance in a hub virtual network by using user-defined routes. Gateway transit provides on-premises connectivity and does not forward traffic between peered networks.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["peering","non-transitive","hub-and-spoke"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-009","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"medium","question":"A hub virtual network named HubVNet contains a VPN gateway and is peered with a spoke virtual network named SpokeVNet. The spoke has no gateway. You need SpokeVNet to use the VPN gateway in HubVNet for on-premises connectivity. What should you configure?","choices":["Enable gateway transit on the hub's peering and select the remote gateway setting on the spoke's peering.","Deploy a second VPN gateway into SpokeVNet.","Disable route propagation on the spoke's GatewaySubnet.","Enable autoregistration on the spoke's private DNS zone."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gateway transit is a peering property that lets a virtual network use a VPN or ExpressRoute gateway in a peered virtual network. You allow gateway transit in the hub's peering to the spoke and select the option on the spoke to use the remote virtual network's gateway. A virtual network can have only one gateway, so adding a second gateway to the spoke is not valid, and disabling route propagation on GatewaySubnet would break the gateway itself.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["gateway transit","peering","VPN gateway"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-010","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to connect two virtual networks that are located in different Azure regions so that they can communicate privately. Which type of peering should you use?","choices":["Virtual network peering","Global virtual network peering","A site-to-site VPN connection","Azure Bastion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Global virtual network peering connects virtual networks that are in different Azure regions, while regional virtual network peering connects virtual networks in the same region. Both types keep traffic on the Microsoft backbone network, so you do not need a VPN connection or an Azure Bastion host for virtual network-to-virtual network connectivity.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["global peering","regions","peering types"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-011","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"hard","question":"VNet1 is in East US and VNet2 is in West Europe, and the two virtual networks are globally peered. A virtual machine in VNet1 must connect to the front-end IP address of a Basic SKU internal load balancer that is deployed in VNet2. What is the result?","choices":["The connection succeeds over the Microsoft backbone network.","The connection succeeds only if both virtual networks also have private endpoints.","The connection fails because a Basic load balancer front end is not reachable across global peering.","The connection succeeds only if a VPN gateway is deployed between the two virtual networks."],"answer":2,"explanation":"With global virtual network peering, resources in one virtual network cannot communicate with the front-end IP address of a Basic load balancer, whether internal or public, in the globally peered virtual network. You must use a Standard SKU load balancer for cross-region scenarios. Private endpoints and VPN gateways are not the limiting factor in this case.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["global peering","basic load balancer","constraints"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-012","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"hard","question":"You increase the address space of a virtual network that is peered with another virtual network. Traffic to the new address range is not routed to the peered virtual network. What should you do to resolve the issue?","choices":["Recreate both virtual network peerings.","Delete and redeploy the peering link in both directions.","Assign a new public IP address to the virtual network.","Sync the virtual network peers."],"answer":3,"explanation":"You can resize the address space of peered virtual networks without downtime, but after the resize the peers must sync with the new address space changes from the Azure portal or with Azure PowerShell. Syncing runs a single operation that updates the routes for the new ranges. Recreating the peering entirely is unnecessary, and a public IP address has no role in address space synchronization.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["peering","address space","sync peers"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-013","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure public IP addresses","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to assign a public IP address to a virtual machine. Internet clients must be able to reach the virtual machine at the same IP address after the virtual machine is stopped and started. Which allocation method should you use?","choices":["Static","Dynamic","DHCP","Global tier"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With the static allocation method, the IP address is assigned when the public IP resource is created and is released only when the resource is deleted, so the address survives a stop and start. A dynamically allocated address is released when the resource is stopped or deleted and can change. DHCP is not an Azure public IP allocation method, and the global tier is a routing option rather than an allocation method.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/ip-services/public-ip-addresses","sourceTitle":"Public IP addresses","tags":["public IP","static allocation","address assignment"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-014","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure public IP addresses","difficulty":"medium","question":"You create a Standard SKU public IP address and associate it with the network interface of a virtual machine. No network security group is associated with either the subnet or the network interface. Users report that they cannot reach the virtual machine from the internet. What should you do?","choices":["Change the allocation method of the public IP address to dynamic.","Create a network security group rule that explicitly allows the required inbound traffic.","Convert the public IP address to the Basic SKU.","Create a user-defined route that points to the internet."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard SKU public IP addresses follow a secure-by-default model: they are closed to inbound traffic when used as a front end, and traffic must be explicitly allowed by a network security group. Changing the allocation method does not change the filtering behavior, and the Basic SKU, which was open by default, is retired. A user-defined route affects where outbound traffic is sent, not whether inbound traffic is permitted.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/ip-services/public-ip-addresses","sourceTitle":"Public IP addresses","tags":["Standard SKU","secure by default","network security group"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-015","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure public IP addresses","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to reserve several contiguous public IP addresses in the same Azure region so that you can assign them to your resources. What should you create?","choices":["A route table","A NAT gateway","A public IP address prefix","A service endpoint"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A public IP address prefix reserves a range of contiguous public IP addresses in a region, and the individual addresses from that range can be assigned to your resources. A NAT gateway provides outbound network address translation, a route table controls routing decisions, and a service endpoint changes how traffic to a PaaS service is routed rather than reserving public addresses.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/ip-services/public-ip-address-prefix","sourceTitle":"Azure Public IP address prefix","tags":["public IP prefix","contiguous IPs","public IP"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-016","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure public IP addresses","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to assign a public IP address to a virtual network gateway. The address must remain available if a single availability zone in the region fails. Which configuration should you choose?","choices":["A non-zonal Basic SKU public IP address","A zonal Standard SKU public IP address","A dynamically allocated Basic SKU public IP address","A zone-redundant Standard SKU public IP address"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A zone-redundant Standard SKU public IP address is created across multiple zones and can survive the failure of any single zone. Basic SKU public IP addresses do not support availability zones at all, and a zonal address shares the fate of the specific zone it is tied to.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/ip-services/public-ip-addresses","sourceTitle":"Public IP addresses","tags":["public IP","availability zones","zone-redundant"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-017","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need all outbound traffic from a subnet that does not match any more specific route to pass through a network virtual appliance for inspection. What should you create?","choices":["A user-defined route with the address prefix 0.0.0.0/0 and the next hop type Virtual appliance","A network security group rule that denies all outbound traffic","A service endpoint for Azure Storage on the subnet","A NAT gateway associated with the subnet"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A user-defined route with the 0.0.0.0/0 address prefix and a Virtual appliance next hop directs all traffic that does not match a more specific route to the appliance you specify by IP address. A network security group only allows or denies traffic and cannot redirect it. A NAT gateway provides direct outbound translation without inspection, and a service endpoint affects only traffic to the specific PaaS service.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["user-defined route","0.0.0.0/0","network virtual appliance"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-018","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"medium","question":"You create a route table, add routes to it, and associate the route table with a subnet that already has system routes. What happens to the system routes of that subnet?","choices":["The system routes are deleted when the route table is associated.","The system routes remain, and user-defined routes override conflicting system routes.","The system routes are converted into user-defined routes.","The system routes are replaced by BGP routes only."],"answer":1,"explanation":"When you associate a route table with a subnet, the routes in the table are combined with the subnet's default system routes, and user-defined routes override the default routes where they conflict. System routes cannot be created or removed by you, and Azure does not convert them into user-defined routes. BGP routes are added only when you exchange routes with a gateway.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["user-defined route","system routes","route table"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-019","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"hard","question":"A subnet's route table contains a user-defined route for the address prefix 10.0.0.0/16 and a system route for the address prefix 10.0.0.0/24. Azure must route a packet destined to 10.0.0.5. Which route is used, and why?","choices":["The user-defined route for 10.0.0.0/16, because user-defined routes always have priority.","The system route for 10.0.0.0/24, because system routes always have priority.","The route for 10.0.0.0/24, because Azure selects the longest matching prefix.","Neither route, because the overlapping prefixes cause the packet to be dropped."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Azure selects a route based on the destination IP address by using the longest prefix match algorithm, so the more specific /24 route is chosen over the /16 route. The priority order among route sources, where user-defined routes outrank BGP routes and BGP routes outrank system routes, applies only when two routes have the same address prefix. Overlapping prefixes do not cause traffic to be dropped.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["longest prefix match","route selection","user-defined route"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-020","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"hard","question":"You enable a service endpoint for Microsoft.Storage on a subnet. Which route does Azure add to the subnet's route table?","choices":["A default route with the next hop type Internet","A route for the Storage address prefixes with the next hop type Virtual network gateway","A route for the Storage address prefixes with the next hop type Virtual appliance","A route for the Storage address prefixes with the next hop type VirtualNetworkServiceEndpoint"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When you enable a service endpoint, Azure adds a route whose address prefixes are the address prefixes of the service and whose next hop type is VirtualNetworkServiceEndpoint, so traffic reaches the service over the Azure backbone network. Traffic to the service then no longer uses the 0.0.0.0/0 Internet route. Routes with the service endpoint next hop type cannot be overridden by user-defined routes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["service endpoint","effective routes","next hop type"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-021","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"medium","question":"You attempt to associate a network security group with a subnet, but the network security group is deployed in a different Azure region than the virtual network. What is the result?","choices":["The association succeeds and the network security group is replicated to the virtual network's region.","The association fails because the network security group must be in the same location as the virtual network.","The association succeeds because network security groups are global resources.","The association succeeds, but the network security group rules are ignored until both resources are in the same region."],"answer":1,"explanation":"When you associate a network security group with a subnet, the network security group must exist in the same subscription and location as the virtual network. Azure does not replicate a network security group to another region, and network security groups are regional rather than global resources, so the association cannot partially succeed.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["subnet","network security group","regions"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-022","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which subnet setting grants an Azure service explicit permissions to create service-specific resources in the subnet by using a unique identifier during service deployment?","choices":["Service endpoints","A network security group association","Subnet delegation","A route table association"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Subnet delegation gives explicit permissions to a service to create service-specific resources in the subnet by using a unique identifier during service deployment. Service endpoints extend virtual network identity to a PaaS service for traffic security rather than delegating resource creation. Network security groups and route tables filter and route traffic respectively.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["subnet delegation","subnet settings","PaaS"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-023","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to create a subnet that provides as few usable IP addresses as possible while still supporting three virtual machines. What is the smallest subnet address range you can specify?","choices":["/30","/31","/28","/29"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The smallest address range you can specify for an Azure subnet is /29, which provides eight addresses. Azure reserves five addresses in every subnet, so a /29 leaves three usable IP addresses for workloads. Ranges such as /30 and /31 cannot be specified, and a /28 is larger than necessary.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["subnet sizing","usable IP addresses","CIDR"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-024","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need traffic from a spoke virtual network to be inspected by a network virtual appliance that is deployed in a peered hub virtual network. What should you configure?","choices":["A user-defined route in the spoke subnet that uses the appliance's IP address in the hub as the next hop","A service endpoint on the spoke subnet","A private endpoint in the spoke virtual network","A network security group rule on the hub subnet"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Virtual network peering supports service chaining, where a user-defined route uses a virtual machine in the peered virtual network as the next hop so that traffic passes through the appliance. Service endpoints and private endpoints provide private access to PaaS services rather than redirecting traffic. A network security group rule only allows or denies traffic.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["service chaining","user-defined route","hub-and-spoke"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-025","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which statement correctly describes the network path used by traffic between resources in peered virtual networks?","choices":["The traffic is encrypted and sent over the public internet.","The traffic stays on the Microsoft backbone network and does not require a gateway or encryption.","The traffic must pass through a VPN gateway in one of the virtual networks.","The traffic is automatically routed through Azure Firewall."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Traffic between peered virtual networks is kept on the Microsoft backbone network, and no public internet, gateway, or encryption is required for the communication between the virtual networks. Gateways are needed only for on-premises connectivity, and Azure Firewall inspects traffic only when routing is configured to send traffic to it.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["peering","Microsoft backbone","traffic path"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-026","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual network peering","difficulty":"easy","question":"You create a virtual network peering between two virtual networks and do not change the default peering settings. What is the connectivity state between the two virtual networks?","choices":["All traffic is blocked until you create an allow rule on both networks.","Only traffic on port 443 is allowed between the networks.","Full connectivity is enabled by default, and you can use network security groups to block specific access.","Connectivity is limited to virtual machines that have the same name in both networks."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When you configure virtual network peering, full connectivity between the virtual networks is the default option, and you can apply network security groups in either virtual network to block or deny specific access. Traffic is not restricted to a single port, and resource names have no effect on peering connectivity.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-peering-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network peering","tags":["peering","default connectivity","network security group"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-027","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure public IP addresses","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to expose an Azure virtual machine to internet clients by using a public IP address. Which resource must the public IP address be associated with so that the virtual machine can use it?","choices":["The virtual network","The subnet","A route table","The network interface of the virtual machine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For a virtual machine, a public IP address is associated with the network interface, and that network interface is what attaches the address to the virtual machine. A public IP address is not associated with a virtual network or a subnet. A route table controls where traffic is sent rather than providing an address.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/ip-services/public-ip-addresses","sourceTitle":"Public IP addresses","tags":["public IP","network interface","virtual machine"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-028","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure public IP addresses","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need the fully qualified domain name contoso.westus.cloudapp.azure.com to resolve to a public IP address that you create in Azure. What should you configure on the public IP address?","choices":["A domain name label","A public DNS zone in Azure DNS","A private DNS zone linked to the virtual network","A service tag"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Specifying a domain name label on a public IP resource creates a mapping from domainnamelabel.location.cloudapp.azure.com to that public IP address by using Azure-managed DNS. Each domain name label must be unique within its Azure location. An Azure DNS public zone and a private DNS zone are separate services that you configure independently, and a service tag is a group of Azure service IP prefixes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/ip-services/public-ip-addresses","sourceTitle":"Public IP addresses","tags":["domain name label","public IP","FQDN"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-029","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure public IP addresses","difficulty":"medium","question":"A partner firewall allows inbound connections only from a fixed set of IP addresses. A virtual machine in your subscription must be reachable by that partner, and its public IP address must never change. Which configuration should you use?","choices":["Dynamic allocation with the Basic SKU","Static allocation with the Standard SKU","Dynamic allocation with the Standard SKU","Static allocation with the global tier"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard SKU public IP addresses support only static allocation, and the address remains dedicated to the resource until it is deleted, so the partner firewall rules stay valid. Dynamic allocation releases the address when the resource is stopped. The global tier is a routing option for cross-region load balancers, not an allocation method.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/ip-services/public-ip-addresses","sourceTitle":"Public IP addresses","tags":["public IP","static allocation","Standard SKU"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-030","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"hard","question":"You create a user-defined route with the 0.0.0.0/0 address prefix and the next hop type Virtual network gateway on a subnet. What happens to the default system routes for the reserved private address prefixes, such as 10.0.0.0/8?","choices":["They are moved to the virtual network gateway's route table.","They are removed from the subnet's route table.","They are converted into virtual network peering routes.","They remain and are still preferred over the user-defined route."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A default route of 0.0.0.0/0 with the next hop type Virtual network gateway causes the default system routes whose next hop type is None, including the reserved private address prefixes, to be removed from the subnet's route table. Those routes are not moved to the gateway and are not converted into peering routes, and a user-defined route overrides a system route when the prefixes match.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["0.0.0.0/0","system routes","user-defined route"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-031","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need traffic for all prefixes that belong to Azure Storage to be routed to a network virtual appliance, without listing every IP range manually. What should you specify as the address prefix of the user-defined route?","choices":["The virtual network's address space","0.0.0.0/0","A public IP address prefix","The service tag name Storage"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A service tag name such as Storage can be specified as the address prefix of a user-defined route, and Microsoft manages and automatically updates the address prefixes that the tag represents. The 0.0.0.0/0 prefix applies to all traffic that has no more specific route, and a public IP address prefix is a resource for reserved public addresses, not a route prefix.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["service tag","user-defined route","address prefix"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-032","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"hard","question":"You create a user-defined route whose next hop type is Virtual appliance and whose next hop IP address is the private IP address of a virtual machine that will forward traffic. What must you enable on that virtual machine's network interface?","choices":["IP forwarding","Accelerated networking","A public IP address","Just-in-time virtual machine access"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Any network interface that forwards traffic to an address other than its own must have the IP forwarding setting enabled, which disables Azure's check of the source and destination on that interface. Accelerated networking improves throughput, a public IP address is not required for forwarding, and just-in-time access controls management port access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["IP forwarding","virtual appliance","user-defined route"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-033","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure user-defined routes","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need to prevent routes that are advertised from an on-premises network through BGP from being added to the route tables of a specific subnet. What should you do?","choices":["Delete the system routes from the subnet's route table.","Disable virtual network gateway route propagation on the subnet's route table.","Create a user-defined route for every BGP prefix.","Enable a service endpoint on the subnet."],"answer":1,"explanation":"You disable ExpressRoute and Azure VPN Gateway route propagation by using a property on the route table, after which Azure does not add gateway routes to the subnets that have propagation disabled. Connectivity is then provided by custom routes whose next hop type is Virtual network gateway. Route propagation must never be disabled on GatewaySubnet because the gateway stops functioning.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network traffic routing","tags":["route propagation","BGP","route table"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-034","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot network connectivity","difficulty":"medium","question":"A virtual machine cannot reach a destination IP address, and you suspect that traffic is leaving the subnet through an unexpected next hop. Which Azure Network Watcher tool should you use?","choices":["Packet capture","Connection monitor","Next hop","Traffic analytics"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Next hop tool checks whether traffic is routed correctly to the intended destination and reports the next hop type, next hop IP address, and route table ID for a specific destination IP address. Packet capture records traffic contents, connection monitor tracks connectivity over time, and traffic analytics visualizes flow log data rather than showing routing decisions.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["Network Watcher","next hop","routing troubleshooting"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-035","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot network connectivity","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to determine whether a specific packet is allowed or denied to a virtual machine and which security rule is responsible. Which Network Watcher tool should you use?","choices":["VPN troubleshoot","Topology","Connection monitor","IP flow verify"],"answer":3,"explanation":"IP flow verify checks whether a packet is allowed or denied to or from an IP address and identifies the security rule that allowed or denied the traffic. VPN troubleshoot targets virtual network gateways and their connections, topology visualizes resource relationships, and connection monitor tracks connectivity over time instead of evaluating a single packet.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["Network Watcher","IP flow verify","security rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-036","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure virtual networks and subnets","difficulty":"medium","question":"You enable a service endpoint for Azure Storage on an existing subnet that contains running virtual machines. What should you expect to happen as a result of the route change?","choices":["Open TCP connections to the service might be terminated during the switch.","All virtual machines in the subnet must be stopped before the endpoint is enabled.","The subnet must contain no resources before the endpoint is enabled.","A virtual network gateway must exist in the virtual network."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Enabling a service endpoint on an existing subnet switches the routes on every network interface in the subnet, and any open TCP connections to the service might be terminated during that switch. You do not have to stop the virtual machines or empty the subnet, and a virtual network gateway is not required to use service endpoints.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-subnet","sourceTitle":"Add, change, or delete a virtual network subnet","tags":["service endpoint","subnet","connection interruption"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-037","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to filter traffic to a set of virtual machines in the same virtual network by using a logical group name instead of maintaining explicit IP addresses in the rules. Which feature should you use?","choices":["A route table","An application security group","A service tag","A private endpoint"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An application security group lets you group network interfaces and reference the group as the source or destination in a network security group rule, so you avoid maintaining explicit IP addresses. A service tag represents the address prefixes of Azure services rather than your own resources. A route table affects routing, and a private endpoint provides private access to a PaaS service.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/application-security-groups","sourceTitle":"Application security groups","tags":["application security group","network security group","logical grouping"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-038","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"How can an application security group be used in a network security group rule?","choices":["It can be specified only as a source, never as a destination.","It can be used only in outbound rules.","It can be specified as the source or as the destination of the rule.","It can be used only with a gateway load balancer."],"answer":2,"explanation":"An application security group can be specified as the source or the destination of a network security group rule, and the rule is applied to the network interfaces that are members of that group. Application security groups are not restricted to inbound or outbound rules and have no dependency on any load balancer type.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/application-security-groups","sourceTitle":"Application security groups","tags":["application security group","rule source","rule destination"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-039","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"hard","question":"Which requirement must all network interfaces that are members of the same application security group meet?","choices":["They must be deployed in the same subnet.","They must be attached to virtual machines of the same size.","They must each have a public IP address.","They must exist in the same virtual network as the first network interface added to the group."],"answer":3,"explanation":"All network interfaces assigned to an application security group must exist in the same virtual network as the first network interface that was added to that group, so you cannot add network interfaces from different virtual networks to the same group. They do not have to share a subnet, and neither virtual machine size nor public IP addressing is relevant.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/application-security-groups","sourceTitle":"Application security groups","tags":["application security group","constraints","virtual network"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-040","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You are designing network security group rules and want to keep the number of rules as small as possible while still allowing traffic from Azure services and from a defined set of your own virtual machines. Which two features should you use?","choices":["Service tags and application security groups","Explicit IP addresses and port ranges","Route tables and NAT gateways","Public IP address prefixes and private DNS zones"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Service tags and application security groups reduce the number of security rules because they let you reference groups of Azure service address prefixes or your own network interfaces instead of listing individual IP addresses. Listing explicit IP addresses and port ranges increases the number of rules. Route tables, NAT gateways, public IP prefixes, and DNS zones do not participate in security rule matching.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/application-security-groups","sourceTitle":"Application security groups","tags":["service tag","application security group","rule count"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-041","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"A virtual machine must accept Remote Desktop traffic from the internet. Network security groups are associated with both the virtual machine's subnet and its network interface. Which statement describes what is required?","choices":["Only the network security group on the subnet must allow the traffic.","Only the network security group on the network interface must allow the traffic.","Both the subnet network security group and the network interface network security group must allow the traffic.","Application security groups must replace both network security groups."],"answer":2,"explanation":"For inbound traffic, Azure evaluates the subnet network security group first and then the network interface network security group, and each one must allow the traffic for the traffic to reach the virtual machine. Configuring only one of the two network security groups is insufficient. Application security groups change how rule sources and destinations are scoped but do not remove the need for allow rules.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-group-how-it-works","sourceTitle":"How network security groups filter network traffic","tags":["network security group","inbound rules","subnet and NIC"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-042","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"Several network security group rules match the same traffic. How does Azure determine the action that is applied?","choices":["All matching rules are applied and the most restrictive action wins.","Rules are evaluated from the highest priority number to the lowest.","Rules are evaluated in alphabetical order by rule name.","Rules are processed in priority order, and processing stops at the first rule that matches."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Rules are processed in priority order, with lower numbers processed before higher numbers, and once traffic matches a rule the processing stops, so rules with lower priorities and the same attributes are never evaluated. Matching rules are not combined into a most restrictive decision, and rule names are not used for ordering.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["network security group","rule priority","evaluation order"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-043","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the valid priority range for a custom network security group rule?","choices":["100 to 4096","1 to 100","5000 to 64000","65000 to 65500"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A custom network security group rule must have a priority between 100 and 4096, and rules are processed from the lowest number to the highest. Priorities of 65000 and above are used by the Azure default rules such as AllowVNetInBound and DenyAllInbound, which you cannot create or modify yourself.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["network security group","rule priority","custom rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-044","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You want to allow traffic from two IP address ranges and two port ranges by using a single network security group rule. Which feature should you use?","choices":["A service tag as the source","An augmented security rule","An application security group as the destination","A security admin rule"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Augmented security rules allow you to specify multiple individual IP addresses and ranges, and multiple ports and port ranges, within a single rule, which reduces the number of rules you maintain. An application security group groups network interfaces rather than mixing explicit IP ranges, and a security admin rule is a global policy that Azure Virtual Network Manager enforces before network security group rules.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["augmented security rule","network security group","rule consolidation"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-045","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Create and configure network security groups and application security groups","difficulty":"hard","question":"Which statement about rule priorities in a network security group is correct?","choices":["Two rules in the same network security group can have the same priority and the same direction.","Two rules in the same network security group cannot have the same priority and the same direction.","Every rule in a network security group must have a unique priority regardless of direction.","Rule priorities must be multiples of 100."],"answer":1,"explanation":"You cannot create two security rules with the same priority and the same direction, because that combination would introduce a conflict in how the system processes traffic. Two rules with the same priority are permitted when their directions differ, so priorities do not have to be globally unique within the network security group. Priorities do not have to be multiples of 100.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["network security group","rule priority conflict","rule direction"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-046","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"hard","question":"You delete the network security group rule that allowed inbound SSH traffic to a virtual machine. An administrator reports that an existing SSH session is still connected. Why does the session remain active?","choices":["The change has not yet been deployed to the virtual machine.","A duplicate rule still exists in the effective security rules.","Virtual machines must be restarted before network security group changes take effect.","Network security group rules affect only new connections, so established connections continue."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Network security groups are stateful and create a flow record for existing connections, so new or updated rules apply exclusively to new connections. An established SSH session therefore continues after the allow rule is removed, while any new SSH connection attempt is blocked. No redeployment or virtual machine restart is required for the rule change to take effect.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["network security group","stateful","existing connections"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-047","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which set lists the default inbound rules that Azure creates in every network security group?","choices":["AllowVNetInBound, AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound, and DenyAllInbound","AllowInternetInBound, DenyVNetInBound, and DenyAllInbound","AllowAllInBound, DenySSHInBound, and DenyRDPInBound","AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound, AllowInternetInBound, and DenyAllInbound"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Azure creates three default inbound rules in every network security group: AllowVNetInBound at priority 65000, AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound at priority 65001, and DenyAllInbound at priority 65500. There is no default rule named AllowInternetInBound, AllowAllInBound, DenySSHInBound, or DenyRDPInBound.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["default rules","network security group","inbound rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-048","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which default inbound rule allows Azure Load Balancer health probe traffic to reach backend virtual machines?","choices":["AllowVNetInBound","AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound","DenyAllInbound","AllowInternetOutBound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The default AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound rule, at priority 65001, allows traffic that originates from the AzureLoadBalancer service tag, which is the source of the load balancer health probes. AllowVNetInBound covers traffic from within the virtual network, DenyAllInbound is the final deny rule, and AllowInternetOutBound is an outbound rule rather than an inbound one.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["default rules","load balancer","health probe"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-049","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to allow traffic that the default DenyAllInbound rule currently blocks. What can you do?","choices":["Delete the DenyAllInbound rule and then create an allow rule.","Change the priority of the DenyAllInbound rule to 4000.","Create a custom allow rule with a higher priority than the default rule.","Disable the default rules on the network security group."],"answer":2,"explanation":"You cannot remove or modify the default rules, but you can override them by creating custom rules with higher priorities, which means lower priority numbers. The DenyAllInbound rule at priority 65500 is bypassed only when a matching allow rule with a lower number is processed first. Default rules cannot be deleted, renumbered, or disabled.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["default rules","override","rule priority"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-050","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Azure service enforces security admin rules that are always evaluated before network security group rules?","choices":["Azure Firewall Manager","Azure DDoS Protection","Microsoft Defender for Cloud","Azure Virtual Network Manager"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Security admin rules originate from Azure Virtual Network Manager and always have a higher priority than network security group rules, so they are evaluated first and can terminate traffic evaluation. Azure Firewall Manager manages Azure Firewall policies, DDoS Protection mitigates volumetric attacks, and Defender for Cloud provides security posture management rather than global traffic rules.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-groups-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure network security groups overview","tags":["security admin rules","Virtual Network Manager","evaluation order"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-051","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to view the aggregate set of security rules that applies to a specific network interface, including the rules from the subnet the interface is in. Which Network Watcher capability should you use?","choices":["Effective security rules","Packet capture","Connection troubleshoot","VPN troubleshoot"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Effective security rules shows all security rules applied to a network interface, shows the rules from the subnet the network interface is in, and shows the aggregate of both. Packet capture records traffic, connection troubleshoot tests a connection at a point in time, and VPN troubleshoot targets virtual network gateways and their connections.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["effective security rules","Network Watcher","network interface"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-052","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"easy","question":"A user reports that inbound traffic to a virtual machine is blocked even though a rule that appears to allow it exists. Which tool should you use to identify the rule that is blocking the traffic?","choices":["Azure Policy compliance","NSG diagnostics","Cost analysis","Azure Advisor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NSG diagnostics detects traffic filtering issues at a virtual machine, virtual machine scale set, or application gateway, tells you which security rule allowed or denied the traffic, and lets you add a new security rule with a higher priority. Azure Policy, cost analysis, and Azure Advisor do not evaluate network security rules for a specific flow.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["NSG diagnostics","Network Watcher","traffic filtering"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-053","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Evaluate effective security rules in NSGs","difficulty":"hard","question":"Network security groups are associated with both a subnet and the network interfaces in that subnet. In which order does Azure evaluate the rules for inbound traffic and for outbound traffic?","choices":["Inbound and outbound traffic both evaluate the subnet network security group first.","Inbound traffic evaluates the network interface network security group first, and outbound traffic evaluates the subnet network security group first.","Inbound traffic evaluates the subnet network security group first, and outbound traffic evaluates the network interface network security group first.","Both directions evaluate only the network interface network security group."],"answer":2,"explanation":"For inbound traffic, Azure processes the rules in the network security group associated with the subnet and then the network security group associated with the network interface. For outbound traffic the order is reversed: the network interface network security group is evaluated first and the subnet network security group second. The same orders apply to intra-subnet traffic.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-group-how-it-works","sourceTitle":"How network security groups filter network traffic","tags":["network security group","evaluation order","inbound and outbound"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-054","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Implement Azure Bastion","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to provide RDP and SSH access to virtual machines that have no public IP addresses, without deploying your own jump server or exposing management ports to the internet. Which Azure service should you use?","choices":["Azure Firewall","Azure VPN Gateway","Azure Application Gateway","Azure Bastion"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Bastion is a fully managed PaaS service that provides secure and seamless RDP and SSH connectivity over TLS directly from the Azure portal, and the virtual machines do not need a public IP address, agent, or special client software. Azure Firewall filters traffic, a VPN gateway connects networks for hybrid connectivity, and Application Gateway load balances web traffic.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/bastion/bastion-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Bastion?","tags":["Azure Bastion","RDP","SSH"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-055","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Implement Azure Bastion","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which subnet name is required when you deploy Azure Bastion with the Basic, Standard, or Premium SKU?","choices":["AzureBastionSubnet","GatewaySubnet","AzureFirewallSubnet","ManagementSubnet"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Azure Bastion deployments with the Basic, Standard, and Premium SKUs require a dedicated subnet named AzureBastionSubnet and a public IP address. GatewaySubnet is reserved for virtual network gateways, and AzureFirewallSubnet is used by Azure Firewall rather than Bastion.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/bastion/bastion-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Bastion?","tags":["Azure Bastion","AzureBastionSubnet","deployment requirements"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-056","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Implement Azure Bastion","difficulty":"hard","question":"Your compliance team requires a recording of every remote management session that is opened through Azure Bastion. Which Bastion SKU is required?","choices":["Developer","Premium","Basic","Standard"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Session recording is available only in the Azure Bastion Premium SKU, which also supports private-only deployment without a public IP address. The Developer SKU uses shared infrastructure and is intended for development and testing, while Basic and Standard do not include session recording.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/bastion/bastion-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Bastion?","tags":["Azure Bastion","SKU","session recording"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-057","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Implement Azure Bastion","difficulty":"medium","question":"Administrators must connect to virtual machines by using the native RDP client that is already installed on their computers, rather than through the browser-based client. Which Bastion SKUs support native client connections?","choices":["Basic only","Developer only","Standard and Premium","Basic and Developer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Native client connections, which use the SSH or RDP client already installed on the local computer, are available in the Azure Bastion Standard and Premium SKUs, along with shareable links. Browser-based connections are available for all SKU tiers, and the Basic and Developer SKUs do not offer native client connectivity.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/bastion/bastion-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Bastion?","tags":["Azure Bastion","native client","SKU comparison"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-058","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Implement Azure Bastion","difficulty":"medium","question":"You deploy Azure Bastion into a hub virtual network. You need the same Bastion deployment to provide RDP access to virtual machines in a spoke virtual network, without deploying an additional Bastion host. What should you do?","choices":["Peer the spoke virtual network with the hub virtual network.","Assign public IP addresses to the virtual machines in the spoke virtual network.","Create a private endpoint in the spoke virtual network.","Create a service endpoint on the spoke subnet."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Azure Bastion is deployed into a virtual network and supports the virtual machines in that network, and you can deploy it once and use virtual network peering to serve multiple networks. The virtual machines do not require public IP addresses to be reached through Bastion, and private endpoints and service endpoints provide PaaS connectivity rather than virtual machine access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/bastion/bastion-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Bastion?","tags":["Azure Bastion","virtual network peering","hub-and-spoke"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-059","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Implement Azure Bastion","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which protocol and port are used by Azure Bastion to connect to virtual machines from the Azure portal?","choices":["RDP over port 3389 directly from the internet","IPsec over UDP port 500","Secure Shell over port 22 directly to the virtual machine's public IP address","RDP or SSH over TLS on port 443"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Bastion provides secure connectivity over TLS on port 443 for RDP and SSH sessions, which is what allows the virtual machines to remain without public IP addresses and protects them from port scanning. Direct RDP or SSH connections over ports 3389 and 22 are exactly what Bastion avoids exposing, and IPsec is used by site-to-site VPN rather than by Bastion.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/bastion/bastion-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Bastion?","tags":["Azure Bastion","TLS","port 443"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-060","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot network connectivity","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to test, at a single point in time, the connection between a virtual machine and a fully qualified domain name. Which Network Watcher tool should you use?","choices":["Connection monitor","Connection troubleshoot","Traffic analytics","Topology"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Connection troubleshoot tests a connection between a virtual machine, virtual machine scale set, application gateway, or Bastion host and a virtual machine, fully qualified domain name, URI, or IPv4 address, and it tests the connection at a point in time. Connection monitor performs the same kind of test but monitors the connection over time, traffic analytics visualizes flow log data, and topology visualizes resource relationships.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["connection troubleshoot","Network Watcher","connectivity test"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-061","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure service endpoints for Azure platform as a service (PaaS)","difficulty":"easy","question":"You enable a service endpoint for Microsoft.Storage on a subnet. What happens to the source IP addresses that the virtual machines use when they access Azure Storage from that subnet?","choices":["They switch from public IPv4 addresses to their private IPv4 addresses.","They switch from private IPv4 addresses to public IPv4 addresses.","They remain the public IPv4 addresses assigned to the virtual machines.","They are translated to the virtual network gateway's address."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With service endpoints, the source IP addresses used for service traffic from the subnet switch from public IPv4 addresses to the private IPv4 addresses assigned to the resources. The opposite direction is not what happens, the addresses are not left unchanged, and no gateway translation occurs. Because of this switch, existing firewall rules that use public IP addresses stop working.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-service-endpoints-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network service endpoints","tags":["service endpoint","source IP","private addresses"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-062","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure service endpoints for Azure platform as a service (PaaS)","difficulty":"easy","question":"How do service endpoints affect DNS resolution for the Azure service that is being accessed from the subnet?","choices":["The service name resolves to the private IP address of the service endpoint.","DNS entries for the service remain as they are and continue to resolve to public IP addresses.","The service endpoint creates a private DNS zone for the service automatically.","DNS resolution for the service is disabled on the subnet."],"answer":1,"explanation":"With service endpoints, DNS entries for Azure services remain as they are and continue to resolve to the public IP addresses that are assigned to the Azure service. Service endpoints do not create private DNS zones and do not change resolution to private addresses. Changing resolution to a private IP address is what private endpoints and private DNS zones are used for.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-service-endpoints-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network service endpoints","tags":["service endpoint","DNS","public IP resolution"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-063","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure service endpoints for Azure platform as a service (PaaS)","difficulty":"medium","question":"For which Azure service does a virtual network service endpoint apply only to service traffic within the virtual network's region?","choices":["Azure Storage","Azure Key Vault","Azure SQL Database","Azure Cosmos DB"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For Azure SQL Database, the virtual networks must be in the same region as the Azure service resource, so the service endpoint applies only to service traffic within the virtual network's region. For other services, including Azure Storage, Azure Key Vault, and Azure Cosmos DB, you can secure service resources to virtual networks in any region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-service-endpoints-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network service endpoints","tags":["service endpoint","Azure SQL Database","regional scope"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-064","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure service endpoints for Azure platform as a service (PaaS)","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need to restrict which Azure Storage accounts can be reached from a subnet over a service endpoint so that data exfiltration to other storage accounts is prevented. What should you configure?","choices":["A network security group rule that uses the Storage service tag as the destination","A user-defined route that sends Storage traffic to a network virtual appliance","A service endpoint for Microsoft.Storage on every subnet in the virtual network","A virtual network service endpoint policy on the subnet"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A virtual network service endpoint policy allows you to allowlist specific Azure Storage accounts over a service endpoint and denies access to every other storage account, which provides granular protection against data exfiltration. A network security group rule that uses the Storage service tag restricts traffic to a Storage region but allows any account in that region. Enabling the endpoint on more subnets broadens access rather than restricting it.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-service-endpoint-policies-overview","sourceTitle":"Virtual network service endpoint policies for Azure Storage","tags":["service endpoint policy","data exfiltration","Azure Storage"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-065","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure service endpoints for Azure platform as a service (PaaS)","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which group of Azure services supports virtual network service endpoints?","choices":["Azure Storage, Azure SQL Database, Azure Key Vault, and Azure Cosmos DB","Only virtual machines and load balancers","Only Azure Blob Storage and Azure Files","Only Microsoft Entra ID and Microsoft 365"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Service endpoints are generally available for Azure Storage, Azure SQL Database, Azure Cosmos DB, Azure Key Vault, Azure Service Bus, Azure Event Hubs, Azure App Service, and Azure Container Registry, among others. Virtual machines and load balancers are IaaS resources rather than PaaS services that you secure with service endpoints, and Microsoft Entra ID does not natively support service endpoints.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-service-endpoints-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network service endpoints","tags":["service endpoint","supported services","PaaS"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-066","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure service endpoints for Azure platform as a service (PaaS)","difficulty":"medium","question":"An Azure Storage account is secured to a virtual network by using a service endpoint. On-premises servers must also reach the storage account. What should you configure?","choices":["A service endpoint on the on-premises network","An allow rule in the storage account's IP firewall for the on-premises public NAT IP addresses","A network security group rule that allows the Storage service tag on the on-premises network","A storage account private endpoint in the on-premises network"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By default, Azure service resources that are secured to a virtual network are not reachable from on-premises networks. To allow access, you must also allow the public NAT IP addresses that your on-premises network or ExpressRoute uses through the resource's IP firewall configuration. Service endpoints cannot be used for traffic from on-premises services, and a network security group rule does not authorize the storage account to accept that traffic.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-service-endpoints-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network service endpoints","tags":["service endpoint","on-premises access","storage firewall"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-067","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure service endpoints for Azure platform as a service (PaaS)","difficulty":"hard","question":"After you enable a service endpoint for Azure Storage on a subnet, existing storage firewall rules that are based on the virtual machines' public IP addresses stop working. Why?","choices":["The firewall rules are deleted automatically when a service endpoint is enabled.","The storage account is moved to the same region as the virtual network.","Service traffic from the subnet now uses the private IPv4 addresses of the resources as source addresses.","The service endpoint changes the DNS name of the storage account."],"answer":2,"explanation":"With service endpoints, the source IP addresses used for service traffic from the subnet switch from public IPv4 addresses to the private IPv4 addresses of the resources, so IP firewall rules that allow specific public addresses no longer match the traffic. The rules are not deleted automatically, the storage account is not moved, and the service's DNS name does not change.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-service-endpoints-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure virtual network service endpoints","tags":["service endpoint","IP firewall","source address switch"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-068","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to connect to an Azure SQL Database server from a virtual network by using a private IP address that belongs to that virtual network. Which resource should you create?","choices":["A service endpoint for Microsoft.Sql on the subnet","A NAT gateway associated with the subnet","A public IP address prefix","A private endpoint"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A private endpoint is a network interface that uses a private IP address from your virtual network and connects privately to a service that is powered by Azure Private Link, such as Azure SQL Database. A service endpoint keeps traffic on the Azure backbone but the service is still reached over its public endpoint and public DNS name. NAT gateways and public IP address prefixes provide public addressing rather than private connectivity to the service.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","Azure SQL Database","Private Link"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-069","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which statement correctly describes the private IP address that a private endpoint uses?","choices":["It is dynamically assigned from the subnet and remains unchanged for the lifecycle of the private endpoint.","It changes every time the private endpoint connection is restarted.","It is a public IP address that Azure translates to a private address.","It is assigned from the address space of the private-link resource."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When you create a private endpoint, Azure creates a read-only network interface and assigns it a dynamic private IP address from the subnet you selected, and that value remains unchanged for the entire lifecycle of the private endpoint. The address is not reassigned when connections restart, it is not a public address, and it comes from your virtual network's subnet rather than from the service.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","private IP","network interface"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-070","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"medium","question":"You create a private endpoint that connects to a storage account owned by another team. The private endpoint is created in the Pending state. What must happen before the private endpoint can send traffic?","choices":["The virtual network must be peered with the virtual network that hosts the storage account.","The owner of the private-link resource must approve the connection.","The private endpoint must be converted into a service endpoint.","A network security group rule must be created on the private endpoint's subnet."],"answer":1,"explanation":"When you create a private endpoint without the permissions needed for automatic approval, the connection is created in a Pending state, and the owner of the private-link resource is responsible for approving it. Only private endpoints in an Approved state can send traffic to the private-link resource. Peering is not required, and a service endpoint is a different connectivity model.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","approval workflow","connection state"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-071","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"easy","question":"On-premises servers must reach a storage account over the private IP address of a private endpoint. Which connectivity options satisfy this requirement?","choices":["Only Azure Bastion","Only a site-to-site VPN connection that uses Microsoft peering","A VPN or ExpressRoute connection, because private endpoints can be reached from on-premises over those connections","Private endpoints cannot be reached from on-premises networks at all"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Private endpoints enable connectivity from the same virtual network, from regionally and globally peered virtual networks, and from on-premises environments that use VPN or ExpressRoute. There is no need to configure ExpressRoute Microsoft peering or to traverse the internet to reach the service. Azure Bastion provides remote management access rather than private service connectivity.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","on-premises","ExpressRoute"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-072","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"hard","question":"Which type of storage account must you use before you can create a private endpoint for it?","choices":["A classic storage account","A Blob Storage account","A FileStorage account","A General Purpose v2 (GPv2) storage account"],"answer":3,"explanation":"You can create private endpoints only on a General Purpose v2 storage account. Classic storage accounts do not support private endpoints, and the other account kinds listed are not the documented requirement for creating a private endpoint.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","General Purpose v2","storage account"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-073","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which network controls can you enable as network policies for a private endpoint?","choices":["Network security groups, user-defined routes, and application security groups","Network security groups only","Azure DDoS Protection and service tags only","Public IP address prefixes and NAT gateways only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Private endpoints support network policies, which enable support for network security groups, user-defined routes, and application security groups, and you manage those policies for the private endpoint. Private endpoint network policy is not limited to network security groups. DDoS Protection, service tags, public IP address prefixes, and NAT gateways are not private endpoint network policies.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","network policies","application security group"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-074","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"easy","question":"An application still connects to a storage account by using its public endpoint even though a private endpoint was created. What must you configure so that the storage account's fully qualified domain name resolves to the private IP address?","choices":["A service endpoint on the subnet","DNS settings, typically a private DNS zone that is linked to the virtual network","A user-defined route that points to the private endpoint","A public IP address prefix"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Existing Azure services often already have DNS configuration for the public endpoint, so connecting over a private endpoint requires separate DNS settings, usually configured with private DNS zones, so that the fully qualified domain name resolves to the private IP address of the private endpoint. A service endpoint keeps the public DNS name and the public IP address. A user-defined route cannot change name resolution.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","private DNS zone","name resolution"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-075","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure private endpoints for Azure PaaS","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to create a private endpoint in a virtual network that is in West Europe, and it must connect to a private-link resource that is deployed in East US. Is this configuration supported?","choices":["No, the private endpoint and the private-link resource must be in the same region.","No, private endpoints can control only resources in the same subscription.","Yes, because the private endpoint must be in the same region and subscription as the virtual network, but the private-link resource can be in a different region.","Yes, but only if both regions are in the same geopolitical region."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The private endpoint must be deployed in the same region and subscription as the virtual network, but the private-link resource can be deployed in a different region, which is what enables cross-region private access. The private-link resource can also be in a different subscription; neither resource has to be in the same geopolitical region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-overview","sourceTitle":"What is a private endpoint?","tags":["private endpoint","regions","cross-region"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-076","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot network connectivity","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need a private, dedicated connection between your on-premises network and Azure that does not traverse the public internet. Which service should you use?","choices":["Azure VPN Gateway with a point-to-site connection","Azure Bastion","Azure Application Gateway","Azure ExpressRoute"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ExpressRoute extends your on-premises network into the Microsoft cloud over a private connection that is provided by a connectivity provider, and the traffic does not go over the public internet. A point-to-site VPN sends encrypted traffic over the internet, Bastion provides remote management sessions rather than network connectivity, and Application Gateway load balances web traffic.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/expressroute/expressroute-introduction","sourceTitle":"What is Azure ExpressRoute?","tags":["ExpressRoute","private connection","hybrid connectivity"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-077","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot network connectivity","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to connect an on-premises site to an Azure virtual network by using an encrypted tunnel over the public internet. Which service should you use?","choices":["Azure VPN Gateway with a site-to-site connection","Azure ExpressRoute","Azure Firewall","An Azure Route Server"],"answer":0,"explanation":"VPN Gateway sends encrypted traffic between an Azure virtual network and on-premises locations over the public internet, and a site-to-site connection creates an IPsec/IKE tunnel between the VPN gateway and an on-premises VPN device. ExpressRoute uses a private connection that does not traverse the internet, Azure Firewall filters traffic rather than providing the tunnel, and a route server exchanges routing information.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/vpn-gateway/vpn-gateway-about-vpngateways","sourceTitle":"What is Azure VPN Gateway?","tags":["VPN Gateway","site-to-site","IPsec"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-078","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot network connectivity","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to provide an administrator's laptop with encrypted access to an Azure virtual network without making changes to the existing on-premises network. Which connection type should you use?","choices":["A site-to-site VPN connection","A point-to-site VPN connection","An ExpressRoute circuit","A virtual network peering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A point-to-site VPN connection is established between a virtual network and a single computer and is useful when you require few or no changes to an existing network, and the communication uses an encrypted tunnel over the internet. A site-to-site connection requires an on-premises VPN device on the network, ExpressRoute requires a connectivity provider, and peering connects virtual networks rather than an individual computer.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Virtual Network?","tags":["point-to-site VPN","remote access","hybrid connectivity"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-079","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which DNS record type must be used to create a Sender Policy Framework (SPF) record in Azure DNS?","choices":["SPF","SRV","TXT","CAA"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SPF records are supported by Azure DNS and must be created by using the TXT record type, and the obsolete SPF record type is not supported. When a DNS zone file is imported, any SPF records that use the SPF record type are converted to the TXT record type. SRV records specify server locations, and CAA records specify which certificate authorities may issue certificates for a domain.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/dns-zones-records","sourceTitle":"Overview of DNS zones and records","tags":["Azure DNS","SPF","TXT record"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-080","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"hard","question":"You create a public DNS zone named contoso.com in Azure DNS. Which record sets are created automatically at the zone apex?","choices":["An A record set and an MX record set","A CNAME record set and a TXT record set","An SOA record set only","An NS record set and an SOA record set"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An NS record set and an SOA record set are created automatically at the apex of every DNS zone and are deleted automatically when the zone is deleted, and they cannot be deleted separately. A CNAME record set cannot be created at the zone apex because the apex already contains the NS and SOA record sets. Azure DNS does not create A or MX record sets automatically.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/dns-zones-records","sourceTitle":"Overview of DNS zones and records","tags":["Azure DNS","zone apex","NS and SOA records"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-081","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need virtual machines in a specific virtual network to be able to resolve the records that are published in an Azure private DNS zone. What must you do?","choices":["Link the virtual network to the private DNS zone.","Create a public DNS zone that has the same name.","Associate a NAT gateway with the virtual network.","Enable a service endpoint on the subnet."],"answer":0,"explanation":"To resolve the records of a private DNS zone from a virtual network, you must link the virtual network with the zone; linked virtual networks have full access and can resolve all DNS records published in the private zone. A public zone with the same name is used only for split-horizon scenarios and does not by itself grant resolution from the virtual network. NAT gateways and service endpoints have no role in private DNS resolution.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/private-dns-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Private DNS?","tags":["private DNS zone","virtual network link","name resolution"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-082","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which DNS record types are limited to a single record per record set in Azure DNS?","choices":["A and AAAA","SOA and CNAME","MX and TXT","SRV and NS"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The SOA and CNAME record types are exceptions, because the DNS standards do not permit multiple records with the same name for these types, so their record sets can contain only a single record. A, AAAA, MX, TXT, SRV, and NS record sets can contain multiple records.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/dns-zones-records","sourceTitle":"Overview of DNS zones and records","tags":["Azure DNS","record set","CNAME"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-083","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to control how long clients cache the records that are published in a DNS zone hosted in Azure DNS. Where do you configure this value?","choices":["On each individual record in the record set","On the DNS zone's SOA record only","On the record set","On the virtual network link"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In Azure DNS, the time to live is specified for the record set rather than for each individual record, so the same value applies to all records within that record set. You can specify any TTL value between 1 and 2,147,483,647 seconds. Virtual network links apply to private DNS zones and do not define TTL values.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/dns-zones-records","sourceTitle":"Overview of DNS zones and records","tags":["Azure DNS","TTL","record set"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-084","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"hard","question":"A virtual network must automatically register its virtual machine records in one private DNS zone, and it must also be able to resolve records that are published in a second private DNS zone. What is the correct configuration?","choices":["Link the virtual network to both private zones with autoregistration enabled on both links.","Link the virtual network to the second zone only and create the records manually in the first zone.","Enable autoregistration on the private zone without linking the virtual network.","Link the virtual network to one private DNS zone with autoregistration enabled and link the virtual network to the second private DNS zone without autoregistration."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A specific virtual network can be linked to only one private DNS zone when automatic registration of virtual machine DNS records is enabled, so autoregistration can be enabled on only one of the two links. The virtual network can still be linked to additional private zones without autoregistration so that it can resolve their records. Autoregistration cannot be enabled without a virtual network link.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/private-dns-autoregistration","sourceTitle":"What is the autoregistration feature in Azure DNS private zones?","tags":["autoregistration","private DNS zone","virtual network link"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-085","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"easy","question":"A virtual network link has autoregistration enabled. What happens to a virtual machine's DNS record when that virtual machine is deleted?","choices":["The automatically registered record is removed from the private DNS zone.","The record remains and must be deleted manually.","The record is converted into a CNAME record.","The entire private DNS zone is deleted."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When a virtual machine that is registered in a private DNS zone with autoregistration enabled is deleted or stopped, Azure DNS automatically removes the corresponding DNS record from the linked private zone. The record does not have to be deleted manually and is not converted to another record type, and the zone itself is unaffected.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/private-dns-autoregistration","sourceTitle":"What is the autoregistration feature in Azure DNS private zones?","tags":["autoregistration","record cleanup","virtual machine"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-086","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure Azure DNS","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which statement about an Azure private DNS zone is correct?","choices":["The zone is scoped to a single region and becomes unavailable if that region fails.","The zone is stored as a global resource and can be linked to virtual networks in different regions.","The zone requires a public DNS zone with the same name before it can be used.","The zone can be resolved from only one virtual network."],"answer":1,"explanation":"When you create a private DNS zone, Azure stores the zone data as a global resource, so the private zone is not dependent on a single virtual network or region and can be linked to virtual networks in different regions. A public zone with the same name is needed only for split-horizon scenarios, and a single private zone can be linked to multiple virtual networks.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/dns/private-dns-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Private DNS?","tags":["private DNS zone","resiliency","global resource"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-087","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"easy","question":"A load balancer must distribute traffic to web servers that are reachable only from inside a virtual network, and clients will connect from an on-premises network over a VPN. Which type of load balancer should you create?","choices":["A public load balancer that has a public frontend IP address","A gateway load balancer","An internal load balancer","A cross-region public load balancer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An internal, or private, load balancer load balances traffic inside a virtual network and provides inbound connectivity to virtual machines in private network connectivity scenarios, such as accessing a load balancer frontend from an on-premises network in a hybrid scenario. A public load balancer exposes the service to the internet, and a gateway load balancer is used for chaining third-party network virtual appliances.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Load Balancer?","tags":["internal load balancer","hybrid connectivity","private traffic"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-088","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which protocols does Azure Load Balancer distribute?","choices":["HTTP and HTTPS only","TCP only","TCP, UDP, and ICMP","TCP and UDP"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Load Balancer operates at layer 4 of the OSI model and distributes TCP and UDP traffic across backend pool instances. HTTP and HTTPS are application-layer protocols handled by services such as Application Gateway. ICMP is not a protocol that Azure Load Balancer distributes.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Load Balancer?","tags":["load balancer","layer 4","TCP and UDP"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-089","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"medium","question":"You create a Standard SKU load balancer that has a public frontend IP address. Which SKU must the public IP address use?","choices":["Standard","Basic","Either Basic or Standard, because they can be mixed","The gateway load balancer SKU"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When you use a public Standard Load Balancer, the SKU of the public IP address must be Standard, and Basic public IP addresses are not supported on a Standard load balancer. Matching SKUs are required for load balancer and public IP resources, and you cannot mix Basic SKU and Standard SKU resources. The Basic SKU of both resource types has been retired.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/skus","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer SKUs","tags":["load balancer","SKU matching","public IP"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-090","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which feature allows a Standard SKU internal load balancer to load balance all ports simultaneously for high availability network virtual appliance scenarios?","choices":["Outbound rules","HA Ports","TCP reset on idle","A zone-redundant frontend IP configuration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HA Ports are available for the internal load balancer with the Standard SKU and allow all ports to be load balanced at the same time. Outbound rules configure declarative outbound NAT, TCP reset on idle controls connection handling, and a zone-redundant frontend IP configuration affects resiliency rather than port coverage. HA Ports are not available with the Basic SKU.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/skus","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer SKUs","tags":["HA Ports","internal load balancer","network virtual appliance"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-091","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"hard","question":"UDP traffic is load balanced by a Standard SKU load balancer, and all backend instances fail their health probes. What happens to the existing UDP flows?","choices":["Existing UDP flows continue because UDP is connectionless.","Existing UDP flows are redirected to the load balancer frontend.","All existing UDP flows terminate.","Existing UDP flows are converted into TCP flows."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When all instances report probes down, no new flows are sent to the backend pool and all existing UDP flows terminate. With a Standard load balancer, established TCP flows continue when the backend pool has more than one backend instance, but that behavior applies to TCP connections rather than to UDP datagrams. UDP flows are not redirected or converted to another protocol.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-custom-probe-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer health probes","tags":["health probe","UDP flows","probe down behavior"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-092","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which distribution mode sends successive requests from the same client IP address and protocol combination to the same backend virtual machine?","choices":["Five-tuple hash, which is the default behavior","Two-tuple affinity that uses the source and destination IP addresses","Destination port affinity only","Three-tuple affinity that uses the source IP address, destination IP address, and protocol type"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Three-tuple affinity, which the portal labels as Client IP and protocol, uses the source IP address, destination IP address, and protocol type, and it is configured with the SourceIPProtocol value. Two-tuple affinity uses only the source and destination IP addresses, the default behavior is five-tuple hash, and there is no destination port affinity distribution mode.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-distribution-mode","sourceTitle":"Configure the distribution mode for Azure Load Balancer","tags":["distribution mode","session persistence","three-tuple"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-093","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"easy","question":"By default, which distribution mode does Azure Load Balancer use?","choices":["A five-tuple hash that allows successive requests from the same client to be handled by any virtual machine","A two-tuple hash based on the source and destination IP addresses","A three-tuple hash based on the source IP address, destination IP address, and protocol","A strict round robin that always alternates between backend virtual machines"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The default five-tuple hash, which the portal shows as None (hash-based), specifies that successive requests from the same client can be handled by any virtual machine. Two-tuple and three-tuple modes are optional session persistence settings that provide source IP affinity, and Azure Load Balancer does not use a strict round robin algorithm.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-distribution-mode","sourceTitle":"Configure the distribution mode for Azure Load Balancer","tags":["distribution mode","five-tuple hash","default behavior"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-094","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need a Standard SKU load balancer to provide outbound public connectivity to backend virtual machines by using a declarative configuration, without assigning an instance-level public IP address to each virtual machine. What should you configure?","choices":["An inbound NAT rule","Outbound rules","A health probe that uses a custom path","HA Ports on the public load balancer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard Load Balancer supports outbound rules, which provide declarative outbound NAT configuration so that backend instances can make outbound connections without individual instance-level public IP addresses. Inbound NAT rules provide port forwarding for inbound traffic, health probes determine backend health, and HA Ports are available only for the internal load balancer.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/skus","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer SKUs","tags":["outbound rules","SNAT","Standard load balancer"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-095","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Configure an internal or public load balancer","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need the load balancer to forward inbound traffic to one specific virtual machine on one specific port instead of distributing it across the whole backend pool. Which type of rule should you use?","choices":["A load-balancing rule with HA Ports enabled","An outbound rule","An inbound NAT rule","A load-balancing rule that uses floating IP"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Inbound NAT rules forward traffic to a specific backend virtual machine on a specific port, which is port forwarding. Load-balancing rules distribute traffic across the backend pool instead of targeting one instance. Outbound rules configure SNAT for outbound connections, and enabling HA Ports is unrelated to port forwarding.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Load Balancer?","tags":["inbound NAT rule","port forwarding","load balancer rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-096","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot load balancing","difficulty":"hard","question":"Backend virtual machines behind a Standard SKU load balancer are marked unhealthy even though the application is running. Network security groups are associated with the subnet and the network interfaces. What should you check?","choices":["That the load balancer's frontend IP address is in the same subnet as the backend virtual machines","That each backend virtual machine has its own public IP address","That the health probe is configured to use HTTPS instead of TCP","That the health probe traffic from the source IP address 168.63.129.16 is allowed by the network security group and any local firewall"],"answer":3,"explanation":"All IPv4 load balancer health probes originate from the IP address 168.63.129.16, and that address must be allowed in any Azure network security groups and local firewall policies. Otherwise the probe cannot reach the instance and the load balancer marks it down. The frontend IP address does not need to be in the backend subnet, backend virtual machines do not need public IP addresses, and the probe can use TCP, HTTP, or HTTPS.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-custom-probe-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer health probes","tags":["health probe","probe source IP","network security group"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-097","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot load balancing","difficulty":"easy","question":"A health probe fails for one backend instance in a load balancer backend pool. What happens to new connections that are directed to that instance?","choices":["The load balancer stops sending new connections to the unhealthy instance.","The load balancer continues sending new connections until every instance fails.","The load balancer removes the entire backend pool from service.","The load balancer restarts the unhealthy virtual machine."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When a health probe fails, the load balancer stops sending new connections to the respective unhealthy instance and continues to use the remaining healthy instances. Outbound connectivity is not affected by probe failures, only inbound distribution. The load balancer does not remove the whole backend pool or restart virtual machines.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-custom-probe-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer health probes","tags":["health probe","unhealthy instance","new connections"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-098","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot load balancing","difficulty":"medium","question":"An HTTP health probe is configured with an interval of 120 seconds, and a backend instance does not respond at all. What happens to the probe?","choices":["The probe waits for the full 120 seconds before it can fail.","The probe reaches its 30-second timeout and fails.","The probe never fails because HTTP probes do not time out.","The probe immediately marks the instance as healthy."],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP and HTTPS health probes have a built-in timeout of 30 seconds. If the configured interval is longer than that timeout and no response is received within 30 seconds, the probe reaches its timeout period and fails. When the configured interval is shorter than the timeout, the probe fails when the next probe is sent.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-custom-probe-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer health probes","tags":["health probe","probe timeout","HTTP probe"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-099","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot load balancing","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which type of load balancer rule does not require a health probe to be attached?","choices":["A load-balancing rule for TCP","A load-balancing rule for UDP","An inbound NAT rule","A load-balancing rule that uses HA Ports"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unlike load-balancing rules, inbound NAT rules do not need a health probe attached to them. Load-balancing rules require a health probe to detect endpoint status, and HA Ports load-balancing rules also rely on a health probe response that reflects the status of the entire instance.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-custom-probe-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer health probes","tags":["inbound NAT rule","health probe","load balancing rules"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D4-100","domain":4,"domainName":"Networking","subtopic":"Troubleshoot load balancing","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to test how a load balancer behaves when a backend instance fails its health probe, without stopping the application on the instance. What should you do?","choices":["Delete the load-balancing rule that uses the health probe.","Change the health probe interval to 5 seconds.","Enable TCP reset on idle for the load-balancing rule.","Create a network security group rule that blocks the probe's destination port or source IP address."],"answer":3,"explanation":"To simulate a health probe failure or mark an individual instance down, you create a network security group rule that explicitly blocks the probe's destination port or source IP address. Changing the probe interval affects only how frequently the probe runs, TCP reset on idle changes connection behavior, and deleting the load-balancing rule removes load balancing instead of testing the probe.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-balancer/load-balancer-custom-probe-overview","sourceTitle":"Azure Load Balancer health probes","tags":["health probe failure","simulation","network security group"],"sourceFile":"domain-4-networking.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-001","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to view near real-time CPU utilization for several Azure virtual machines on a single chart. Which Azure Monitor feature should you use?","choices":["Metrics explorer","Azure Monitor Logs with a KQL query","The Azure activity log","Azure Advisor recommendations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Platform metrics are collected automatically and stored in the Azure Monitor metrics database, so metrics explorer can chart them in near real time without any collection configuration. Log queries need the data collected into a Log Analytics workspace first, and the activity log records management operations rather than performance data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/data-platform-metrics","sourceTitle":"Azure Monitor Metrics overview","tags":["azure monitor","metrics","metrics explorer"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-002","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"How long does Azure Monitor retain platform metrics and custom metrics?","choices":["30 days","93 days","90 days","7 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Microsoft documents that platform and custom metrics are stored for 93 days. The portal limits a single metrics chart to 30 days of data, which is a display limit rather than the retention period, so 30 days is incorrect.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/data-platform-metrics","sourceTitle":"Azure Monitor Metrics overview","tags":["azure monitor","metrics","retention"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-003","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"A metric such as Available disk space has a name/value pair called Drive that contains the values C: and D:. What is this name/value pair called?","choices":["A metric namespace","A metric alert condition","A metric dimension","A resource tag"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Metric dimensions are name/value pairs that carry additional data to describe a metric value, which lets you chart or filter the metric per drive. A namespace is a category for a metric, and tags are used for resource organization rather than metric filtering.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/data-platform-metrics","sourceTitle":"Azure Monitor Metrics overview","tags":["azure monitor","metrics","dimensions"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-004","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"An administrator charts a platform metric for the last 40 days in metrics explorer. What will the chart display?","choices":["All 40 days of data on one chart","Only the last 14 days of data","No data, because platform metrics are retained for 30 days","A maximum of 30 days of data, and the chart can be panned to view the remaining retention window."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Platform and custom metrics are retained for 93 days, but the portal limits a single metrics chart to 30 days of data; after selecting a 30-day interval the chart can be panned across the full retention window. The 14-day figure applies to classic guest OS metrics written to a storage account.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/data-platform-metrics","sourceTitle":"Azure Monitor Metrics overview","tags":["azure monitor","metrics","retention"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-005","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which type of monitoring data does Azure Monitor collect automatically, with no configuration required?","choices":["Resource logs","Platform metrics","Guest OS performance counters","Blob storage access logs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Platform metrics are collected automatically without configuration and are stored in the Azure Monitor metrics database. Resource logs and guest OS performance counters are not collected by default; they require a diagnostic setting or a data collection rule.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["azure monitor","platform metrics","data collection"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-006","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to collect guest operating system performance counters from Azure VMs and alert on them by using Azure Monitor metrics. What should you use?","choices":["The Log Analytics agent","The Azure Diagnostics extension writing to a storage account","The Azure Monitor agent with a data collection rule","The VMSnapshot extension"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Guest OS metrics sent to Azure Monitor Metrics are collected by the Azure Monitor agent based on a data collection rule. The Log Analytics agent was retired in August 2024, and the Azure Diagnostics extension keeps classic guest OS metrics in a storage account with a shorter, non-guaranteed retention.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/data-platform-metrics","sourceTitle":"Azure Monitor Metrics overview","tags":["azure monitor agent","data collection rule","guest metrics"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-007","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"A chart shows HTTP failures for a resource that has three servers, and the metric has a Server dimension. Which action adds separate lines for each server to the chart?","choices":["Changing the aggregation type to Count","Increasing the time granularity","Adding a resource tag filter","Splitting the chart by the Server dimension"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Splitting a chart by a dimension displays the underlying per-dimension data, such as one line per server. Changing the aggregation changes which statistic is calculated, and increasing the time granularity only smooths the existing lines.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/metrics-aggregation-explained","sourceTitle":"Azure Monitor Metrics aggregation and display explained","tags":["azure monitor","metrics","dimensions","splitting"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-008","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need to keep platform metrics for an Azure storage account in a Log Analytics workspace so that you can query them with KQL for long-term trending. What should you configure?","choices":["A diagnostic setting on the storage account that routes the platform metrics to the workspace","An alert rule with a metric signal","A workspace transformation data collection rule","A metric alert action group"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A diagnostic setting can route supported platform metrics for a resource to a Log Analytics workspace, where they can be queried with KQL. Alert rules and action groups only notify or act on data, and a workspace transformation DCR transforms data that is already being sent to the workspace.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","platform metrics","log analytics"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-009","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Interpret metrics in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"A platform metric intermittently reports no data for a period. How are those missing values handled when an average aggregation is calculated?","choices":["The values are recorded as zero and included in the average.","The values are interpolated from the nearest data points.","The values are recorded as NULL and are not counted as valid measurements.","The entire time series is discarded for that time range."],"answer":2,"explanation":"When data is expected but not received, the system records NULL, and NULL values are not counted as valid measurements, so the average uses fewer data points. Treating the gaps as zeros would lower the average more than the documented behavior, and no interpolation or time-series deletion occurs.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/metrics-aggregation-explained","sourceTitle":"Azure Monitor Metrics aggregation and display explained","tags":["azure monitor","metrics","aggregation","null values"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-010","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which type of monitoring data is not collected by default and requires a diagnostic setting before it can be collected?","choices":["Platform metrics","Activity log entries","Azure Advisor recommendations","Resource logs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Resource logs capture data-plane operations inside a resource and are not collected until a diagnostic setting is created. Platform metrics and activity log entries are collected automatically without any configuration.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","resource logs","azure monitor"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-011","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to stream a resource's logs to a non-Microsoft SIEM solution. Which diagnostic setting destination should you use?","choices":["Azure Event Hubs","Azure Storage account","Log Analytics workspace","An Azure Cosmos DB account"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Event Hubs is the destination designed for streaming data to external systems such as non-Microsoft SIEM solutions. A storage account keeps data for audit or static analysis but does not stream it, and a Log Analytics workspace is queried inside Azure Monitor.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","event hubs","siem"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-012","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must send a resource's diagnostic logs to two different Log Analytics workspaces. What should you do?","choices":["Create one diagnostic setting and select both workspaces.","Create two diagnostic settings for the resource, one for each workspace.","Create a workspace transformation data collection rule that duplicates the data.","Create an Event Hubs destination that forwards the data to the second workspace."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A single diagnostic setting can define no more than one destination of each type, so reaching two workspaces requires two settings. A setting cannot list two workspaces, and neither a transformation DCR nor an Event Hubs destination creates a second workspace copy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","log analytics","limits"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-013","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You export the activity log to a Log Analytics workspace. Which table stores the exported events?","choices":["AzureDiagnostics","AzureMetrics","Event","AzureActivity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Activity log data in a Log Analytics workspace is stored in the AzureActivity table. AzureDiagnostics holds resource logs sent in the legacy Azure Diagnostics mode, and AzureMetrics holds metric data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/activity-log","sourceTitle":"Activity log in Azure Monitor","tags":["activity log","log analytics","azureactivity"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-014","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to retain resource logs for several years at the lowest possible cost for audit purposes. Which diagnostic setting destination should you use?","choices":["Azure Storage account","Log Analytics workspace","Azure Event Hubs","A second Log Analytics workspace in another region"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A storage account is the least expensive destination and can keep data indefinitely for audit or static analysis, and immutable storage can prevent modification. Log Analytics ingestion and retention are charged, and Event Hubs is for streaming to external consumers.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","storage account","audit"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-015","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"A storage account that receives diagnostic data has a firewall enabled to restrict access to virtual networks. What must you do so that Azure Monitor can continue to send data to it?","choices":["Create a private endpoint for the storage account.","Enable the setting that allows trusted Microsoft services to bypass the storage account firewall.","Move the storage account to the same resource group as the monitored resource.","Create a service endpoint for Microsoft.Insights on the storage subnet."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Diagnostic settings cannot access storage accounts with virtual networks enabled unless trusted Microsoft services are allowed to bypass the firewall, which grants the Azure Monitor diagnostic settings service access. Private endpoints and service endpoints do not grant that platform access.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","storage account","firewall","trusted services"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-016","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"You plan to delete an Azure virtual machine and then recreate it with the same name. What should you do with the virtual machine's diagnostic settings?","choices":["Leave the settings deployed so that collection resumes for the new virtual machine.","Export the settings to an ARM template and leave them deployed.","Delete the diagnostic settings before you delete the virtual machine.","Disable the subscription-level activity log export."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Microsoft warns that stale diagnostic settings for a deleted resource can be applied to a re-created resource and resume log collection unexpectedly, so the settings should be removed with the resource. Leaving them intentionally is exactly the situation that guidance warns against.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","resource lifecycle","cleanup"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-017","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure log settings in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need to collect Windows event logs from 500 Azure virtual machines into a Log Analytics workspace. What should you create?","choices":["A data collection rule and associate it with the Azure Monitor agent on the virtual machines.","A diagnostic setting on each virtual machine.","An alert processing rule.","A storage account with immutable blob storage."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Data collected from the guest operating system by the Azure Monitor agent is defined by a data collection rule, which specifies what to collect and where to send it. Diagnostic settings apply to resource-level logs from the resource provider, not to guest OS event logs.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/data-collection/data-collection-rule-overview","sourceTitle":"Data collection rules (DCRs) in Azure Monitor","tags":["data collection rule","azure monitor agent","windows event logs"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-018","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which tool do you use in the Azure portal to write and run Kusto Query Language (KQL) queries against Azure Monitor Logs?","choices":["Metrics explorer","The activity log","Log Analytics","Azure Advisor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Log Analytics is the portal tool for querying data in Azure Monitor Logs with KQL. Metrics explorer works with numeric metric data, and the activity log has its own view for management operations.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["kql","log analytics","queries"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-019","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which KQL operator filters a table to only the rows that match a condition?","choices":["project","summarize","extend","where"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The where operator filters rows by a condition, for example where TimeGenerated > ago(1h). The project operator selects or renames columns, summarize aggregates groups of rows, and extend adds calculated columns.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["kql","where","filtering"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-020","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which KQL operator returns a small sample of records from a table in no particular order?","choices":["project","take","summarize","sort by"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The take operator returns up to the specified number of records in no particular order, which makes it useful for understanding a table's structure. summarize aggregates data, and sort by orders the entire result set, which is slower.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["kql","take","sampling"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-021","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which KQL operator selects only the specified columns in the results of a query?","choices":["where","project","take","summarize"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The project operator selects the columns to include and can also rename columns or define new ones. The where operator filters rows, and summarize groups and aggregates rows.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["kql","project","column selection"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-022","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need the average value of a performance counter for each computer from the last hour. Which query should you use?","choices":["Perf | take 10 | where CounterValue > 1","Perf | project Computer, CounterValue, TimeGenerated","SecurityEvent | summarize count() by Activity","Perf | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) | summarize avg(CounterValue) by Computer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"summarize with avg() grouped by Computer calculates the average per computer, and where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) limits the results to the last hour. The project query returns raw rows without aggregation, and the SecurityEvent query is scoped to a different table.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["kql","summarize","ago","perf table"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-023","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which KQL expression returns records that were collected in the last 24 hours?","choices":["where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)","where TimeGenerated > bin(24h)","summarize by TimeGenerated","where TimeGenerated > 24h"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ago() function returns a datetime offset from the current time, so ago(24h) correctly expresses the last 24 hours. bin() rounds a timestamp into buckets rather than producing a relative time, and 24h alone is not a valid comparison value.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["kql","ago","timespan"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-024","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need a time chart of the average CounterValue for each computer in one-hour buckets. Which KQL expression produces the one-hour buckets?","choices":["summarize avg(CounterValue) by Computer, ago(1h)","summarize count() by CounterName","summarize avg(CounterValue) by Computer, bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)","project TimeGenerated, Computer, bin(CounterValue, 1)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The bin() function groups a timestamp column into fixed-size buckets, so bin(TimeGenerated, 1h) creates hourly buckets alongside the Computer group. ago() returns a relative timestamp instead of grouping, and binning CounterValue would group by value rather than by time.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["kql","bin","summarize","time chart"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-025","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"A table in a Log Analytics workspace holds data that is older than the analytics retention period, and you must query that data. What should you use?","choices":["An alert rule","A workspace transformation data collection rule","A search job","A diagnostic setting"],"answer":2,"explanation":"To retrieve data from long-term retention back into the interactive state you run a search job. Alert rules and diagnostic settings act on data that is already in the interactive retention state, and a transformation DCR modifies incoming data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/log-analytics-workspace-overview","sourceTitle":"Log Analytics workspace overview","tags":["log analytics","retention","search job"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-026","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Query and analyze logs in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"A user must be able to run queries against a Log Analytics workspace but must not be able to change its configuration. Which built-in role should you assign?","choices":["Log Analytics Reader","Log Analytics Contributor","Monitoring Contributor","Reader"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Log Analytics Reader role provides the workspace query permissions required to run queries. Log Analytics Contributor can also change workspace settings, and the generic Reader role does not grant workspace query permissions.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/logs/get-started-queries","sourceTitle":"Get started with log queries in Azure Monitor Logs","tags":["log analytics","rbac","log analytics reader"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-027","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to be notified when a virtual machine's Percentage CPU metric exceeds 90 percent for 10 minutes. Which type of alert rule should you create?","choices":["A log search alert rule","A metric alert rule","An activity log alert rule","A smart detection alert"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Metric alert rules evaluate resource metric values at regular intervals, which is exactly what a threshold on the Percentage CPU platform metric requires. Log search alert rules query a Log Analytics workspace, and activity log alerts respond to control-plane events.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/alerts-types","sourceTitle":"Choose the right type of alert rule","tags":["metric alerts","alert rules","azure monitor"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-028","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"You need an alert when a specific Windows event ID is written to a Log Analytics workspace. What should the alert rule target?","choices":["The virtual machine","The virtual machine's resource group","The Azure subscription","The Log Analytics workspace"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A log search alert rule evaluates a Log Analytics query, so it targets the workspace that holds the collected event data. Windows event log data is not a native virtual machine metric, so targeting the virtual machine does not evaluate the log records.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/alerts-types","sourceTitle":"Choose the right type of alert rule","tags":["log search alerts","log analytics","target resource"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-029","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Azure Monitor object defines who is notified and which automated actions run when an alert fires?","choices":["An alert processing rule","A diagnostic setting","A data collection rule","An action group"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An action group is a collection of notification preferences such as email, SMS, voice, and push notifications, plus automated actions such as runbooks, Functions, and webhooks. An alert processing rule modifies alerts as they fire but does not itself hold the notification list.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/action-groups","sourceTitle":"Action groups","tags":["action groups","notifications","alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-030","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Azure virtual machines will undergo planned maintenance. You must stop alert notifications for those machines during the window without changing any alert rules. What should you create?","choices":["A new action group that contains no notifications","An alert processing rule with the Suppress notifications action and a schedule","A log search alert rule with a condition that excludes maintenance windows","A diagnostic setting that disables log collection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An alert processing rule can suppress notifications for a scoped set of resources on a schedule, which is the documented use case for planned maintenance windows. Editing alert rules does not cover alerts that have no alert rules, and an empty action group does not suppress notifications from other action groups.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/alerts-processing-rules","sourceTitle":"Alert processing rules","tags":["alert processing rules","suppression","maintenance"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-031","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"You create an alert processing rule that suppresses notifications for a resource group outside business hours. What is the effect on alerts that fire during that window?","choices":["The alerts are never created and cannot be seen anywhere.","The alerts are deleted from Azure Resource Graph.","The action groups are not invoked, but the fired alerts remain visible in the portal.","Notifications are delayed and sent when the window ends."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Suppression removes all action groups from the affected fired alerts, so no notification is sent even at the end of the maintenance window, but the alerts are still visible in the portal, Azure Resource Graph, the API, and PowerShell. Alerts are not deleted, and notifications are not queued for later.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/alerts-processing-rules","sourceTitle":"Alert processing rules","tags":["alert processing rules","suppression","notifications"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-032","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"A critical alert fires at 2:00 AM and you need an Azure Automation runbook to start automatically. Which action group action type should you configure?","choices":["Automation Runbook","Secure Webhook","Azure app push notification","Event Hubs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Automation Runbook action type starts an Azure Automation runbook in response to an alert. A webhook calls an external endpoint, push notification is a notification type rather than an automated action, and Event Hubs publishes the alert stream to a consumer.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/action-groups","sourceTitle":"Action groups","tags":["action groups","automation runbook","alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-033","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"A monitoring platform subscribes to the Azure Monitor alert notification stream by using a receiver. Which action group action type should you configure?","choices":["Event Hubs","Logic Apps","ITSM","Voice"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Event Hubs action publishes alert notifications to an event hub, where a receiver can subscribe to the stream, and it is the only action type that supports Azure Private Link and network security perimeter. A Logic Apps action invokes a workflow, and an ITSM action creates a ticket.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/action-groups","sourceTitle":"Action groups","tags":["action groups","event hubs","alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-034","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which type of Azure Monitor alert always fires every time its conditions are met and is never stateful?","choices":["Metric alerts","Activity log alerts","Log search alerts","Prometheus alerts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"All activity log alerts are stateless, so they fire on every matching activity log event. Metric alerts are stateful by default, and log search alerts can be configured as either stateful or stateless.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/alerts-overview","sourceTitle":"What are Azure Monitor alerts?","tags":["alerts","activity log alerts","stateful","stateless"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-035","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Set up alert rules, action groups, and alert processing rules in Azure Monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"You must add an email action group to every high-severity alert in a subscription, including alerts that have no alert rule, such as Azure Backup alerts and VM insights guest health alerts. What should you create?","choices":["An action group scoped to the subscription.","A metric alert rule scoped to the subscription.","A diagnostic setting that forwards alerts to a workspace.","An alert processing rule scoped to the subscription that applies the action group and filters on severity."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Alert processing rules can add action groups to fired alerts and they cover alert types that do not support action groups in their own rules, such as Azure Backup alerts. An action group alone has no scope to apply it to other alerts, and alert processing rules do not affect Azure Service Health alerts.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/alerts/alerts-processing-rules","sourceTitle":"Alert processing rules","tags":["alert processing rules","action groups","scope","severity"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-036","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret monitoring by using Azure Monitor Insights","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need a unified view of the availability, latency, and capacity of your storage accounts without configuring any additional collection. Which feature should you use?","choices":["VM insights","Network insights","Storage insights","Container insights"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Storage insights provides a unified view of storage performance, capacity, and availability and requires no configuration because storage metrics are collected by default. VM insights and network insights monitor other resource types.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/insights/storage-insights-overview","sourceTitle":"Monitoring your storage service with Azure Monitor Storage insights","tags":["storage insights","azure monitor","storage"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-037","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret monitoring by using Azure Monitor Insights","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which type of metric data do Azure virtual machines automatically send to Azure Monitor at no cost?","choices":["Guest metrics for applications and processes","Host metrics such as CPU usage, network traffic, and disk I/O","Windows event log records","Syslog records from Linux virtual machines"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Host metrics are collected automatically for Azure virtual machines at no cost and give visibility into overall performance and load. Guest metrics, Windows event logs, and Syslog records require the Azure Monitor agent and a data collection rule.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/vm/vminsights-overview","sourceTitle":"Monitor virtual machines in Azure","tags":["vm insights","host metrics","azure monitor"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-038","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret monitoring by using Azure Monitor Insights","difficulty":"medium","question":"You enable the enhanced monitoring experience for an Azure virtual machine in the Azure portal. What happens as a result?","choices":["Azure Monitor agent is installed on the virtual machine, and a data collection rule is created or selected.","The Log Analytics agent is installed and a workspace key is embedded in the virtual machine.","The Azure Diagnostics extension is installed and data is written to a storage account.","The Network Watcher extension is installed and data is sent to a Network Watcher storage account."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Enabling enhanced monitoring installs the Azure Monitor agent on the virtual machine and uses a data collection rule to define what is collected and where it is sent. The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired, and the Network Watcher extension is used for network monitoring rather than guest performance data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/vm/tutorial-monitor-vm-enable","sourceTitle":"Tutorial: Enable enhanced monitoring for an Azure virtual machine","tags":["vm insights","enhanced monitoring","azure monitor agent"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-039","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret monitoring by using Azure Monitor Insights","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need a visual topology of your virtual networks together with health and metrics for the deployed network resources, and you do not want to configure anything extra. Which should you use?","choices":["Connection troubleshoot","The Azure activity log","Azure Monitor network insights","Storage insights"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Network insights provides topology, network health, metrics, connectivity, traffic, and a diagnostic toolkit for deployed network resources without requiring configuration. Connection troubleshoot tests a single connection at a point in time, and the activity log records control-plane operations.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/insights/network-insights-overview","sourceTitle":"Network insights","tags":["network insights","topology","azure monitor"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-040","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret monitoring by using Azure Monitor Insights","difficulty":"hard","question":"Which statement describes monitoring support for Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets?","choices":["Scale sets support only host metrics because the Azure Monitor agent cannot be installed on them.","Scale sets are monitored exactly like single virtual machines, including the new metrics experience.","Scale sets can be monitored only by using Network Watcher.","Host metrics and guest performance data are supported through the Azure Monitor agent, but scale sets do not support the new metrics experience."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets support host metrics and guest performance data collected by the Azure Monitor agent, similar to Azure virtual machines, but the new metrics experience is not available for scale sets. The Azure Monitor agent can be installed on scale sets, so limiting them to host metrics is incorrect.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/vm/vminsights-overview","sourceTitle":"Monitor virtual machines in Azure","tags":["vm scale sets","vm insights","azure monitor agent"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-041","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret monitoring by using Azure Monitor Insights","difficulty":"medium","question":"Users report that a storage account is returning throttling errors. Which Storage insights workbook should you open to investigate?","choices":["The Capacity workbook","The Performance workbook","The Failures workbook","The Availability workbook"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Failures workbook shows client-side errors, client-throttling errors, and transactions by API name and response type. The Capacity workbook reports how much storage is used and by which data service, and the Performance workbook covers end-to-end and server latency.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/insights/storage-insights-overview","sourceTitle":"Monitoring your storage service with Azure Monitor Storage insights","tags":["storage insights","workbooks","throttling"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-042","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret monitoring by using Azure Monitor Insights","difficulty":"hard","question":"You plan to use Traffic analytics with network security group flow logs. Which resources must exist first?","choices":["Only a Log Analytics workspace","Only a storage account","An Azure Monitor workspace for Prometheus metrics","A storage account for the flow logs and a Log Analytics workspace for Traffic analytics"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Flow logs are written to an Azure storage account, and Traffic analytics enriches that flow log data and stores the results in a Log Analytics workspace. A workspace alone is not enough because the flow logs must be logged and stored first, and an Azure Monitor workspace is used for Prometheus metrics.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/traffic-analytics","sourceTitle":"Traffic analytics overview","tags":["traffic analytics","flow logs","log analytics"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-043","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to determine whether a packet is allowed or denied to or from a virtual machine and which security rule made the decision. Which Network Watcher tool should you use?","choices":["Next hop","IP flow verify","Packet capture","VPN troubleshoot"],"answer":1,"explanation":"IP flow verify checks whether a packet is allowed or denied to or from an IPv4 or IPv6 address and reports the security rule responsible. Next hop checks routing, packet capture records traffic, and VPN troubleshoot diagnoses virtual network gateways.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["network watcher","ip flow verify","nsg"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-044","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"Users cannot reach a server, and you suspect that a route is sending traffic to the wrong destination. Which Network Watcher tool should you use?","choices":["Next hop","IP flow verify","Connection monitor","Traffic analytics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Next hop reports the next hop type, IP address, and route table ID for a destination IP address, which is how routing problems are diagnosed. IP flow verify evaluates security rules rather than routes, and Traffic analytics visualizes recorded flow log data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["network watcher","next hop","routing"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-045","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to record the actual packets sent to and from a virtual machine for a limited period so that you can analyze them later. Which Network Watcher tool should you use?","choices":["Connection monitor","IP flow verify","Effective security rules","Packet capture"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Packet capture remotely creates packet capture sessions that record traffic to and from a virtual machine or scale set. Connection monitor reports aggregated latency and packet loss rather than packet contents, and effective security rules only lists the NSG rules that apply to a network interface.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["network watcher","packet capture","troubleshooting"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-046","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"medium","question":"You create a Connection monitor that uses an Azure virtual machine as a source. What must you install on the source virtual machine?","choices":["The Azure Monitor agent","The Azure Connected Machine agent","The Network Watcher agent virtual machine extension","The VMSnapshot extension"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Azure virtual machines and scale sets must have the Network Watcher agent VM extension installed to act as Connection monitor source endpoints. The Azure Connected Machine agent is required for on-premises source hosts, and the VMSnapshot extension is used by Azure Backup.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/connection-monitor-overview","sourceTitle":"Connection monitor overview","tags":["connection monitor","network watcher extension","source endpoints"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-047","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"What does Connection monitor measure between source and destination endpoints?","choices":["Aggregated packet loss and network latency over TCP, ICMP, and HTTP pings","The contents of individual packets","The security rules that block traffic","DNS resolution times only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Connection monitor tests measure aggregated packet loss and network latency across TCP, ICMP, and HTTP pings and visualize the end-to-end network path. Packet contents require packet capture, and security rule evaluation is provided by IP flow verify.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/connection-monitor-overview","sourceTitle":"Connection monitor overview","tags":["connection monitor","latency","packet loss"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-048","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need Connection monitor to test connectivity from an on-premises Windows server. What is required on that server?","choices":["Only the Network Watcher agent virtual machine extension","The Azure Connected Machine (Arc) agent, with the Azure Monitor agent enabled","The Microsoft Azure Recovery Services (MARS) agent","A site-to-site VPN gateway in the same subnet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"On-premises hosts must be enabled with the Azure Arc agent and then the Azure Monitor agent to act as Connection monitor sources, and currently only Arc-enabled on-premises hosts are supported as sources. Destination endpoints do not require any extension.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/connection-monitor-overview","sourceTitle":"Connection monitor overview","tags":["connection monitor","azure arc","on-premises"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-049","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need flow logging that records traffic denied by Azure Virtual Network Manager security admin rules and that can evaluate the encryption status of network traffic. Which feature should you enable?","choices":["Virtual network flow logs","Network security group flow logs","Traffic analytics","Connection monitor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Virtual network flow logs identify traffic that Virtual Network Manager security admin rules allow or deny and can evaluate the encryption status of network traffic. NSG flow logs are retiring on September 30, 2027 and do not provide those capabilities, and Traffic analytics only visualizes existing flow log data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/vnet-flow-logs-overview","sourceTitle":"Virtual network flow logs","tags":["vnet flow logs","network watcher","virtual network manager"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-050","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Use Azure Network Watcher and Connection monitor","difficulty":"easy","question":"When is Network Watcher automatically enabled in a region?","choices":["When the first virtual machine is created in the region.","When a virtual network gateway is deployed.","Only when an administrator enables it manually in each region.","When a virtual network is created or updated in that region."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Network Watcher is automatically enabled in a virtual network's region when you create or update a virtual network, with no charge for the automatic enablement. Manual enablement is needed only if you previously opted out.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/network-watcher/network-watcher-monitoring-overview","sourceTitle":"What is Azure Network Watcher?","tags":["network watcher","automatic enablement","regions"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-051","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create a Recovery Services vault","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which vault type must you create to back up an Azure virtual machine by using Azure Backup?","choices":["A Backup vault","A Recovery Services vault","A storage account with immutable blobs","A Log Analytics workspace"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure virtual machine backup is protected by a Recovery Services vault, which can also hold backup data for Azure Files, SQL Server in Azure virtual machines, and on-premises servers. A Backup vault supports newer workloads such as Azure Blobs, Azure Disks, and Azure Database for PostgreSQL.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-recovery-services-vault-overview","sourceTitle":"Recovery Services vaults overview","tags":["recovery services vault","azure backup","virtual machines"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-052","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create a Recovery Services vault","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must protect Azure virtual machines that run in three different Azure regions by using Azure Backup. What is the minimum number of Recovery Services vaults you should create?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Six"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Recovery Services vault must be in the same region as the data source, so resources in three regions require three vaults. A single vault can protect resources across resource groups and subscriptions only within its own region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-create-rs-vault","sourceTitle":"Create and configure a Recovery Services vault","tags":["recovery services vault","regions","design"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-053","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create a Recovery Services vault","difficulty":"medium","question":"When can you change the storage replication type of a Recovery Services vault from geo-redundant to locally redundant?","choices":["At any time, including after backups have been configured.","Before you configure the first backup in the vault.","Only during the vault creation wizard.","Only after protection has been stopped for every backup item."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The storage replication type must be set before a backup is configured in the vault, because the option is disabled once protection is configured. Changing it afterwards is not a supported in-place operation.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-create-rs-vault","sourceTitle":"Create and configure a Recovery Services vault","tags":["recovery services vault","storage redundancy","configuration"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-054","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create a Recovery Services vault","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must be able to restore Azure virtual machines in a paired region during a compliance audit without waiting for Microsoft to declare a disaster. Which vault feature should you enable?","choices":["Soft delete","Zone-redundant storage","Cross Region Restore","Enhanced soft delete"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cross Region Restore lets you restore replicated data in the secondary paired region at any time, which supports audit and compliance drills. Cross Region Restore requires a vault that uses geo-redundant storage, and zone-redundant storage provides in-region resilience instead.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-recovery-services-vault-overview","sourceTitle":"Recovery Services vaults overview","tags":["cross region restore","recovery services vault","disaster recovery"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-055","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create a Recovery Services vault","difficulty":"easy","question":"An on-premises Windows Server must be backed up directly to Azure by using the Microsoft Azure Recovery Services (MARS) agent. To which vault do you register the server?","choices":["A Recovery Services vault","A Backup vault","A storage account with vaulted backups","An Azure Automation account"],"answer":0,"explanation":"MARS-protected servers are registered to a Recovery Services vault, which also supports System Center Data Protection Manager and Azure Backup Server. Backup vaults are used for newer workloads such as blobs, disks, and PostgreSQL.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-recovery-services-vault-overview","sourceTitle":"Recovery Services vaults overview","tags":["mars agent","recovery services vault","on-premises"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-056","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create a Recovery Services vault","difficulty":"hard","question":"An administrator tries to delete a Recovery Services vault but receives an error stating that backup items are in a soft-deleted state. What must happen before the vault can be deleted?","choices":["The vault's storage replication type must be changed to locally redundant.","The vault must be moved to another resource group.","The backup policy must be deleted from the vault.","The soft-deleted items must be permanently deleted, which happens after the 14-day soft delete period, or they must be restored and deleted again."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A vault cannot be deleted while backup items remain, including items in the soft-deleted state, and soft-deleted items are permanently deleted after 14 days. Deleting the policy alone does not remove the protected items.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-delete-vault","sourceTitle":"Delete an Azure Backup Recovery Services vault","tags":["recovery services vault","soft delete","vault deletion"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-057","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create an Azure Backup vault","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which workload is designed to be protected by an Azure Backup vault rather than a Recovery Services vault?","choices":["Azure virtual machine","SQL Server running in an Azure virtual machine","Azure Database for PostgreSQL server","On-premises server protected by the MARS agent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Backup vaults house backup data for newer workloads such as Azure Database for PostgreSQL servers, Azure Blobs, and Azure Disks. Azure virtual machines, SQL Server in Azure virtual machines, and MARS-protected servers are protected by a Recovery Services vault.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-vault-overview","sourceTitle":"Backup vaults overview","tags":["backup vault","recovery services vault","postgresql"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-058","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create an Azure Backup vault","difficulty":"easy","question":"You need to back up the managed disks of an Azure virtual machine. Which vault should you create?","choices":["A Recovery Services vault in the same region as the disks","A Backup vault in the same region as the disks","A storage account with immutable blobs","A Recovery Services vault in a paired region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Disk Backup is protected by a Backup vault, and the vault must be in the same region as the disks. Recovery Services vaults are used for workload backups such as Azure virtual machines, SQL Server in Azure virtual machines, and Azure Files.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-vault-overview","sourceTitle":"Backup vaults overview","tags":["backup vault","azure disk backup","managed disks"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-059","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create an Azure Backup vault","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which statement about Azure Blob Storage vaulted backup is correct?","choices":["Backup data stays only in the source storage account.","Backup data is stored in a Recovery Services vault.","Backup data is stored only in the same resource group as the storage account.","Backup data is copied to and stored in a Backup vault according to the schedule and frequency in the backup policy."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Vaulted backup copies blob data offsite into a Backup vault according to the schedule and retention defined in the backup policy, which protects against deletion of the storage account. Operational backup keeps data locally in the source storage account and does not transfer it to a vault.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/blob-backup-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of Azure Blob backup","tags":["blob backup","vaulted backup","backup vault"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-060","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create an Azure Backup vault","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need local, continuous protection for block blobs that keeps all changes in the source storage account and requires no backup schedule. Which backup type should you configure?","choices":["Operational backup","Vaulted backup","Snapshot backup to a Recovery Services vault","File share snapshot backup"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Operational backup is a managed local data protection solution that retains all changes in the source storage account and allows restore to a selected point in time without a defined schedule. Vaulted backup copies data offsite to a Backup vault on a schedule defined in the policy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/blob-backup-overview","sourceTitle":"Overview of Azure Blob backup","tags":["blob backup","operational backup","point-in-time restore"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-061","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create an Azure Backup vault","difficulty":"hard","question":"How does a Backup vault obtain the permissions it needs to protect resources such as storage accounts and PostgreSQL servers and to access Key Vaults used for encryption?","choices":["By using a workspace key that is embedded in the backup policy.","By using a shared access signature stored in the vault.","By using the credentials of the user who created the vault.","Through a system-assigned managed identity that is granted Azure RBAC roles."],"answer":3,"explanation":"RBAC for Backup vaults is enforced through managed identities, and a system-assigned managed identity is automatically created when the vault is provisioned. User credentials and workspace keys are not used to authorize the vault.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/create-manage-backup-vault","sourceTitle":"Manage Backup vaults","tags":["backup vault","managed identity","rbac"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-062","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create an Azure Backup vault","difficulty":"medium","question":"You enable Cross Region Restore on a Backup vault so that a PostgreSQL server can be restored in a secondary paired region. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Cross Region Restore for a Backup vault is currently available only for Azure Database for PostgreSQL servers.","Cross Region Restore for a Backup vault works for every workload, including Azure Kubernetes Service.","Cross Region Restore requires locally redundant storage on the vault.","Cross Region Restore can be configured only when the vault is created."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For Backup vaults, Cross Region Restore is currently available only for PostgreSQL backups and it requires geo-redundant storage on the vault. It is not supported for Azure Kubernetes Service or Azure VMware Solution backups, and it can be enabled later from the vault properties.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/create-manage-backup-vault","sourceTitle":"Manage Backup vaults","tags":["backup vault","cross region restore","postgresql"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-063","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create and configure a backup policy","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which object defines the backup schedule and retention range for a protected Azure virtual machine?","choices":["An alert processing rule","A data collection rule","A backup policy in the Recovery Services vault","A retention policy on the storage account"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The backup policy contains the schedule and the retention ranges that apply to protected items. Data collection rules define monitoring data for the Azure Monitor agent, and alert processing rules modify alerts.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-vms-introduction","sourceTitle":"An overview of Azure VM backup","tags":["backup policy","schedule","retention"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-064","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create and configure a backup policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which retention range is the minimum that a virtual machine backup policy supports?","choices":["One day","Seven days","14 days","30 days"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Virtual Machine backup policy supports a minimum retention range from seven days up to 9999 days. A one-day range is below that minimum, and 14 and 30 days are longer ranges rather than the minimum.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-vm-backup-faq","sourceTitle":"Frequently asked questions-Back up Azure VMs","tags":["backup policy","retention range","virtual machines"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-065","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create and configure a backup policy","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which retention frequencies can you configure in a virtual machine backup policy?","choices":["Daily, weekly, monthly, and yearly","Hourly only","Daily and weekly only","Daily, weekly, and monthly only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A virtual machine backup policy defines daily, weekly, monthly, and yearly retention points in addition to the snapshot retention used by Instant Restore. Hourly backups belong to the enhanced policy, and limiting retention to daily and weekly ignores the monthly and yearly options.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-vm-backup-faq","sourceTitle":"Frequently asked questions-Back up Azure VMs","tags":["backup policy","retention","schedule"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-066","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create and configure a backup policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"A backup administrator needs to know how many virtual machines can be associated with a single backup policy before additional policies should be created.","choices":["10 virtual machines","50 virtual machines","100 virtual machines","1,000 virtual machines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A limit of 100 virtual machines applies to the same backup policy, and Microsoft recommends creating additional policies for more than 100 virtual machines. The smaller figures understate the documented limit, and 1,000 is far above it.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-vm-backup-faq","sourceTitle":"Frequently asked questions-Back up Azure VMs","tags":["backup policy","limits","virtual machines"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-067","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create and configure a backup policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"You modify the retention settings of a backup policy that protects several virtual machines. What should you expect?","choices":["The change takes effect only after 30 days.","New backups use the new settings immediately, while pruning of existing recovery points can take up to 24 hours.","Existing recovery points are deleted immediately.","The change requires that protection is stopped and restarted for every virtual machine."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Changes such as retention and schedule apply immediately to new backups, and pruning of recovery points according to the new policy takes up to 24 hours. Virtual machines do not need to be unprotected, and recovery points are not deleted instantly.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-vm-backup-faq","sourceTitle":"Frequently asked questions-Back up Azure VMs","tags":["backup policy","retention change","recovery points"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-068","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create and configure a backup policy","difficulty":"easy","question":"An Azure virtual machine that runs Linux has no custom pre/post scripts configured. Which type of snapshot consistency does Azure Backup provide for that virtual machine?","choices":["Application-consistent","Crash-consistent","Transactionally-consistent","File-consistent"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For Linux virtual machines, Azure Backup takes a file-consistent backup by default and requires custom pre/post scripts for application-consistent snapshots. Application-consistent snapshots on Windows use VSS, and crash-consistent snapshots are taken without a guest agent.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-vms-introduction","sourceTitle":"An overview of Azure VM backup","tags":["backup policy","snapshot consistency","linux"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-069","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Create and configure a backup policy","difficulty":"medium","question":"You run an on-demand backup for a virtual machine immediately after its initial backup has completed. What does Azure Backup transfer?","choices":["A full copy of the virtual machine to the vault.","Only the changes from the previous snapshot to the vault.","Nothing, because on-demand backups are blocked until the policy schedule runs.","Only the contents of the operating system disk."],"answer":1,"explanation":"After the initial backup is complete, on-demand backups send only the changes since the previous snapshot because later backups are always incremental. Only an on-demand backup taken while the initial backup is still pending creates a full copy.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-manage-vms","sourceTitle":"Manage Azure VM backups with Azure Backup service","tags":["on-demand backup","incremental","azure backup"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-070","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"easy","question":"You must restore an entire virtual machine from a recovery point, and you do not want to replace the existing virtual machine. Which restore option should you use?","choices":["Replace existing","Restore disks","Cross Region Restore","Create new"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Create new option creates a new virtual machine, or restores disks that can be used to build a customized machine. Replace existing changes the disks of the existing virtual machine, and Restore disks produces disks for manual recovery rather than a running virtual machine.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-restore-vms","sourceTitle":"How to restore Azure VM data in Azure portal","tags":["restore","create new","recovery point"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-071","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"medium","question":"An administrator must recover two individual files from a virtual machine recovery point without restoring the whole virtual machine. What should the administrator do?","choices":["Use the Restore disks option and attach the restored virtual hard disks.","Use the Replace existing restore option.","Use File Recovery to generate a script or executable that mounts the recovery point, and then copy the files.","Restore the virtual machine to a new resource group and copy the files from there."],"answer":2,"explanation":"File Recovery generates an executable for Windows virtual machines or a Python script for Linux virtual machines that mounts the recovery point with read-only access so that individual files can be copied. The script is valid for only 12 hours, and the disk-level restore options work with whole disks rather than selected files.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-restore-files-from-vm","sourceTitle":"Recover files and folders from Azure VM backup","tags":["file recovery","restore","recovery point"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-072","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"easy","question":"You must revert an existing virtual machine's operating system disk to an earlier recovery point while keeping the same virtual machine. Which restore option should you use?","choices":["Replace existing","Create new","Restore disks","File Recovery"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Replace existing option replaces the disks on an existing virtual machine with the selected recovery point. Create new builds a separate machine, and Restore disks produces disks that must be attached manually.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-restore-vms","sourceTitle":"How to restore Azure VM data in Azure portal","tags":["restore","replace existing","recovery point"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-073","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"hard","question":"For which virtual machine can you not use the Replace existing restore option?","choices":["An unencrypted managed virtual machine created from a custom image","A virtual machine with a user-assigned managed identity","A classic virtual machine that uses unmanaged disks","A virtual machine with a linked Key Vault"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replace existing is supported for unencrypted managed virtual machines, including machines created from custom images, and for machines with linked resources such as a user-assigned managed identity or Key Vault. It is not supported for classic virtual machines, unmanaged-disk machines, or generalized machines.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-restore-vms","sourceTitle":"How to restore Azure VM data in Azure portal","tags":["restore","replace existing","unmanaged disks"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-074","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must restore an Azure virtual machine into its paired secondary region, and Microsoft has not declared a regional outage. What must be enabled on the Recovery Services vault?","choices":["Cross Region Restore","Soft delete","Zone-redundant storage","Enhanced soft delete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cross Region Restore allows restores from replicated data in the paired region at any time, without waiting for Microsoft to declare a disaster. Soft delete protects deleted backup items, and zone-redundant storage protects data within the primary region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-recovery-services-vault-overview","sourceTitle":"Recovery Services vaults overview","tags":["cross region restore","restore","paired region"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-075","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"medium","question":"An administrator deletes an Azure virtual machine without first stopping its backups. What happens to the virtual machine's backups?","choices":["All recovery points are deleted immediately.","Backups continue to run successfully against the deleted virtual machine.","The backup item is automatically moved to another vault.","New backups fail, and the most recent recovery point is kept until protection is stopped and the data is deleted."],"answer":3,"explanation":"New backups fail when the data source no longer exists, and old recovery points expire according to the policy, but the most recent recovery point is retained until you stop the backups and delete the data. The backup item is not moved automatically.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-manage-vms","sourceTitle":"Manage Azure VM backups with Azure Backup service","tags":["azure backup","deleted vm","recovery points"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-076","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"easy","question":"When does billing for a protected virtual machine's backup data stop?","choices":["When the backup policy is modified.","When protection is stopped and all backup data is deleted.","When the virtual machine is stopped (deallocated).","When the recovery points expire according to the policy."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Billing for a protected virtual machine stops only when protection is stopped and all backup data is deleted. Deallocating the virtual machine and letting recovery points expire do not stop the billing.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-vms-introduction","sourceTitle":"An overview of Azure VM backup","tags":["azure backup","billing","stop protection"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-077","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"easy","question":"A backup item was deleted accidentally. For how long is the backup data retained by default before it is permanently deleted?","choices":["7 days","30 days","90 days","14 days"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Soft delete, which is enabled by default for new vaults, retains backup data for 14 additional days so that deleted backups can be recovered without data loss, and the additional retention does not incur cost. The 90-day figure relates to activity log retention, not to backup items.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-security-feature-cloud","sourceTitle":"Secure by default with soft delete for Azure Backup","tags":["soft delete","azure backup","deleted backup item"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-078","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform backup and restore operations by using Azure Backup","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which statement describes snapshot backup of Azure file shares?","choices":["Snapshots are copied to the Recovery Services vault immediately.","Snapshots are stored in the same storage account as the file share, and Azure Backup applies a delete lock to the storage account.","Snapshots are stored in a separate storage account in the same region.","Snapshots are stored in a Backup vault."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Files snapshot backups are kept in the same storage account as the backed-up file share, and Azure Backup takes a delete lock on the storage account to protect against accidental deletion of the account and its shares. Vaulted backup for Azure Files is a separate option that stores data in the Recovery Services vault.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-files-faq","sourceTitle":"Frequently asked questions about backing up Azure Files","tags":["azure files","snapshot backup","delete lock"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-079","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure Azure Site Recovery for Azure resources","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Azure service provides disaster recovery for Azure virtual machines by replicating them continuously to another region?","choices":["Azure Site Recovery","Azure Backup","Azure Traffic Manager","Azure Load Balancer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Azure Site Recovery provides continuous replication, failover, and failback for workloads including Azure virtual machines. Azure Backup protects data with recovery points, while Traffic Manager and Load Balancer distribute traffic rather than replicate data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-overview","sourceTitle":"About Site Recovery","tags":["site recovery","disaster recovery","replication"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-080","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure Azure Site Recovery for Azure resources","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which vault must exist before you can configure replication for Azure virtual machines by using Azure Site Recovery?","choices":["A Backup vault","A storage account with immutable blobs","A Recovery Services vault","A Log Analytics workspace"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Site Recovery replicas and their jobs are managed from a Recovery Services vault. A Backup vault supports newer backup workloads such as blobs, disks, and PostgreSQL rather than Site Recovery, and a Log Analytics workspace stores monitoring data.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/azure-to-azure-how-to-enable-replication","sourceTitle":"Replicate Azure VMs to another Azure region","tags":["site recovery","recovery services vault","replication"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-081","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure Azure Site Recovery for Azure resources","difficulty":"medium","question":"What is the purpose of the cache storage account that Site Recovery requires in the source region?","choices":["It temporarily stores changes from the source virtual machines before they are replicated to the target location.","It stores the final replicated copies of the virtual machines in the target region.","It stores the recovery plans for the vault.","It stores the vault's diagnostic logs."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The cache storage account holds virtual machine changes in the source region before they are sent to the target, which minimizes the impact on production applications. Replica managed disks or target storage hold the replicated copies, not the cache account.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/azure-to-azure-architecture","sourceTitle":"Azure to Azure disaster recovery architecture","tags":["site recovery","cache storage account","replication"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-082","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure Azure Site Recovery for Azure resources","difficulty":"medium","question":"How often does Site Recovery create crash-consistent recovery points, and can that frequency be changed?","choices":["Every 30 seconds by default, and the setting can be modified.","Every five minutes by default, and the setting cannot be modified.","Every 15 minutes by default, and the setting can be modified.","Every hour by default, and the setting cannot be modified."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Site Recovery creates crash-consistent recovery points every five minutes by default, and that interval is not configurable. The app-consistent snapshot frequency is the configurable setting, and it should always be less than the recovery point retention.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/azure-to-azure-architecture","sourceTitle":"Azure to Azure disaster recovery architecture","tags":["site recovery","crash-consistent","recovery points"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-083","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure Azure Site Recovery for Azure resources","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Site Recovery replication policy setting specifies how long recovery points are kept?","choices":["App-consistent snapshot frequency","Cache storage duration","Recovery point retention","Target region recovery point objective"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The recovery point retention setting determines how long Site Recovery keeps recovery points and defaults to one day. The app-consistent snapshot frequency controls how often app-consistent snapshots are taken, not how long they are kept.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/azure-to-azure-architecture","sourceTitle":"Azure to Azure disaster recovery architecture","tags":["site recovery","replication policy","retention"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-084","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure Azure Site Recovery for Azure resources","difficulty":"hard","question":"Several virtual machines must fail over together with shared crash-consistent and app-consistent recovery points. What should you configure, and what is the trade-off?","choices":["A recovery plan with no replication group, which has no performance impact.","An availability set in the target region, which guarantees consistent recovery points.","Separate cache storage accounts for each virtual machine, which removes the performance impact.","A replication group for multi-VM consistency, which can affect workload performance."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Virtual machines that must replicate together with shared recovery points are gathered into a replication group with multi-VM consistency, and this impacts workload performance, so it should be used only where cross-machine consistency is required. Availability sets and cache storage accounts do not create shared recovery points.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/azure-to-azure-architecture","sourceTitle":"Azure to Azure disaster recovery architecture","tags":["site recovery","multi-vm consistency","replication group"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-085","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure Azure Site Recovery for Azure resources","difficulty":"medium","question":"You enable replication for an Azure virtual machine to a secondary region and want the replicated machines placed in a dedicated resource group. What does Site Recovery do by default?","choices":["It places the replicated virtual machines in the source resource group.","It creates a new resource group in the target region with an asr suffix in the name.","It places the replicated virtual machines in the Recovery Services vault's resource group.","It requires you to create the target resource group before enabling replication."],"answer":1,"explanation":"By default, Site Recovery creates a new target resource group with an asr suffix, and if that resource group already exists it is reused. The target resource group settings can be customized during enablement, but creating the group manually beforehand is not required.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/azure-to-azure-how-to-enable-replication","sourceTitle":"Replicate Azure VMs to another Azure region","tags":["site recovery","target resource group","replication"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-086","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform a failover to a secondary region by using Site Recovery","difficulty":"easy","question":"You want to validate that disaster recovery works without affecting the production workloads. Which failover type should you use?","choices":["Test failover","Unplanned failover","Planned failover","Commit failover"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Test failover creates the virtual machines in an isolated network so that replication continues and production workloads are not affected. Planned and unplanned failovers move the production workload to the target region.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-overview","sourceTitle":"About Site Recovery","tags":["site recovery","test failover","disaster recovery drill"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-087","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform a failover to a secondary region by using Site Recovery","difficulty":"medium","question":"You initiate an unplanned failover and must recover as much data as possible, accepting that all replicated data has to be processed. Which recovery point option should you select?","choices":["Latest processed","Latest app-consistent","Latest multi-VM processed","Latest"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Latest option processes all the data that has been sent to Site Recovery and creates a recovery point for each machine, which provides the lowest recovery point objective. Latest processed uses only the last recovery point already processed and therefore offers a lower recovery time objective instead.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-failover","sourceTitle":"Run a failover from on-premises to Azure","tags":["site recovery","failover","latest recovery point","rpo"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-088","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform a failover to a secondary region by using Site Recovery","difficulty":"hard","question":"You must fail over with the shortest possible recovery time and do not need the absolute latest data. Which recovery point option should you select?","choices":["Latest","Latest app-consistent","Latest processed","Latest multi-VM app-consistent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Latest processed fails over to the latest recovery point already processed by Site Recovery, so no time is spent processing unprocessed data and the recovery time objective is lower. Latest provides the lowest recovery point objective but must process all replicated data first.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-failover","sourceTitle":"Run a failover from on-premises to Azure","tags":["site recovery","failover","latest processed","rto"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-089","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform a failover to a secondary region by using Site Recovery","difficulty":"medium","question":"What does committing a failover do?","choices":["It restarts replication back to the primary region.","It creates a test copy of the failed-over virtual machines.","It deletes all the recovery points that are available with the service.","It reverts the failover to the previous recovery point."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Commit action deletes all recovery points available with the service, after which the Change recovery point option is no longer available. Reprotection, not commit, restarts replication in the opposite direction.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-failover","sourceTitle":"Run a failover from on-premises to Azure","tags":["site recovery","commit","recovery points"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-090","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform a failover to a secondary region by using Site Recovery","difficulty":"easy","question":"After a successful failover to the target region, what must you do so that the failed-over virtual machines are protected in the reverse direction?","choices":["Commit the failover.","Run another test failover.","Delete the replication policy.","Reprotect the virtual machines."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Reprotection starts replicating the failed-over virtual machines back to the source location so that you can fail back when the source is available again. Committing the failover finalizes the recovery points but does not resume replication.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-failover","sourceTitle":"Run a failover from on-premises to Azure","tags":["site recovery","reprotect","failback"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-091","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform a failover to a secondary region by using Site Recovery","difficulty":"medium","question":"You plan to move production workloads to the target region during a scheduled maintenance window with no data loss. Which failover type should you use?","choices":["Planned failover","Unplanned failover","Test failover","Commit failover"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A planned failover is a zero data loss failover option in which the source virtual machines are shut down first, the latest data synchronizes, and then the failover starts. An unplanned failover is used for unexpected outages and can incur data loss, and test failover does not move production workloads.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-overview","sourceTitle":"About Site Recovery","tags":["site recovery","planned failover","zero data loss"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-092","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Perform a failover to a secondary region by using Site Recovery","difficulty":"hard","question":"Site Recovery raises a failover-readiness health issue for a replicated virtual machine in which situation?","choices":["A test failover was run within the last 24 hours.","No successful test failover exists, or the most recent success is older than 180 days.","The recovery point retention is set to one day.","The target resource group was created automatically by Site Recovery."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Site Recovery recommends a successful test failover at least once every 180 days and raises a failover-readiness health issue when no successful test failover exists or the most recent success is older than 180 days. A recent test failover improves readiness rather than causing an issue.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/site-recovery/site-recovery-failover","sourceTitle":"Run a failover from on-premises to Azure","tags":["site recovery","test failover","failover readiness"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-093","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need to use Azure Backup reports for a Recovery Services vault. What must you configure first?","choices":["A storage account that receives the vault's diagnostics data.","An Event Hubs namespace for the vault's job logs.","A backup policy that generates the report data.","A diagnostic setting on the vault that sends its diagnostics data to a Log Analytics workspace."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Backup reports are powered by Azure Monitor logs and Azure workbooks, so the vault must send diagnostics data to a Log Analytics workspace. Sending diagnostics to a storage account or to Event Hubs supports other scenarios but does not populate the reports.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-configure-reports","sourceTitle":"Configure Azure Backup reports","tags":["backup reports","diagnostic settings","log analytics"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-094","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which alerting solution for Azure Backup generates alerts by default for critical scenarios such as deletion of backup data, backup failure, and restore failure?","choices":["Classic backup alerts","Log search alerts on the AzureActivity table","Built-in Azure Monitor alerts","Metric alerts created by the administrator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Azure Backup automatically generates built-in Azure Monitor alerts for critical scenarios, and notifications can be configured by using alert processing rules and action groups. Classic backup alerts are being replaced by this experience, and custom log or metric alerts must be written by an administrator.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-monitoring-alerts","sourceTitle":"Manage Azure Monitor based alerts for Azure Backup","tags":["azure backup","built-in alerts","azure monitor alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-095","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"medium","question":"Azure Backup built-in alerts are generated automatically. You must route them to a distribution list without creating any alert rules. What should you create?","choices":["A metric alert rule with an email action group.","An alert processing rule that applies an action group to the alerts.","A log search alert rule on the vault's diagnostic data.","A diagnostic setting on the vault."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Built-in backup alerts are not associated with alert rules that can hold action groups, so notifications are routed by creating an alert processing rule that applies an action group. Creating metric or log search alert rules would duplicate the alerts rather than route the built-in ones.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-monitoring-alerts","sourceTitle":"Manage Azure Monitor based alerts for Azure Backup","tags":["azure backup","alert processing rules","action groups"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-096","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"hard","question":"You want to send a Recovery Services vault's diagnostic data to a storage account in a different Azure region from the vault. What is the result?","choices":["It is not supported, because a storage account destination must be in the same region as the monitored resource.","It is supported, because only Log Analytics workspaces have a regional restriction.","It is supported, but only for the activity log data source.","It is supported, because diagnostic settings ignore regions for storage accounts."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a regional resource, a storage account or event hub destination must be in the same region as the monitored resource, although the destination can be in a different subscription. A Log Analytics workspace has no such regional requirement, which is the opposite of the second option.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/essentials/diagnostic-settings","sourceTitle":"Diagnostic settings in Azure Monitor","tags":["diagnostic settings","storage account","regions"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-097","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"medium","question":"Where can the Log Analytics workspace that stores Azure Backup reporting data be located?","choices":["In any region and subscription, independently of where the vaults are located.","Only in the same region as the vaults.","Only in the same subscription as the vaults.","Only in the same resource group as the vaults."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The location and subscription of the Log Analytics workspace used for Backup reports are independent of the location and subscription of the vaults. Regional restrictions apply to storage account and Event Hubs destinations rather than to workspace destinations for reporting.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-configure-reports","sourceTitle":"Configure Azure Backup reports","tags":["backup reports","log analytics workspace","regions"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-098","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"medium","question":"After you configure diagnostics for backup reporting, how long can it take before data appears in the reports?","choices":["Data appears immediately in real time.","Up to five minutes, and reports are accurate within the same hour.","Up to 30 days, because reports are generated monthly.","Up to 24 hours for the initial data push, and Microsoft recommends viewing reports two days after configuration."],"answer":3,"explanation":"After diagnostics are configured, the initial data push can take up to 24 hours, and data for the current partial day is not shown, so Microsoft recommends starting to view reports two days after configuration. Reports are neither real time nor generated monthly.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-configure-reports","sourceTitle":"Configure Azure Backup reports","tags":["backup reports","diagnostic data","latency"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-099","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"hard","question":"You need custom backup alerts that are based on vault diagnostic data, and some delay in the alert is acceptable. Which alert rule type should you use?","choices":["A metric alert rule on the vault","An activity log alert rule on the vault","A log search alert rule on the Log Analytics workspace","A built-in Azure Monitor backup alert"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Log Analytics based alerts are the documented choice for custom backup alert logic where some delay is acceptable, and they require the vault to send diagnostics to a workspace. Metric alerts are used for backup job and health metrics rather than custom log logic, and built-in alerts cover only default scenarios.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-monitoring-alerts","sourceTitle":"Manage Azure Monitor based alerts for Azure Backup","tags":["azure backup","log search alerts","custom alerts"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"},{"id":"AZ104-D5-100","domain":5,"domainName":"Monitor and maintain","subtopic":"Configure and interpret reports and alerts for backups","difficulty":"medium","question":"You must configure diagnostics settings for every Recovery Services vault in a subscription automatically. What should you use?","choices":["An alert processing rule.","A built-in Azure Policy definition that configures vault diagnostics settings at scale.","A backup policy created in each vault.","A workbook in Azure Monitor."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Azure Backup provides a built-in Azure Policy definition that automates the configuration of diagnostics settings for all Recovery Services vaults in a given scope. Backup policies define backup schedules and retention, and workbooks visualize data rather than configure collection.","sourceUrl":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/backup/backup-azure-configure-reports","sourceTitle":"Configure Azure Backup reports","tags":["azure policy","diagnostic settings","governance"],"sourceFile":"domain-5-monitor-and-maintain.xlsx"}];
